const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"On a ship at sea.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"On a ship at sea; a tempestuous noise of thunder and lightning heard","id":1},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a SHIPMASTER and a BOATSWAIN severally","id":2},{"t":"sp","who":"MASTER","lines":["Boatswain!"],"id":3,"m":"Boatswain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["Here, master: what cheer?"],"id":4,"m":"Here, captain. How are things?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MASTER","lines":["Good! Speak to the mariners: fall to't yarely, or","we run ourselves aground: bestir, bestir."],"id":5,"m":"Good! Talk to the sailors. Get to work quickly, or we'll run aground. Move, move!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":6},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MARINERS","id":7},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["Heigh, my hearts! cheerly, cheerly, my hearts!","yare, yare! Take in the topsail. Tend to th' master's","whistle.—Blow till thou burst thy wind, if room enough."],"id":8,"m":"Come on, my lads! Cheer up, cheer up, my lads! Quick, quick! Take in the topsail. Listen for the captain's whistle. Blow till you burst, wind, as long as we have room to move."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ALONSO, SEBASTIAN, ANTONIO, FERDINAND, GONZALO, and OTHERS","id":9},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Good boatswain, have care. Where's the master?","Play the men."],"id":10,"m":"Good boatswain, be careful. Where's the captain? Make the men work like men."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["I pray now, keep below."],"id":11,"m":"Please, stay below deck."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Where is the master, boson?"],"id":12,"m":"Where is the captain, boatswain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["Do you not hear him? You mar our labour:","keep your cabins: you do assist the storm."],"id":13,"m":"Can't you hear him? You're getting in the way of our work. Stay in your cabins: you're helping the storm."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Nay, good, be patient."],"id":14,"m":"Now, good man, be patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["When the sea is. Hence! What cares these","roarers for the name of king? To cabin! silence! Trouble","us not."],"id":15,"m":"I will when the sea is. Get away! What do these roaring waves care about the title of king? To your cabins! Quiet! Don't bother us."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Good, yet remember whom thou hast aboard."],"id":16,"m":"All right, but remember who you have on board."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["None that I more love than myself. You are counsellor: if you can","command these elements to silence, and work the peace of the present,","we will not hand a rope more. Use your authority: if you cannot, give","thanks you have lived so long, and make yourself ready in your cabin","for the mischance of the hour, if it so hap.—Cheerly, good","hearts!—Out of our way, I say."],"id":17,"m":"Nobody I love more than myself. You're a royal adviser: if you can order these elements to be quiet and make peace right now, we won't touch another rope. Use your authority. If you can't, be thankful you've lived so long, and go to your cabin and get ready for disaster, if it comes to that. Cheer up, lads! Out of our way, I said."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":18},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I have great comfort from this fellow. Methinks","he hath no drowning mark upon him: his complexion is","perfect gallows. Stand fast, good Fate, to his hanging!","make the rope of his destiny our cable, for our own doth","little advantage! If he be not born to be hang'd, our","case is miserable."],"id":19,"m":"This fellow is a great comfort to me. I don't think he's marked for drowning: he has the look of a man born to be hanged. Hold to it, good Fate, and hang him! Let the rope of his destiny be our anchor cable, since ours does us little good! If he wasn't born to be hanged, we're in a bad way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":20},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter BOATSWAIN","id":21},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["Down with the topmast! yare! lower, lower!","Bring her to try wi' th' maincourse. [A cry within] A","plague upon this howling! They are louder than the","weather or our office.—"],"id":22,"m":"Down with the topmast! Quick! Lower, lower! Try to hold her steady with the mainsail. A curse on this howling! They're louder than the storm or our shouting orders."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter SEBASTIAN, ANTONIO, and GONZALO","id":23},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","cont":1,"lines":["Yet again! What do you here? Shall we give o'er, and","drown? Have you a mind to sink?"],"id":24,"m":"Here again! What are you doing here? Should we give up and drown? Do you want to sink?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A pox o' your throat, you bawling, blasphemous,","incharitable dog!"],"id":25,"m":"A plague on your throat, you yelling, swearing, heartless dog!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["Work you, then."],"id":26,"m":"Then you do the work."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Hang, cur, hang! you whoreson, insolent noisemaker,","we are less afraid to be drowned than thou art."],"id":27,"m":"Hang, you mongrel, hang! You son of a whore, you rude loudmouth, we're less afraid of drowning than you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I'll warrant him for drowning, though the ship were","no stronger than a nutshell, and as leaky as an unstanched","wench."],"id":28,"m":"I promise he won't drown, even if the ship were no stronger than a nutshell and as leaky as a loose woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["Lay her a-hold, a-hold! set her two courses: off","to sea again: lay her off."],"id":29,"m":"Hold her into the wind! Set the two mainsails! Back out to sea! Steer her away from shore!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MARINERS, Wet","id":30},{"t":"sp","who":"MARINERS","lines":["All lost! to prayers, to prayers! all lost!"],"id":31,"m":"All is lost! Pray, pray! All is lost!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":32},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["What, must our mouths be cold?"],"id":33,"m":"What, must we die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["The King and Prince at prayers! let us assist them,","For our case is as theirs."],"id":34,"m":"The King and the Prince are praying! Let's join them, because we're in the same danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I am out of patience."],"id":35,"m":"I've lost all patience."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["We are merely cheated of our lives by drunkards.—","This wide-chapp'd rascal—would thou might'st lie drowning","The washing of ten tides!"],"id":36,"m":"We're simply being cheated out of our lives by drunkards. This big-mouthed rascal: I wish you'd lie drowning while ten tides washed over you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["He'll be hang'd yet,","Though every drop of water swear against it,","And gape at wid'st to glut him."],"id":37,"m":"He'll be hanged yet, even if every drop of water swears he won't and opens wide to swallow him."},{"t":"sd","text":"A confused noise within:—'Mercy on us!'— 'We split, we split!'—'Farewell, my wife and children!'— 'Farewell, brother!'—'We split, we split, we split!'—","id":38},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Let's all sink wi' the King."],"id":39,"m":"Let's all go down with the King."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":40},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Let's take leave of him."],"id":41,"m":"Let's say goodbye to him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":42},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Now would I give a thousand furlongs of sea for","an acre of barren ground; long heath, brown furze, any","thing. The wills above be done! but I would fain die","dry death."],"id":43,"m":"Right now I'd give a thousand miles of sea for an acre of barren land: long heather, brown gorse, anything. Heaven's will be done! But I'd rather die a dry death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":44},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The island. Before Prospero's cell.","id":45},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PROSPERO and MIRANDA","id":46},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["If by your art, my dearest father, you have","Put the wild waters in this roar, allay them.","The sky, it seems, would pour down stinking pitch,","But that the sea, mounting to th' welkin's cheek,","Dashes the fire out. O! I have suffered","With those that I saw suffer: a brave vessel,","Who had, no doubt, some noble creatures in her,","Dash'd all to pieces. O! the cry did knock","Against my very heart. Poor souls, they perish'd.","Had I been any god of power, I would","Have sunk the sea within the earth, or e'er","It should the good ship so have swallow'd and","The fraughting souls within her."],"id":47,"m":"If your magic, my dearest father, has made the wild waters roar like this, calm them. It looks as if the sky would pour down stinking tar, except that the sea, rising up to the face of the heavens, puts out its fire. Oh, I have suffered with those I saw suffering! A fine ship, which surely had some noble people in it, smashed to pieces. Oh, their cry hit my very heart! Poor souls, they died. If I had been a powerful god, I would have sunk the sea into the earth before it could swallow the good ship like that, with all the souls it carried."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Be collected:","No more amazement: tell your piteous heart","There's no harm done."],"id":48,"m":"Calm yourself. No more alarm. Tell your pitying heart that no harm has been done."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O! woe the day!"],"id":49,"m":"Oh, what a terrible day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["No harm.","I have done nothing but in care of thee,","Of thee, my dear one, thee, my daughter, who","Art ignorant of what thou art, nought knowing","Of whence I am: nor that I am more better","Than Prospero, master of a full poor cell,","And thy no greater father."],"id":50,"m":"No harm. Everything I have done has been to take care of you, you, my dear one, my daughter, who doesn't know who you are, and knows nothing of where I come from, or that I am far greater than Prospero, the master of a very poor cave, and your father, nothing more."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["More to know","Did never meddle with my thoughts."],"id":51,"m":"It never crossed my mind to want to know more."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["'Tis time","I should inform thee farther. Lend thy hand,","And pluck my magic garment from me.—So:"],"id":52,"m":"It's time I told you more. Give me your hand and help me take off my magic cloak. There."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lays down his mantle","id":53},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Lie there my art.—Wipe thou thine eyes; have comfort.","The direful spectacle of the wrack, which touch'd","The very virtue of compassion in thee,","I have with such provision in mine art","So safely ordered that there is no soul—","No, not so much perdition as an hair","Betid to any creature in the vessel","Which thou heard'st cry, which thou saw'st sink. Sit down;","For thou must now know farther."],"id":54,"m":"Lie there, my magic. Dry your eyes and take heart. I have used my art so carefully to stage the dreadful sight of that shipwreck, which stirred all your compassion, that not one soul, not so much as a hair of anyone in that ship, which you heard cry out and saw sink, has been lost. Sit down, because now you must learn more."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["You have often","Begun to tell me what I am: but stopp'd,","And left me to a bootless inquisition,","Concluding 'Stay; not yet.'"],"id":55,"m":"You've often started to tell me who I am, but stopped, and left me wondering in vain, ending with 'Wait; not yet.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["The hour's now come,","The very minute bids thee ope thine ear;","Obey, and be attentive. Canst thou remember","A time before we came unto this cell?","I do not think thou canst: for then thou wast not","Out three years old."],"id":56,"m":"The time has come. This very minute tells you to open your ears. Do as I say and listen closely. Can you remember a time before we came to this cave? I don't think you can, because you weren't quite three years old then."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Certainly, sir, I can."],"id":57,"m":"Yes, sir, I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["By what? By any other house, or person?","Of any thing the image, tell me, that","Hath kept with thy remembrance."],"id":58,"m":"Remember what? Some other house, or person? Tell me any picture of anything that has stayed in your memory."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["'Tis far off,","And rather like a dream than an assurance","That my remembrance warrants. Had I not","Four, or five, women once, that tended me?"],"id":59,"m":"It's a long way off, and more like a dream than something my memory can swear to. Didn't I once have four or five women who looked after me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou hadst, and more, Miranda. But how is it","That this lives in thy mind? What seest thou else","In the dark backward and abysm of time?","If thou rememb'rest aught ere thou cam'st here,","How thou cam'st here, thou mayst."],"id":60,"m":"You did, and more, Miranda. But how is it that this stays in your mind? What else do you see in the dark distant past? If you remember anything from before you came here, you might remember how you came here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["But that I do not."],"id":61,"m":"But I don't."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Twelve year since, Miranda, twelve year since,","Thy father was the Duke of Milan, and","A prince of power."],"id":62,"m":"Twelve years ago, Miranda, twelve years ago, your father was the Duke of Milan and a powerful prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Sir, are not you my father?"],"id":63,"m":"Sir, aren't you my father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thy mother was a piece of virtue, and","She said thou wast my daughter: and thy father","Was Duke of Milan, and his only heir","And princess,—no worse issued."],"id":64,"m":"Your mother was a model of virtue, and she said you were my daughter. And your father was Duke of Milan, and his only heir was a princess, no less nobly born."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O, the heavens!","What foul play had we that we came from thence?","Or blessed was't we did?"],"id":65,"m":"Oh, heavens! What foul play forced us out of there? Or was it a blessing that we left?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Both, both, my girl.","By foul play, as thou say'st, were we heav'd thence;","But blessedly holp hither."],"id":66,"m":"Both, both, my girl. We were thrown out by foul play, as you say, but helped here by a blessing."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O! my heart bleeds","To think o' th' teen that I have turn'd you to,","Which is from my remembrance. Please you, further."],"id":67,"m":"Oh, my heart bleeds to think of the grief I must have caused you, which I can't remember. Please go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["My brother and thy uncle, call'd Antonio—","I pray thee, mark me,—that a brother should","Be so perfidious!—he, whom next thyself,","Of all the world I lov'd, and to him put","The manage of my state; as at that time","Through all the signories it was the first,","And Prospero the prime duke, being so reputed","In dignity, and for the liberal arts,","Without a parallel: those being all my study,","The government I cast upon my brother,","And to my state grew stranger, being transported","And rapt in secret studies. Thy false uncle—","Dost thou attend me?"],"id":68,"m":"My brother, your uncle, called Antonio (please listen to me), that a brother could be so treacherous! He was the one I loved most in the world after you, and I gave him the running of my state. At that time Milan was the first of all the Italian states, and Prospero the leading duke, famous for his rank and without equal in learning. Since learning was all I cared about, I handed the government to my brother and grew a stranger to my own state, carried away and absorbed in secret studies. Your treacherous uncle... are you listening to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Sir, most heedfully."],"id":69,"m":"Sir, very carefully."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Being once perfected how to grant suits,","How to deny them, who t' advance, and who","To trash for over-topping; new created","The creatures that were mine, I say, or chang'd 'em,","Or else new form'd 'em: having both the key","Of officer and office, set all hearts i' th' state","To what tune pleas'd his ear: that now he was","The ivy which had hid my princely trunk,","And suck'd my verdure out on't.—Thou attend'st not."],"id":70,"m":"Once he had mastered how to grant requests and how to refuse them, whom to promote and whom to cut down for growing too tall, he remade the officials who were loyal to me: he either replaced them or changed their loyalties. Holding the key both to the officers and to their posts, he tuned every heart in the state to whatever pleased his ear. So he became the ivy that hid my princely trunk and sucked the life out of it. You're not listening."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O, good sir! I do."],"id":71,"m":"Oh, good sir, I am!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["I pray thee, mark me.","I thus neglecting worldly ends, all dedicated","To closeness and the bettering of my mind","With that, which, but by being so retir'd,","O'er-priz'd all popular rate, in my false brother","Awak'd an evil nature; and my trust,","Like a good parent, did beget of him","A falsehood, in its contrary as great","As my trust was; which had indeed no limit,","A confidence sans bound. He being thus lorded,","Not only with what my revenue yielded,","But what my power might else exact,—like one","Who having, into truth, by telling of it,","Made such a sinner of his memory,","To credit his own lie,—he did believe","He was indeed the Duke; out o' the substitution,","And executing th' outward face of royalty,","With all prerogative.—Hence his ambition growing—","Dost thou hear?"],"id":72,"m":"Please listen to me. I neglected worldly matters and gave myself to solitude and the improvement of my mind, with studies that, if they hadn't kept me so withdrawn, would have been worth more than anything the people value. This woke an evil nature in my treacherous brother. My trust, like a good parent with a bad child, produced in him a falseness as great as my trust, which truly had no limit, a confidence without bounds. Made a lord in this way, not only with my income but with whatever else my power could demand, he was like a man who tells a lie so often that he cheats his own memory into believing it. He came to believe that he really was the Duke, because he was standing in for me and acting out the outward show of royalty with all its privileges. So his ambition grew. Do you hear me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Your tale, sir, would cure deafness."],"id":73,"m":"Your story, sir, would cure deafness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["To have no screen between this part he play'd","And him he play'd it for, he needs will be","Absolute Milan. Me, poor man—my library","Was dukedom large enough: of temporal royalties","He thinks me now incapable; confederates,—","So dry he was for sway,—wi' th' King of Naples","To give him annual tribute, do him homage;","Subject his coronet to his crown, and bend","The dukedom, yet unbow'd—alas, poor Milan!—","To most ignoble stooping."],"id":74,"m":"To remove any barrier between the part he played and the man he played it for, he was determined to be Duke of Milan outright. As for me, poor man, my library was dukedom enough: he now thinks me unfit to rule. He was so thirsty for power that he made a deal with the King of Naples to pay him yearly tribute and do him homage, to place his duke's coronet under the King's crown, and to bend the dukedom, which had never bowed (alas, poor Milan!), into shameful submission."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O the heavens!"],"id":75,"m":"Oh, heavens!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Mark his condition, and the event; then tell me","If this might be a brother."],"id":76,"m":"Note the terms of his deal and what happened, then tell me if this could be a brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["I should sin","To think but nobly of my grandmother:","Good wombs have borne bad sons."],"id":77,"m":"It would be a sin to think anything but well of my grandmother. Good mothers have given birth to bad sons."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Now the condition.","This King of Naples, being an enemy","To me inveterate, hearkens my brother's suit;","Which was, that he, in lieu o' the premises","Of homage and I know not how much tribute,","Should presently extirpate me and mine","Out of the dukedom, and confer fair Milan,","With all the honours on my brother: whereon,","A treacherous army levied, one midnight","Fated to the purpose, did Antonio open","The gates of Milan; and, i' th' dead of darkness,","The ministers for th' purpose hurried thence","Me and thy crying self."],"id":78,"m":"Now hear the terms. This King of Naples, a long-standing enemy of mine, listened to my brother's request. It was that, in return for homage and who knows how much tribute, the King should immediately throw me and my family out of the dukedom and give fair Milan, with all its honours, to my brother. So a treacherous army was raised, and one midnight, chosen for the purpose, Antonio opened the gates of Milan, and in the dead of darkness his agents hurried me and you, crying, out of the city."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Alack, for pity!","I, not rememb'ring how I cried out then,","Will cry it o'er again: it is a hint","That wrings mine eyes to't."],"id":79,"m":"Oh, how sad! I don't remember how I cried then, but I'll cry over it again. The story wrings tears from my eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Hear a little further,","And then I'll bring thee to the present business","Which now's upon us; without the which this story","Were most impertinent."],"id":80,"m":"Hear a little more, and then I'll bring you to the business we're dealing with now, without which this story would be pointless."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Wherefore did they not","That hour destroy us?"],"id":81,"m":"Why didn't they kill us there and then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Well demanded, wench:","My tale provokes that question. Dear, they durst not,","So dear the love my people bore me, nor set","A mark so bloody on the business; but","With colours fairer painted their foul ends.","In few, they hurried us aboard a bark,","Bore us some leagues to sea, where they prepared","A rotten carcass of a boat, not rigg'd,","Nor tackle, sail, nor mast: the very rats","Instinctively have quit it. There they hoist us,","To cry to th' sea, that roar'd to us: to sigh","To th' winds, whose pity, sighing back again,","Did us but loving wrong."],"id":82,"m":"A good question, girl: my story invites it. My dear, they didn't dare, because my people loved me so much, and they didn't want to leave such a bloody stain on their business. Instead they painted over their foul aims with fairer colours. In short, they hurried us onto a ship and carried us some miles out to sea, where they had got ready a rotten wreck of a boat, with no rigging, ropes, sail or mast: even the rats had instinctively left it. There they put us, to cry to the sea, which roared back at us, and to sigh to the winds, whose pity, sighing back, only did us loving harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Alack! what trouble","Was I then to you!"],"id":83,"m":"Oh, what a burden I must have been to you then!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["O, a cherubin","Thou wast that did preserve me! Thou didst smile,","Infused with a fortitude from heaven,","When I have deck'd the sea with drops full salt,","Under my burden groan'd: which rais'd in me","An undergoing stomach, to bear up","Against what should ensue."],"id":84,"m":"Oh, you were a little angel who saved me! You smiled, filled with a courage from heaven, while I wept salt tears into the sea and groaned under my burden. Your smile gave me the courage to endure whatever was coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["How came we ashore?"],"id":85,"m":"How did we get ashore?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["By Providence divine.","Some food we had and some fresh water that","A noble Neapolitan, Gonzalo,","Out of his charity,—who being then appointed","Master of this design,—did give us, with","Rich garments, linens, stuffs, and necessaries,","Which since have steaded much: so, of his gentleness,","Knowing I lov'd my books, he furnish'd me,","From mine own library with volumes that","I prize above my dukedom."],"id":86,"m":"By God's providence. We had some food and some fresh water, which a noble man of Naples, Gonzalo, out of kindness, gave us, since he had been put in charge of the plan. He also gave us rich clothes, linen, cloth and other necessities, which have been very useful since. And in the same kindness, knowing I loved my books, he gave me volumes from my own library that I value more than my dukedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Would I might","But ever see that man!"],"id":87,"m":"I wish I could see that man some day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Now I arise:—"],"id":88,"m":"Now I'll stand up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Resumes his mantle","id":89},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Sit still, and hear the last of our sea-sorrow.","Here in this island we arriv'd: and here","Have I, thy schoolmaster, made thee more profit","Than other princes can, that have more time","For vainer hours, and tutors not so careful."],"id":90,"m":"Sit still and hear the end of our sea story. We arrived on this island, and here I, as your teacher, have given you a better education than other princesses get, who have more time for idle pastimes and tutors who are less careful."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Heavens thank you for't! And now, I pray you, sir,—","For still 'tis beating in my mind,—your reason","For raising this sea-storm?"],"id":91,"m":"Heaven thank you for it! And now, please, sir, since it is still pounding in my mind, why did you raise this storm?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Know thus far forth.","By accident most strange, bountiful Fortune,","Now my dear lady, hath mine enemies","Brought to this shore; and by my prescience","I find my zenith doth depend upon","A most auspicious star, whose influence","If now I court not but omit, my fortunes","Will ever after droop. Here cease more questions;","Thou art inclin'd to sleep; 'tis a good dulness,","And give it way;—I know thou canst not choose.—"],"id":92,"m":"Know this much. By a very strange accident, generous Fortune, now my friend, has brought my enemies to this shore. And I can see by my foreknowledge that my highest point depends on a very lucky star: if I don't seize its influence now but let it pass, my fortunes will sink for ever after. No more questions now. You're drowsy. It's a good sleepiness, so give in to it. I know you can't help it."},{"t":"sd","text":"MIRANDA sleeps","id":93},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Come away, servant, come! I am ready now.","Approach, my Ariel; Come!"],"id":94,"m":"Come here, servant, come! I'm ready now. Come close, my Ariel. Come!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ARIEL","id":95},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["All hail, great master! grave sir, hail! I come","To answer thy best pleasure; be't to fly,","To swim, to dive into the fire, to ride","On the curl'd clouds; to thy strong bidding task","Ariel and all his quality."],"id":96,"m":"Greetings, great master! Wise sir, greetings! I've come to do whatever you wish, whether it's to fly, to swim, to dive into fire, or to ride on the curling clouds. Set Ariel and all his fellow spirits to work on your strong command."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Hast thou, spirit,","Perform'd to point the tempest that I bade thee?"],"id":97,"m":"Spirit, did you perform the storm exactly as I told you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["To every article.","I boarded the King's ship; now on the beak,","Now in the waist, the deck, in every cabin,","I flam'd amazement; sometime I'd divide,","And burn in many places; on the topmast,","The yards, and boresprit, would I flame distinctly,","Then meet and join: Jove's lightning, the precursors","O' th' dreadful thunder-claps, more momentary","And sight-outrunning were not: the fire and cracks","Of sulphurous roaring the most mighty Neptune","Seem to besiege and make his bold waves tremble,","Yea, his dread trident shake."],"id":98,"m":"Down to every detail. I boarded the King's ship. Now at the bow, now in the middle, on the deck, in every cabin, I blazed up terror. Sometimes I split myself and burned in many places at once; on the topmast, the yards and the bowsprit I would flame separately, then meet and join together. Jove's lightning bolts, which come before the terrible thunderclaps, were not quicker or more dazzling. The fire and the cracks of sulphur-smelling roaring seemed to lay siege to mighty Neptune himself, make his bold waves tremble, and even shake his dreadful trident."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["My brave spirit!","Who was so firm, so constant, that this coil","Would not infect his reason?"],"id":99,"m":"My brave spirit! Was anyone so steady and calm that this uproar didn't affect his mind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Not a soul","But felt a fever of the mad, and play'd","Some tricks of desperation. All but mariners","Plunged in the foaming brine and quit the vessel,","Then all afire with me: the King's son, Ferdinand,","With hair up-staring—then like reeds, not hair—","Was the first man that leapt; cried 'Hell is empty,","And all the devils are here.'"],"id":100,"m":"Not a soul. Every one of them felt a madman's fever and did something desperate. All except the sailors plunged into the foaming sea and abandoned the ship, which was all on fire with me. The King's son, Ferdinand, with his hair standing on end like reeds, not hair, was the first man to jump. He cried, 'Hell is empty, and all the devils are here.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Why, that's my spirit!","But was not this nigh shore?"],"id":101,"m":"That's my spirit! But wasn't this close to shore?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Close by, my master."],"id":102,"m":"Close by, my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["But are they, Ariel, safe?"],"id":103,"m":"But are they safe, Ariel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Not a hair perish'd;","On their sustaining garments not a blemish,","But fresher than before: and, as thou bad'st me,","In troops I have dispers'd them 'bout the isle.","The king's son have I landed by himself,","Whom I left cooling of the air with sighs","In an odd angle of the isle, and sitting,","His arms in this sad knot."],"id":104,"m":"Not a hair was lost. Their clothes, which kept them afloat, don't have a stain on them; they're fresher than before. And as you told me, I've scattered them in groups around the island. The King's son I brought ashore by himself. I left him cooling the air with his sighs in a remote corner of the island, sitting with his arms folded sadly, like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Of the King's ship","The mariners, say how thou hast dispos'd,","And all the rest o' th' fleet?"],"id":105,"m":"Tell me what you've done with the sailors of the King's ship, and all the rest of the fleet."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Safely in harbour","Is the King's ship; in the deep nook, where once","Thou call'dst me up at midnight to fetch dew","From the still-vex'd Bermoothes; there she's hid:","The mariners all under hatches stowed;","Who, with a charm join'd to their suff'red labour,","I have left asleep: and for the rest o' th' fleet","Which I dispers'd, they all have met again,","And are upon the Mediterranean flote","Bound sadly home for Naples,","Supposing that they saw the king's ship wrack'd,","And his great person perish."],"id":106,"m":"The King's ship is safe in harbour, in the deep bay where you once called me up at midnight to fetch dew from the always stormy Bermudas. She's hidden there. The sailors are all stowed below deck; with a spell added to their exhaustion I have left them asleep. As for the rest of the fleet, which I scattered, they have all met up again and are on the Mediterranean sea, sailing sadly home to Naples, believing they saw the King's ship wrecked and the King himself die."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Ariel, thy charge","Exactly is perform'd; but there's more work:","What is the time o' th' day?"],"id":107,"m":"Ariel, you have done your job exactly, but there is more work to do. What time of day is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Past the mid season."],"id":108,"m":"Past noon."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["At least two glasses. The time 'twixt six and now","Must by us both be spent most preciously."],"id":109,"m":"By at least two hours. The time between now and six o'clock must be used very carefully by both of us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Is there more toil? Since thou dost give me pains,","Let me remember thee what thou hast promis'd,","Which is not yet perform'd me."],"id":110,"m":"Is there more work? Since you're giving me more labour, let me remind you of what you promised me, which you haven't given me yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["How now! moody?","What is't thou canst demand?"],"id":111,"m":"What's this? Sulking? What can you possibly ask for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["My liberty."],"id":112,"m":"My freedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Before the time be out! No more!"],"id":113,"m":"Before your time is up? Not another word!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I prithee,","Remember I have done thee worthy service;","Told thee no lies, made no mistakings, serv'd","Without or grudge or grumblings: thou didst promise","To bate me a full year."],"id":114,"m":"Please remember that I have served you well. I've told you no lies, made no mistakes, and served you without any resentment or complaining. You promised to cut a whole year off my service."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Dost thou forget","From what a torment I did free thee?"],"id":115,"m":"Have you forgotten the torment I freed you from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["No."],"id":116,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou dost; and think'st it much to tread the ooze","Of the salt deep,","To run upon the sharp wind of the north,","To do me business in the veins o' th' earth","When it is bak'd with frost."],"id":117,"m":"You have. And you think it's a lot to walk on the mud of the salty sea bed, to run on the sharp north wind, to do my work in the veins of the earth when it's frozen hard."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I do not, sir."],"id":118,"m":"I don't, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou liest, malignant thing! Hast thou forgot","The foul witch Sycorax, who with age and envy","Was grown into a hoop? Hast thou forgot her?"],"id":119,"m":"You're lying, you spiteful thing! Have you forgotten the foul witch Sycorax, who was bent double with age and malice? Have you forgotten her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["No, sir."],"id":120,"m":"No, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou hast. Where was she born?","Speak; tell me."],"id":121,"m":"You have. Where was she born? Speak, tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Sir, in Argier."],"id":122,"m":"In Algiers, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["O! was she so? I must","Once in a month recount what thou hast been,","Which thou forget'st. This damn'd witch Sycorax,","For mischiefs manifold, and sorceries terrible","To enter human hearing, from Argier,","Thou know'st, was banish'd: for one thing she did","They would not take her life. Is not this true?"],"id":123,"m":"Oh, was she? Once a month I have to remind you what you've been, since you forget it. This damned witch Sycorax, as you know, was banished from Algiers for countless crimes and for sorceries too terrible for human ears. Because of one thing she did, they wouldn't execute her. Isn't that true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Ay, sir."],"id":124,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["This blue-ey'd hag was hither brought with child,","And here was left by the sailors. Thou, my slave,","As thou report'st thyself, wast then her servant:","And, for thou wast a spirit too delicate","To act her earthy and abhorr'd commands,","Refusing her grand hests, she did confine thee,","By help of her more potent ministers,","And in her most unmitigable rage,","Into a cloven pine; within which rift","Imprison'd, thou didst painfully remain","A dozen years; within which space she died,","And left thee there, where thou didst vent thy groans","As fast as mill-wheels strike. Then was this island—","Save for the son that she did litter here,","A freckl'd whelp, hag-born—not honour'd with","A human shape."],"id":125,"m":"This hag with the blue-ringed eyes was brought here pregnant and left here by the sailors. You, my slave, as you describe yourself, were her servant then. And because you were a spirit too delicate to carry out her coarse and hateful orders, and refused her great commands, she imprisoned you, with the help of her stronger servants and in her most relentless rage, in a split pine tree. Imprisoned in that crack you stayed in pain for twelve years. During that time she died and left you there, where you groaned as fast as the paddles of a mill wheel strike the water. At that time this island had no human form to honour it, except for the son she gave birth to here, a freckled pup born of a hag."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Yes; Caliban her son."],"id":126,"m":"Yes, Caliban, her son."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Dull thing, I say so; he, that Caliban,","Whom now I keep in service. Thou best know'st","What torment I did find thee in; thy groans","Did make wolves howl, and penetrate the breasts","Of ever-angry bears: it was a torment","To lay upon the damn'd, which Sycorax","Could not again undo; it was mine art,","When I arriv'd and heard thee, that made gape","The pine, and let thee out."],"id":127,"m":"That's what I said, stupid: that Caliban whom I now keep as a servant. You know best what torment I found you in. Your groans made wolves howl and pierced the hearts of ever-angry bears. It was a torment fit for the damned, which Sycorax could not undo. It was my magic, when I arrived and heard you, that made the pine split open and let you out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I thank thee, master."],"id":128,"m":"I thank you, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["If thou more murmur'st, I will rend an oak","And peg thee in his knotty entrails till","Thou hast howl'd away twelve winters."],"id":129,"m":"If you complain any more, I'll split an oak and peg you inside its knotty insides until you've howled away twelve winters."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Pardon, master:","I will be correspondent to command,","And do my spriting gently."],"id":130,"m":"Forgive me, master. I'll obey your commands and do my spirit work willingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Do so; and after two days","I will discharge thee."],"id":131,"m":"Do that, and in two days I'll set you free."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["That's my noble master!","What shall I do? Say what? What shall I do?"],"id":132,"m":"That's my noble master! What shall I do? Tell me what? What shall I do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Go make thyself like a nymph o' th' sea: be subject","To no sight but thine and mine; invisible","To every eyeball else. Go, take this shape,","And hither come in 't: go, hence with diligence!"],"id":133,"m":"Go and turn yourself into a sea nymph. Be visible to no eyes but yours and mine, invisible to everyone else's. Go, take this shape and come back here in it. Go now, and hurry!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit ARIEL","id":134},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Awake, dear heart, awake! thou hast slept well;","Awake!"],"id":135,"m":"Wake up, dear heart, wake up! You've slept well. Wake up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["[Waking] The strangeness of your story put","Heaviness in me."],"id":136,"m":"The strangeness of your story made me drowsy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Shake it off. Come on;","We'll visit Caliban my slave, who never","Yields us kind answer."],"id":137,"m":"Shake it off. Come on, we'll visit my slave Caliban, who never gives us a kind answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["'Tis a villain, sir,","I do not love to look on."],"id":138,"m":"He's a villain, sir, and I don't like looking at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["But as 'tis,","We cannot miss him: he does make our fire,","Fetch in our wood; and serves in offices","That profit us.—What ho! slave! Caliban!","Thou earth, thou! Speak."],"id":139,"m":"But as things are, we can't do without him. He makes our fire, fetches our wood, and does jobs that are useful to us. Hey there! Slave! Caliban! You lump of earth! Speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["[Within] There's wood enough within."],"id":140,"m":"There's enough wood in there."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Come forth, I say; there's other business for thee:","Come, thou tortoise! when?"],"id":141,"m":"Come out, I said. There's other work for you. Come on, you tortoise! When are you coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ARIEL like a water-nymph.","id":142},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Fine apparition! My quaint Ariel,","Hark in thine ear."],"id":143,"m":"A fine sight! My clever Ariel, listen closely."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["My lord, it shall be done."],"id":144,"m":"My lord, it will be done."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":145},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou poisonous slave, got by the devil himself","Upon thy wicked dam, come forth!"],"id":146,"m":"You poisonous slave, fathered by the devil himself on your wicked mother, come out!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CALIBAN","id":147},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["As wicked dew as e'er my mother brush'd","With raven's feather from unwholesome fen","Drop on you both! A south-west blow on ye,","And blister you all o'er!"],"id":148,"m":"May dew as poisonous as any my mother ever swept with a raven's feather from an unhealthy swamp drop on both of you! May a south-west wind blow on you and cover you in blisters!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["For this, be sure, to-night thou shalt have cramps,","Side-stitches that shall pen thy breath up; urchins","Shall forth at vast of night that they may work","All exercise on thee: thou shalt be pinch'd","As thick as honeycomb, each pinch more stinging","Than bees that made them."],"id":149,"m":"For that, you can be sure, tonight you'll have cramps and stitches in your side that will choke your breath. Goblins in the shape of hedgehogs will come out in the dead of night to work all their torments on you. You'll be pinched as full of holes as a honeycomb, each pinch stinging more than the bees that made it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I must eat my dinner.","This island's mine, by Sycorax my mother,","Which thou tak'st from me. When thou cam'st first,","Thou strok'st me and made much of me; wouldst give me","Water with berries in't; and teach me how","To name the bigger light, and how the less,","That burn by day and night: and then I lov'd thee,","And show'd thee all the qualities o' th' isle,","The fresh springs, brine-pits, barren place, and fertile.","Curs'd be I that did so! All the charms","Of Sycorax, toads, beetles, bats, light on you!","For I am all the subjects that you have,","Which first was mine own king; and here you sty me","In this hard rock, whiles you do keep from me","The rest o' th' island."],"id":150,"m":"I have to eat my dinner. This island is mine, through my mother Sycorax, and you're taking it from me. When you first came, you stroked me and made a fuss of me. You gave me water with berries in it, and taught me the names of the bigger light and the smaller one that burn by day and night. And then I loved you, and showed you all the features of the island: the fresh springs, the salt pits, the barren places and the fertile ones. Curse me for doing it! May all the spells of Sycorax, toads, beetles, bats, fall on you! I am the only subject you have, and I used to be my own king. Now you pen me like a pig in this hard rock, while you keep the rest of the island from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou most lying slave,","Whom stripes may move, not kindness! I have us'd thee,","Filth as thou art, with human care, and lodg'd thee","In mine own cell, till thou didst seek to violate","The honour of my child."],"id":151,"m":"You lying slave, who can be moved by whipping but not by kindness! Filth as you are, I treated you with human care and let you live in my own cave, until you tried to rape my child."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Oh ho! Oh ho! Would it had been done!","Thou didst prevent me; I had peopl'd else","This isle with Calibans."],"id":152,"m":"Oh ho! Oh ho! I wish I had done it! You stopped me, or else I would have filled this island with little Calibans."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Abhorred slave,","Which any print of goodness wilt not take,","Being capable of all ill! I pitied thee,","Took pains to make thee speak, taught thee each hour","One thing or other: when thou didst not, savage,","Know thine own meaning, but wouldst gabble like","A thing most brutish, I endow'd thy purposes","With words that made them known: but thy vile race,","Though thou didst learn, had that in't which good natures","Could not abide to be with; therefore wast thou","Deservedly confin'd into this rock, who hadst","Deserv'd more than a prison."],"id":153,"m":"Hateful slave, who will take no mark of goodness and is capable of every evil! I pitied you. I took pains to teach you to speak, taught you one thing or another every hour. When you, savage, didn't know your own meaning but would gabble like a brute, I gave your thoughts words to make them known. But your vile nature, though you learned, had something in it that good natures could not bear to be near. So you were rightly confined to this rock, though you deserved more than a prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["You taught me language, and my profit on't","Is, I know how to curse: the red plague rid you,","For learning me your language!"],"id":154,"m":"You taught me language, and all I've gained from it is that I know how to curse. May the red plague kill you for teaching me your language!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Hag-seed, hence!","Fetch us in fuel; and be quick, thou 'rt best,","To answer other business. Shrug'st thou, malice?","If thou neglect'st, or dost unwillingly","What I command, I'll rack thee with old cramps,","Fill all thy bones with aches; make thee roar,","That beasts shall tremble at thy din."],"id":155,"m":"Child of a hag, go! Fetch us firewood, and you'd better be quick, so you can do other jobs. Are you shrugging, you spiteful thing? If you neglect or do unwillingly what I command, I'll torture you with old people's cramps, fill all your bones with aches, and make you roar so loud that wild animals will tremble at your noise."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["No, pray thee.—","[Aside] I must obey. His art is of such power,","It would control my dam's god, Setebos,","And make a vassal of him."],"id":156,"m":"No, please! I have to obey. His magic is so powerful it could control my mother's god, Setebos, and make him his servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["So, slave: hence!"],"id":157,"m":"So, slave, go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit CALIBAN","id":158},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ARIEL invisible, playing and singing; FERDINAND following","id":159},{"t":"sd","text":"Ariel sings","id":160},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","cont":1,"lines":["Come unto these yellow sands,","And then take hands:","Curtsied when you have, and kiss'd,—","The wild waves whist,—","Foot it featly here and there;","And, sweet sprites, the burden bear.","Hark, hark!","[Burden: Bow, wow, dispersedly.]","The watch dogs bark:","[Burden: Bow, wow, dispersedly.]","Hark, hark! I hear","The strain of strutting Chanticleer","[Cry, Cock-a-diddle-dow.]"],"id":161,"m":"Come to these yellow sands, and then join hands. When you have bowed and kissed, and the wild waves are hushed, dance nimbly here and there, and, sweet spirits, sing the chorus. Listen, listen! The watchdogs bark. Listen, listen! I hear the call of the strutting rooster."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Where should this music be? i' th' air or th' earth?","It sounds no more;—and sure it waits upon","Some god o' th' island. Sitting on a bank,","Weeping again the king my father's wrack,","This music crept by me upon the waters,","Allaying both their fury and my passion,","With its sweet air: thence I have follow'd it,—","Or it hath drawn me rather,—but 'tis gone.","No, it begins again."],"id":162,"m":"Where can this music be coming from? The air or the earth? It has stopped. Surely it is playing for some god of the island. While I sat on a bank, weeping again over the wreck of my father the King, this music crept past me over the waters, calming both their fury and my grief with its sweet tune. I have followed it from there, or rather it has drawn me, but it's gone. No, it's starting again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Ariel sings","id":163},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","cont":1,"lines":["Full fathom five thy father lies:","Of his bones are coral made:","Those are pearls that were his eyes:","Nothing of him that doth fade","But doth suffer a sea-change","Into something rich and strange.","Sea-nymphs hourly ring his knell:","[Burden: Ding-dong.]","Hark! now I hear them—ding-dong, bell."],"id":164,"m":"Your father lies thirty feet deep. His bones have turned to coral, and those are pearls that were his eyes. Nothing of him that might decay but has been changed by the sea into something rich and strange. Sea nymphs ring his funeral bell every hour. Listen! Now I hear them: ding-dong, bell."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["The ditty does remember my drown'd father.","This is no mortal business, nor no sound","That the earth owes:—I hear it now above me."],"id":165,"m":"The song remembers my drowned father. This is no human doing, and no sound that belongs to the earth. Now I hear it above me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["The fringed curtains of thine eye advance,","And say what thou seest yond."],"id":166,"m":"Lift up the fringed curtains of your eyes and tell me what you see over there."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["What is't? a spirit?","Lord, how it looks about! Believe me, sir,","It carries a brave form:—but 'tis a spirit."],"id":167,"m":"What is it? A spirit? Lord, how it looks around! Believe me, sir, it has a splendid shape. But it's a spirit."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["No, wench; it eats and sleeps, and hath such senses","As we have, such; this gallant which thou see'st","Was in the wrack; and but he's something stain'd","With grief,—that beauty's canker,—thou mightst call him","A goodly person: he hath lost his fellows","And strays about to find 'em."],"id":168,"m":"No, girl. It eats and sleeps and has the same senses we have, the very same. This fine young man you see was in the shipwreck. If he weren't somewhat marked by grief, which eats away at beauty, you might call him handsome. He has lost his companions and is wandering about looking for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["I might call him","A thing divine; for nothing natural","I ever saw so noble."],"id":169,"m":"I would call him something divine, because I've never seen anything natural so noble."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] It goes on, I see,","As my soul prompts it.—Spirit, fine spirit! I'll free thee","Within two days for this."],"id":170,"m":"It's going just as I planned. Spirit, fine spirit! I'll free you within two days for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Most sure, the goddess","On whom these airs attend!—Vouchsafe, my prayer","May know if you remain upon this island;","And that you will some good instruction give","How I may bear me here: my prime request,","Which I do last pronounce, is,—O you wonder!—","If you be maid or no?"],"id":171,"m":"Surely this is the goddess whom this music serves! Please grant my prayer and tell me if you live on this island, and give me some good advice on how to behave here. My chief request, which I make last, is (oh, you wonder!) whether you are a human girl or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["No wonder, sir;","But certainly a maid."],"id":172,"m":"No wonder, sir, but certainly a girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["My language! Heavens!—","I am the best of them that speak this speech,","Were I but where 'tis spoken."],"id":173,"m":"She speaks my language! Heavens! I would be the highest-ranking person who speaks it, if I were back where it's spoken."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["How! the best?","What wert thou, if the King of Naples heard thee?"],"id":174,"m":"What? The highest? What would you be if the King of Naples heard you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["A single thing, as I am now, that wonders","To hear thee speak of Naples. He does hear me;","And, that he does, I weep: myself am Naples,","Who with mine eyes,—never since at ebb,—beheld","The King, my father wrack'd."],"id":175,"m":"A lone man, as I am now, who is amazed to hear you speak of Naples. The King of Naples does hear me, and I weep because he does. I myself am King of Naples, since I saw with my own eyes, which have not stopped crying since, the King my father wrecked."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Alack, for mercy!"],"id":176,"m":"Oh, have mercy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Yes, faith, and all his lords, the Duke of Milan,","And his brave son being twain."],"id":177,"m":"Yes, truly, and all his lords, including the Duke of Milan and his fine son."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside.] The Duke of Milan,","And his more braver daughter could control thee,","If now 'twere fit to do't.—At the first sight [Aside.]","They have changed eyes;—delicate Ariel,","I'll set thee free for this!—[To FERDINAND] A word, good sir:","I fear you have done yourself some wrong: a word."],"id":178,"m":"The real Duke of Milan and his even finer daughter could prove you wrong, if this were the right time. At first sight they have fallen in love. Delicate Ariel, I'll set you free for this! A word, good sir. I'm afraid you've made a mistake. A word."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["[Aside.] Why speaks my father so ungently? This","Is the third man that e'er I saw; the first","That e'er I sigh'd for; pity move my father","To be inclin'd my way!"],"id":179,"m":"Why does my father speak so roughly? This is the third man I've ever seen, and the first I've ever sighed for. May pity move my father to feel as I do!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["[Aside.] O! if a virgin,","And your affection not gone forth, I'll make you","The Queen of Naples."],"id":180,"m":"Oh, if you're a virgin and you haven't given your heart to anyone, I'll make you Queen of Naples."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Soft, sir; one word more—","[Aside] They are both in either's powers: but this swift","business I must uneasy make, lest too light winning","Make the prize light. [To FERDINAND] One word more:","I charge thee","That thou attend me. Thou dost here usurp","The name thou ow'st not; and hast put thyself","Upon this island as a spy, to win it","From me, the lord on't."],"id":181,"m":"Wait, sir, one more word. They're both in each other's power. But I must make this quick business difficult, in case winning too easily makes the prize seem cheap. One more word. I order you to listen to me. You are claiming a title that isn't yours, and you've come to this island as a spy, to take it from me, its lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["No, as I am a man."],"id":182,"m":"No, on my word as a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["There's nothing ill can dwell in such a temple:","If the ill spirit have so fair a house,","Good things will strive to dwell with't."],"id":183,"m":"Nothing evil can live in such a temple. If an evil spirit had such a beautiful house, good things would fight to live there with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[To FERDINAND] Follow me.—","[To MIRANDA] Speak not you for him; he's a traitor.—","[To FERDINAND] Come;","I'll manacle thy neck and feet together:","Sea-water shalt thou drink; thy food shall be","The fresh-brook mussels, wither'd roots, and husks","Wherein the acorn cradled. Follow."],"id":184,"m":"Follow me. Don't speak up for him: he's a traitor. Come on. I'll chain your neck and feet together. You'll drink sea water, and your food will be freshwater mussels, withered roots and the husks that held acorns. Follow me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["No;","I will resist such entertainment till","Mine enemy has more power."],"id":185,"m":"No. I'll resist that kind of treatment until my enemy proves stronger than me."},{"t":"sd","text":"He draws, and is charmed from moving.","id":186},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O dear father!","Make not too rash a trial of him, for","He's gentle, and not fearful."],"id":187,"m":"Oh, dear father, don't judge him too harshly and too quickly. He's noble, not a coward."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["What! I say,","My foot my tutor? Put thy sword up, traitor;","Who mak'st a show, but dar'st not strike, thy conscience","Is so possess'd with guilt: come from thy ward,","For I can here disarm thee with this stick","And make thy weapon drop."],"id":188,"m":"What? Is my foot going to teach me, its head? Put your sword away, traitor. You make a show of fighting but don't dare to strike, because your conscience is so full of guilt. Drop your guard, because I can disarm you with this stick and make your weapon fall."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Beseech you, father!"],"id":189,"m":"Please, father!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Hence! Hang not on my garments."],"id":190,"m":"Get away! Don't hang on my clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Sir, have pity;","I'll be his surety."],"id":191,"m":"Sir, have pity. I'll vouch for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Silence! One word more","Shall make me chide thee, if not hate thee. What!","An advocate for an impostor? hush!","Thou think'st there is no more such shapes as he,","Having seen but him and Caliban: foolish wench!","To the most of men this is a Caliban,","And they to him are angels."],"id":192,"m":"Quiet! One more word will make me scold you, if not hate you. What! Speaking up for a fraud? Hush! You think there are no other men as handsome as him, having seen only him and Caliban. Foolish girl! Compared with most men, this one is a Caliban, and they are angels next to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["My affections","Are then most humble; I have no ambition","To see a goodlier man."],"id":193,"m":"Then my love is very humble. I have no wish to see a better-looking man."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[To FERDINAND] Come on; obey:","Thy nerves are in their infancy again,","And have no vigour in them."],"id":194,"m":"Come on, obey. Your muscles are as weak as a baby's again and have no strength in them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["So they are:","My spirits, as in a dream, are all bound up.","My father's loss, the weakness which I feel,","The wrack of all my friends, nor this man's threats,","To whom I am subdued, are but light to me,","Might I but through my prison once a day","Behold this maid: all corners else o' th' earth","Let liberty make use of; space enough","Have I in such a prison."],"id":195,"m":"So they are. My spirits are all tied up, as if in a dream. My father's loss, the weakness I feel, the wreck of all my friends, and the threats of this man who has overpowered me are all light burdens, if only I may see this girl through my prison window once a day. Let free men use every other corner of the earth: I'll have space enough in such a prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] It works.—[To FERDINAND] Come on.—","Thou hast done well, fine Ariel! [To FERDINAND] Follow me.—","[To ARIEL] Hark what thou else shalt do me."],"id":196,"m":"It's working. Come on. You've done well, fine Ariel! Follow me. Listen to what else you must do for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Be of comfort;","My father's of a better nature, sir,","Than he appears by speech: this is unwonted,","Which now came from him."],"id":197,"m":"Take heart. My father has a kinder nature, sir, than his words suggest. What he just said is not like him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou shalt be as free","As mountain winds; but then exactly do","All points of my command."],"id":198,"m":"You'll be as free as the mountain winds. But first, carry out every detail of my orders exactly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["To the syllable."],"id":199,"m":"To the letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[To FERDINAND] Come, follow.—Speak not for him."],"id":200,"m":"Come, follow me. Don't speak up for him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":201},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Another part of the island.","id":202},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ALONSO, SEBASTIAN, ANTONIO, GONZALO, ADRIAN, FRANCISCO, and OTHERS","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Beseech you, sir, be merry; you have cause,","So have we all, of joy; for our escape","Is much beyond our loss. Our hint of woe","Is common: every day, some sailor's wife,","The masters of some merchant and the merchant,","Have just our theme of woe; but for the miracle,","I mean our preservation, few in millions","Can speak like us: then wisely, good sir, weigh","Our sorrow with our comfort."],"id":204,"m":"Please, sir, cheer up. You have reason to be joyful, as we all do, because our escape is far greater than our loss. Our cause for sorrow is common. Every day some sailor's wife, the officers of some merchant ship and the merchant himself have the same reason to grieve as we do. But as for the miracle, I mean our survival, very few in millions can talk like us. So, good sir, weigh our sorrow wisely against our comfort."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Prithee, peace."],"id":205,"m":"Please, be quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["He receives comfort like cold porridge."],"id":206,"m":"He takes comfort like cold porridge."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["The visitor will not give him o'er so."],"id":207,"m":"The comforter won't give up on him so easily."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Look, he's winding up the watch of his wit; by","and by it will strike."],"id":208,"m":"Look, he's winding up the clock of his wit. Soon it will strike."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Sir,—"],"id":209,"m":"Sir..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["One: tell."],"id":210,"m":"One. Count them."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["When every grief is entertain'd that's offer'd,","Comes to the entertainer—"],"id":211,"m":"When a man takes in every grief that comes his way, what he gets for it is..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A dollar."],"id":212,"m":"A dollar."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Dolour comes to him, indeed: you have spoken","truer than you purposed."],"id":213,"m":"Dolour, sorrow, is what he gets, indeed. You spoke more truly than you meant to."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["You have taken it wiselier than I meant you should."],"id":214,"m":"You took it more cleverly than I intended."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Therefore, my lord,—"],"id":215,"m":"So, my lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Fie, what a spendthrift is he of his tongue!"],"id":216,"m":"Ugh, how he wastes his tongue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I prithee, spare."],"id":217,"m":"Please, spare me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Well, I have done: but yet—"],"id":218,"m":"Well, I've finished. But still..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["He will be talking."],"id":219,"m":"He will keep talking."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Which, of he or Adrian, for a good wager, first","begins to crow?"],"id":220,"m":"I'll bet you: who'll start crowing first, him or Adrian?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["The old cock."],"id":221,"m":"The old rooster."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["The cockerel."],"id":222,"m":"The young cockerel."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Done. The wager?"],"id":223,"m":"Agreed. What's the bet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["A laughter."],"id":224,"m":"A laugh."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A match!"],"id":225,"m":"Done!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Though this island seem to be desert,—"],"id":226,"m":"Although this island seems to be deserted..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Ha, ha, ha! So, you're paid."],"id":227,"m":"Ha, ha, ha! There, you've been paid."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Uninhabitable, and almost inaccessible,—"],"id":228,"m":"Uninhabitable, and almost impossible to reach..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Yet—"],"id":229,"m":"Yet..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Yet—"],"id":230,"m":"Yet..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["He could not miss it."],"id":231,"m":"He couldn't help saying it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["It must needs be of subtle, tender, and delicate","temperance."],"id":232,"m":"It must have a fine, gentle and delicate climate."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Temperance was a delicate wench."],"id":233,"m":"Temperance was a delicate girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Ay, and a subtle; as he most learnedly delivered."],"id":234,"m":"Yes, and a fine one, as he so learnedly told us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["The air breathes upon us here most sweetly."],"id":235,"m":"The air breathes on us very sweetly here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["As if it had lungs, and rotten ones."],"id":236,"m":"As if it had lungs, and rotten ones."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Or, as 'twere perfum'd by a fen."],"id":237,"m":"Or as if it had been perfumed by a swamp."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Here is everything advantageous to life."],"id":238,"m":"Everything here is good for life."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["True; save means to live."],"id":239,"m":"True, except the means to live."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Of that there's none, or little."],"id":240,"m":"There's none of that, or very little."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["How lush and lusty the grass looks! how green!"],"id":241,"m":"How rich and healthy the grass looks! How green!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["The ground indeed is tawny."],"id":242,"m":"Actually, the ground is brown."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["With an eye of green in't."],"id":243,"m":"With a spot of green in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["He misses not much."],"id":244,"m":"He doesn't miss much."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["No; he doth but mistake the truth totally."],"id":245,"m":"No, he just gets the truth completely wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["But the rarity of it is,—which is indeed almost","beyond credit,—"],"id":246,"m":"But the remarkable thing, which is almost beyond belief..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["As many vouch'd rarities are."],"id":247,"m":"Like a lot of guaranteed marvels."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["That our garments, being, as they were, drenched","in the sea, hold notwithstanding their freshness and","glosses, being rather new-dyed than stain'd with salt","water."],"id":248,"m":"Is that our clothes, drenched in the sea as they were, have still kept their freshness and shine, as if they were newly dyed rather than stained with salt water."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["If but one of his pockets could speak, would it","not say he lies?"],"id":249,"m":"If just one of his pockets could talk, wouldn't it say he's lying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Ay, or very falsely pocket up his report."],"id":250,"m":"Yes, or else very dishonestly hide what it knows."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Methinks, our garments are now as fresh as when","we put them on first in Afric, at the marriage of the","king's fair daughter Claribel to the King of Tunis."],"id":251,"m":"I think our clothes are as fresh now as when we first put them on in Africa, at the marriage of the King's beautiful daughter Claribel to the King of Tunis."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["'Twas a sweet marriage, and we prosper well in our return."],"id":252,"m":"It was a lovely wedding, and we're doing very nicely on the way home."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Tunis was never graced before with such a paragon","to their queen."],"id":253,"m":"Tunis was never honoured with such a perfect queen before."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Not since widow Dido's time."],"id":254,"m":"Not since the time of the widow Dido."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Widow! a pox o' that! How came that widow in? Widow Dido!"],"id":255,"m":"Widow? Damn that! Where did that widow come from? Widow Dido!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["What if he had said, widower Aeneas too?","Good Lord, how you take it!"],"id":256,"m":"What if he'd said 'widower Aeneas' as well? Good Lord, how you take things!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Widow Dido said you? You make me study of that; she was of","Carthage, not of Tunis."],"id":257,"m":"Did you say widow Dido? You make me think about that. She was from Carthage, not Tunis."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["This Tunis, sir, was Carthage."],"id":258,"m":"This Tunis, sir, was Carthage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Carthage?"],"id":259,"m":"Carthage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I assure you, Carthage."],"id":260,"m":"I assure you, Carthage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["His word is more than the miraculous harp."],"id":261,"m":"His word is more powerful than the magic harp that built the walls of Thebes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["He hath rais'd the wall, and houses too."],"id":262,"m":"He has raised the walls, and the houses too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["What impossible matter will he make easy next?"],"id":263,"m":"What impossible thing will he make easy next?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I think he will carry this island home in his","pocket, and give it his son for an apple."],"id":264,"m":"I think he'll carry this island home in his pocket and give it to his son as an apple."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And, sowing the kernels of it in the sea, bring","forth more islands."],"id":265,"m":"And plant its seeds in the sea to grow more islands."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Ay."],"id":266,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Why, in good time."],"id":267,"m":"Well, it's about time."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["[To ALONSO.] Sir, we were talking that our garments seem now","as fresh as when we were at Tunis at the marriage of","your daughter, who is now Queen."],"id":268,"m":"Sir, we were saying that our clothes seem as fresh now as when we were in Tunis at the marriage of your daughter, who is now Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And the rarest that e'er came there."],"id":269,"m":"And the finest queen that ever came there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Bate, I beseech you, widow Dido."],"id":270,"m":"Except, if you please, widow Dido."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["O! widow Dido; ay, widow Dido."],"id":271,"m":"Oh, widow Dido! Yes, widow Dido."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Is not, sir, my doublet as fresh as the first day I","wore it? I mean, in a sort."],"id":272,"m":"Isn't my jacket as fresh, sir, as on the first day I wore it? I mean, in a way."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["That sort was well fish'd for."],"id":273,"m":"He fished hard for that 'in a way'."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["When I wore it at your daughter's marriage?"],"id":274,"m":"When I wore it at your daughter's marriage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["You cram these words into mine ears against","The stomach of my sense. Would I had never","Married my daughter there! for, coming thence,","My son is lost; and, in my rate, she too,","Who is so far from Italy remov'd,","I ne'er again shall see her. O thou, mine heir","Of Naples and of Milan! what strange fish","Hath made his meal on thee?"],"id":275,"m":"You stuff these words into my ears though my mind has no appetite for them. I wish I had never married my daughter off there! Because on the way back from there my son was lost, and, as I see it, she is lost too, since she is so far from Italy that I'll never see her again. Oh, my heir to Naples and Milan, what strange fish has made a meal of you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCISCO","lines":["Sir, he may live:","I saw him beat the surges under him,","And ride upon their backs: he trod the water,","Whose enmity he flung aside, and breasted","The surge most swoln that met him: his bold head","'Bove the contentious waves he kept, and oar'd","Himself with his good arms in lusty stroke","To th' shore, that o'er his wave-worn basis bowed,","As stooping to relieve him. I not doubt","He came alive to land."],"id":276,"m":"Sir, he may be alive. I saw him beat the waves beneath him and ride on their backs. He trod the water, pushing aside its hostility, and met the most swollen wave head on. He kept his bold head above the battling waves and rowed himself with strong strokes of his good arms to the shore, whose cliff, worn away by waves, bent over as if stooping to help him. I'm sure he reached land alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["No, no; he's gone."],"id":277,"m":"No, no, he's gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Sir, you may thank yourself for this great loss,","That would not bless our Europe with your daughter,","But rather lose her to an African;","Where she, at least, is banish'd from your eye,","Who hath cause to wet the grief on't."],"id":278,"m":"Sir, you have only yourself to thank for this great loss. You wouldn't bless Europe with your daughter but chose to lose her to an African. There she's at least banished from your sight, which has good reason to weep over it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Prithee, peace."],"id":279,"m":"Please, be quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["You were kneel'd to, and importun'd otherwise","By all of us; and the fair soul herself","Weigh'd between loathness and obedience at","Which end o' th' beam should bow. We have lost your son,","I fear, for ever: Milan and Naples have","More widows in them of this business' making,","Than we bring men to comfort them; the fault's your own."],"id":280,"m":"All of us knelt to you and begged you not to do it. And the fair girl herself was torn between reluctance and obedience, unable to decide which way the scales should tip. We have lost your son, I fear, for ever. Milan and Naples have more widows because of this business than we are bringing men home to comfort them. The fault is your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["So is the dearest of the loss."],"id":281,"m":"So is the worst of the loss."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["My lord Sebastian,","The truth you speak doth lack some gentleness","And time to speak it in; you rub the sore,","When you should bring the plaster."],"id":282,"m":"My lord Sebastian, the truth you speak lacks kindness and a proper time to say it. You rub the sore when you should bring the bandage."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Very well."],"id":283,"m":"Very well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And most chirurgeonly."],"id":284,"m":"And very like a surgeon."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["It is foul weather in us all, good sir,","When you are cloudy."],"id":285,"m":"It's foul weather in all of us, good sir, when you are clouded over."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Foul weather?"],"id":286,"m":"Foul weather?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Very foul."],"id":287,"m":"Very foul."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Had I plantation of this isle, my lord,—"],"id":288,"m":"If I had the colonising of this island, my lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["He'd sow 't with nettle-seed."],"id":289,"m":"He'd sow it with nettle seeds."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Or docks, or mallows."],"id":290,"m":"Or weeds, or mallows."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["And were the king on't, what would I do?"],"id":291,"m":"And if I were king of it, what would I do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["'Scape being drunk for want of wine."],"id":292,"m":"Avoid getting drunk, for lack of wine."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I' the commonwealth I would by contraries","Execute all things; for no kind of traffic","Would I admit; no name of magistrate;","Letters should not be known; riches, poverty,","And use of service, none; contract, succession,","Bourn, bound of land, tilth, vineyard, none;","No use of metal, corn, or wine, or oil;","No occupation; all men idle, all:","And women too, but innocent and pure;","No sovereignty,—"],"id":293,"m":"In my state I would do everything the opposite way from usual. I would allow no kind of trade, no title of magistrate. Reading and writing would be unknown. There would be no riches, no poverty and no servants. No contracts, no inheritance, no boundaries or fences, no ploughed fields or vineyards. No use of metal, grain, wine or oil. No jobs: all men idle, all of them, and women too, but innocent and pure. No ruler..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Yet he would be king on't."],"id":294,"m":"And yet he'd be king of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["The latter end of his commonwealth forgets the beginning."],"id":295,"m":"The end of his state forgets its beginning."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["All things in common nature should produce","Without sweat or endeavour; treason, felony,","Sword, pike, knife, gun, or need of any engine,","Would I not have; but nature should bring forth,","Of it own kind, all foison, all abundance,","To feed my innocent people."],"id":296,"m":"Nature would produce everything for everyone in common, without sweat or effort. Treason, crime, swords, spears, knives, guns or any need for weapons I would not have. Instead nature would bring forth, by itself, every kind of plenty and abundance to feed my innocent people."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["No marrying 'mong his subjects?"],"id":297,"m":"No marriage among his subjects?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["None, man: all idle; whores and knaves."],"id":298,"m":"None, man. All idle: whores and scoundrels."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I would with such perfection govern, sir,","To excel the golden age."],"id":299,"m":"I would govern so perfectly, sir, that I'd surpass the golden age."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Save his Majesty!"],"id":300,"m":"God save his Majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Long live Gonzalo!"],"id":301,"m":"Long live Gonzalo!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["And,—do you mark me, sir?"],"id":302,"m":"And... are you listening to me, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Prithee, no more: thou dost talk nothing to me."],"id":303,"m":"Please, no more. What you say means nothing to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I do well believe your highness; and did it to","minister occasion to these gentlemen, who are of such","sensible and nimble lungs that they always use to laugh","at nothing."],"id":304,"m":"I quite believe your Highness. I said it to give these gentlemen a chance, since their lungs are so sensitive and quick that they always laugh at nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["'Twas you we laugh'd at."],"id":305,"m":"It was you we laughed at."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Who in this kind of merry fooling am nothing to","you; so you may continue, and laugh at nothing still."],"id":306,"m":"And in this kind of merry fooling I am nothing compared with you. So you may carry on and laugh at nothing still."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["What a blow was there given!"],"id":307,"m":"What a blow that was!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["An it had not fallen flat-long."],"id":308,"m":"If only it hadn't landed with the flat of the blade."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["You are gentlemen of brave mettle: you would","lift the moon out of her sphere, if she would continue","in it five weeks without changing."],"id":309,"m":"You are gentlemen of fine spirit. You would lift the moon out of her orbit if she stayed in it for five weeks without changing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ARIEL, invisible, playing solemn music","id":310},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["We would so, and then go a-bat-fowling."],"id":311,"m":"We would, and then use her as a lamp to go hunting birds at night."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Nay, good my lord, be not angry."],"id":312,"m":"No, my good lord, don't be angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["No, I warrant you; I will not adventure my","discretion so weakly. Will you laugh me asleep, for I am","very heavy?"],"id":313,"m":"No, I promise you, I won't risk my good judgment on something so trivial. Will you laugh me to sleep? I feel very drowsy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Go sleep, and hear us."],"id":314,"m":"Go to sleep and listen to us."},{"t":"sd","text":"All sleep but ALONSO, SEBASTIAN, and ANTONIO","id":315},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["What! all so soon asleep! I wish mine eyes","Would, with themselves, shut up my thoughts: I find","They are inclin'd to do so."],"id":316,"m":"What! All asleep so soon! I wish my eyes would close and shut off my thoughts as well. I feel they want to."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Please you, sir,","Do not omit the heavy offer of it:","It seldom visits sorrow; when it doth,","It is a comforter."],"id":317,"m":"Please, sir, don't refuse the drowsiness that's being offered. It seldom comes to people in sorrow, and when it does, it brings comfort."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["We two, my lord,","Will guard your person while you take your rest,","And watch your safety."],"id":318,"m":"We two, my lord, will guard you while you rest and watch over your safety."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Thank you. Wondrous heavy!"],"id":319,"m":"Thank you. I'm amazingly sleepy!"},{"t":"sd","text":"ALONSO sleeps. Exit ARIEL.","id":320},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["What a strange drowsiness possesses them!"],"id":321,"m":"What a strange drowsiness has come over them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["It is the quality o' th' climate."],"id":322,"m":"It's the effect of the climate."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Why","Doth it not then our eyelids sink? I find not","Myself dispos'd to sleep."],"id":323,"m":"Then why doesn't it make our eyelids droop? I don't feel at all sleepy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Nor I: my spirits are nimble.","They fell together all, as by consent;","They dropp'd, as by a thunder-stroke. What might,","Worthy Sebastian? O! what might?—No more:—","And yet methinks I see it in thy face,","What thou should'st be: The occasion speaks thee; and","My strong imagination sees a crown","Dropping upon thy head."],"id":324,"m":"Nor do I. My mind is wide awake. They all fell asleep together, as if by agreement; they dropped as if struck by lightning. What might happen, worthy Sebastian? Oh, what might? No more. And yet I think I see in your face what you ought to be. The opportunity calls to you, and my strong imagination sees a crown dropping onto your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["What! art thou waking?"],"id":325,"m":"What! Are you awake?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Do you not hear me speak?"],"id":326,"m":"Can't you hear me speak?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I do: and surely","It is a sleepy language, and thou speak'st","Out of thy sleep. What is it thou didst say?","This is a strange repose, to be asleep","With eyes wide open; standing, speaking, moving,","And yet so fast asleep."],"id":327,"m":"I can, and surely it's the language of sleep, and you're talking in your sleep. What was it you said? This is a strange kind of rest, to be asleep with your eyes wide open, standing, speaking, moving, and yet fast asleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Noble Sebastian,","Thou let'st thy fortune sleep—die rather: wink'st","Whiles thou art waking."],"id":328,"m":"Noble Sebastian, you let your good fortune sleep, or rather die. You keep your eyes shut while you're awake."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Thou dost snore distinctly:","There's meaning in thy snores."],"id":329,"m":"You snore clearly. There's meaning in your snores."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I am more serious than my custom; you","Must be so too, if heed me: which to do","Trebles thee o'er."],"id":330,"m":"I'm more serious than usual. You must be serious too, if you listen to me, and doing that will make you three times greater."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Well, I am standing water."],"id":331,"m":"Well, I'm like still water."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I'll teach you how to flow."],"id":332,"m":"I'll teach you how to flow in."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Do so: to ebb,","Hereditary sloth instructs me."],"id":333,"m":"Do that. My natural laziness, born a younger brother, teaches me to ebb."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["O!","If you but knew how you the purpose cherish","Whiles thus you mock it! how, in stripping it,","You more invest it! Ebbing men indeed,","Most often, do so near the bottom run","By their own fear or sloth."],"id":334,"m":"Oh, if only you knew how much you love the idea while you mock it! How, by making light of it, you show how much you want it! Men on the ebb usually run aground because of their own fear or laziness."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Prithee, say on:","The setting of thine eye and cheek proclaim","A matter from thee, and a birth, indeed","Which throes thee much to yield."],"id":335,"m":"Please, go on. The look in your eye and your face show you have something to say, something it pains you to bring out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Thus, sir:","Although this lord of weak remembrance, this","Who shall be of as little memory","When he is earth'd, hath here almost persuaded,—","For he's a spirit of persuasion, only","Professes to persuade,—the King his son's alive,","'Tis as impossible that he's undrown'd","As he that sleeps here swims."],"id":336,"m":"It's this, sir. This lord with a weak memory, who will be just as little remembered once he's buried, has almost persuaded the King that his son is alive, since persuading is all he's good for. But it's as impossible that the son hasn't drowned as that this man sleeping here can swim."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I have no hope","That he's undrown'd."],"id":337,"m":"I have no hope that he hasn't drowned."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["O! out of that 'no hope'","What great hope have you! No hope that way is","Another way so high a hope, that even","Ambition cannot pierce a wink beyond,","But doubts discovery there. Will you grant with me","That Ferdinand is drown'd?"],"id":338,"m":"Oh, what great hope you have in that 'no hope'! No hope in that direction is, in another direction, a hope so high that even ambition can't see any further and doubts what it sees there. Do you agree with me that Ferdinand is drowned?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["He's gone."],"id":339,"m":"He's gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Then tell me,","Who's the next heir of Naples?"],"id":340,"m":"Then tell me, who's the next heir to Naples?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Claribel."],"id":341,"m":"Claribel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["She that is Queen of Tunis; she that dwells","Ten leagues beyond man's life; she that from Naples","Can have no note, unless the sun were post—","The Man i' th' Moon's too slow—till newborn chins","Be rough and razorable: she that from whom","We all were sea-swallow'd, though some cast again,","And by that destiny, to perform an act","Whereof what's past is prologue, what to come","In yours and my discharge."],"id":342,"m":"The one who is Queen of Tunis; who lives thirty miles beyond the end of the world; who can get no news from Naples, unless the sun carried the post (the Man in the Moon is too slow), until newborn babies have beards to shave. The one we were all sailing back from when the sea swallowed us, though some of us were thrown up again, and destined by that to perform an act. What has happened is only the prologue; what comes next is up to you and me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["What stuff is this!—How say you?","'Tis true, my brother's daughter's Queen of Tunis;","So is she heir of Naples; 'twixt which regions","There is some space."],"id":343,"m":"What nonsense is this? What are you saying? It's true my brother's daughter is Queen of Tunis, and she is heir to Naples, and there is some distance between the two."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["A space whose every cubit","Seems to cry out 'How shall that Claribel","Measure us back to Naples?—Keep in Tunis,","And let Sebastian wake.'—Say this were death","That now hath seiz'd them; why, they were no worse","Than now they are. There be that can rule Naples","As well as he that sleeps; lords that can prate","As amply and unnecessarily","As this Gonzalo: I myself could make","A chough of as deep chat. O, that you bore","The mind that I do! What a sleep were this","For your advancement! Do you understand me?"],"id":344,"m":"A distance where every foot seems to cry out, 'How will that Claribel follow us back to Naples? Stay in Tunis, and let Sebastian wake up.' Suppose this were death that has taken them now: they would be no worse off than they are. There are people who could rule Naples as well as the man sleeping here, and lords who could chatter as long and as pointlessly as this Gonzalo. I could teach a crow to talk as deeply. Oh, if only you thought as I do! What a sleep this would be for your advancement! Do you understand me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Methinks I do."],"id":345,"m":"I think I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And how does your content","Tender your own good fortune?"],"id":346,"m":"And how does your happiness welcome your own good luck?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I remember","You did supplant your brother Prospero."],"id":347,"m":"I remember you overthrew your brother Prospero."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["True.","And look how well my garments sit upon me;","Much feater than before; my brother's servants","Were then my fellows; now they are my men."],"id":348,"m":"True. And look how well my clothes suit me, much better than before. My brother's servants used to be my equals; now they work for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["But, for your conscience,—"],"id":349,"m":"But what about your conscience?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Ay, sir; where lies that? If 'twere a kibe,","'Twould put me to my slipper: but I feel not","This deity in my bosom: twenty consciences","That stand 'twixt me and Milan, candied be they","And melt ere they molest! Here lies your brother,","No better than the earth he lies upon,","If he were that which now he's like, that's dead:","Whom I, with this obedient steel,—three inches of it,—","Can lay to bed for ever; whiles you, doing thus,","To the perpetual wink for aye might put","This ancient morsel, this Sir Prudence, who","Should not upbraid our course. For all the rest,","They'll take suggestion as a cat laps milk:","They'll tell the clock to any business that","We say befits the hour."],"id":350,"m":"Yes, sir, where is that? If it were a chilblain, it would make me wear a slipper. But I don't feel this god in my heart. If twenty consciences stood between me and Milan, they could turn to sugar and melt before they bothered me! Here lies your brother, no better than the earth he lies on, if he were what he now looks like, that is, dead. With this obedient blade, three inches of it, I can put him to bed for ever, while you, doing the same, could close the eyes for good of this old leftover, this Sir Caution, so he can't condemn what we do. As for the rest, they'll take a hint as easily as a cat laps milk. They'll say the time is right for any business we tell them suits the hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Thy case, dear friend,","Shall be my precedent: as thou got'st Milan,","I'll come by Naples. Draw thy sword: one stroke","Shall free thee from the tribute which thou pay'st,","And I the king shall love thee."],"id":351,"m":"Your example, dear friend, will be my guide. Just as you got Milan, I'll get Naples. Draw your sword. One stroke will free you from the tribute you pay, and I, the King, will love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Draw together:","And when I rear my hand, do you the like,","To fall it on Gonzalo."],"id":352,"m":"Draw together, and when I raise my hand, you do the same, to bring it down on Gonzalo."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["O! but one word."],"id":353,"m":"Oh, just one word."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":354},{"t":"sd","text":"Music. Re-enter ARIEL, invisible.","id":355},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["My master through his art foresees the danger","That you, his friend, are in; and sends me forth—","For else his project dies—to keep thee living.","[Sings in GONZALO'S ear]","While you here do snoring lie,","Open-ey'd Conspiracy","His time doth take.","If of life you keep a care,","Shake off slumber, and beware.","Awake! awake!"],"id":356,"m":"My master knows by his magic the danger that you, his friend, are in, and sends me to keep you alive, or else his plan will fail. While you lie here snoring, wide-eyed conspiracy is seizing its chance. If you care about your life, shake off sleep and beware. Wake up! Wake up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Then let us both be sudden."],"id":357,"m":"Then let's both be quick."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Now, good angels","Preserve the King!"],"id":358,"m":"Now, good angels, protect the King!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They wake","id":359},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Why, how now! Ho, awake! Why are you drawn?","Wherefore this ghastly looking?"],"id":360,"m":"What's going on? Hey, wake up! Why are your swords drawn? Why do you look so pale?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":361,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Whiles we stood here securing your repose,","Even now, we heard a hollow burst of bellowing","Like bulls, or rather lions; did't not wake you?","It struck mine ear most terribly."],"id":362,"m":"While we stood here guarding your rest, just now we heard a hollow burst of bellowing, like bulls, or rather lions. Didn't it wake you? It struck my ears terribly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I heard nothing."],"id":363,"m":"I heard nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["O! 'twas a din to fright a monster's ear,","To make an earthquake: sure it was the roar","Of a whole herd of lions."],"id":364,"m":"Oh, it was a noise to frighten a monster's ears, to cause an earthquake! Surely it was the roar of a whole pack of lions."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Heard you this, Gonzalo?"],"id":365,"m":"Did you hear this, Gonzalo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Upon mine honour, sir, I heard a humming,","And that a strange one too, which did awake me.","I shak'd you, sir, and cried; as mine eyes open'd,","I saw their weapons drawn:—there was a noise,","That's verily. 'Tis best we stand upon our guard,","Or that we quit this place: let's draw our weapons."],"id":366,"m":"On my honour, sir, I heard a humming, and a strange one too, which woke me. I shook you, sir, and shouted. As my eyes opened I saw their weapons drawn. There was a noise, that's true. We'd better stay on guard, or leave this place. Let's draw our weapons."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Lead off this ground: and let's make further search","For my poor son."],"id":367,"m":"Lead us away from here, and let's search further for my poor son."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Heavens keep him from these beasts!","For he is, sure, i' th' island."],"id":368,"m":"Heaven keep him safe from these beasts! For surely he's on the island."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Lead away."],"id":369,"m":"Lead the way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with the others.","id":370},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Prospero my lord shall know what I have done:","So, King, go safely on to seek thy son."],"id":371,"m":"My lord Prospero will know what I have done. So, King, go safely on and look for your son."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":372},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Another part of the island.","id":373},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CALIBAN, with a burden of wood. A noise of thunder heard","id":374},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["All the infections that the sun sucks up","From bogs, fens, flats, on Prosper fall, and make him","By inch-meal a disease! His spirits hear me,","And yet I needs must curse. But they'll nor pinch,","Fright me with urchin-shows, pitch me i' the mire,","Nor lead me, like a firebrand, in the dark","Out of my way, unless he bid 'em; but","For every trifle are they set upon me:","Sometime like apes that mow and chatter at me,","And after bite me; then like hedge-hogs which","Lie tumbling in my bare-foot way, and mount","Their pricks at my foot-fall; sometime am I","All wound with adders, who with cloven tongues","Do hiss me into madness.—"],"id":375,"m":"May all the diseases the sun sucks up from bogs, swamps and marshes fall on Prospero and make him sick inch by inch! His spirits can hear me, and yet I have to curse. But they won't pinch me, scare me with goblins, throw me in the mud, or lead me off my path in the dark like a will-o'-the-wisp, unless he tells them to. But they're set on me for every little thing. Sometimes they're like apes that pull faces and chatter at me and then bite me; then like hedgehogs that lie curled up in my barefoot path and stick their prickles up as I step. Sometimes I'm all wrapped in snakes, who hiss me mad with their forked tongues."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TRINCULO","id":376},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","cont":1,"lines":["Lo, now, lo!","Here comes a spirit of his, and to torment me","For bringing wood in slowly. I'll fall flat;","Perchance he will not mind me."],"id":377,"m":"Look, look! Here comes one of his spirits to torment me for bringing the wood in slowly. I'll lie flat. Maybe he won't notice me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Here's neither bush nor shrub to bear off any weather at all, and","another storm brewing; I hear it sing i' th' wind; yond same black","cloud, yond huge one, looks like a foul bombard that would shed his","liquor.","If it should thunder as it did before, I know not where to hide my","head: yond same cloud cannot choose but fall by pailfuls.—What have","we here? a man or a fish? dead or alive? A fish: he smells like a","fish: a very ancient and fish-like smell; a kind of not of the newest","Poor-John.","A strange fish! Were I in England now,—as once I was, and had but","this fish painted, not a holiday fool there but would give a piece of","silver: there would this monster make a man; any strange beast there","makes a man.","When they will not give a doit to relieve a lame beggar, they will lay","out ten to see a dead Indian. Legg'd like a man, and his fins like","arms! Warm, o' my troth! I do now let loose my opinion: hold it no","longer; this is no fish, but an islander, that hath lately suffered by","thunderbolt.","[Thunder] Alas, the storm is come again! My best way is to creep under","his gaberdine; there is no other shelter hereabout: misery acquaints a","man with strange bed-fellows. I will here shroud till the dregs of the","storm be past."],"id":378,"m":"There isn't a bush or shrub here to keep off any weather at all, and another storm is brewing: I can hear it singing in the wind. That black cloud over there, that huge one, looks like a filthy leather wine jug about to spill its drink. If it thunders like it did before, I don't know where to hide my head. That cloud is bound to pour down by the bucketful. What have we here? A man or a fish? Dead or alive? A fish: he smells like a fish, a very old, fishy smell, a sort of stale dried cod. A strange fish! If I were in England now, as I once was, and had a painting of this fish on a sign, every fool out on a holiday would pay a silver coin to see it. This monster would make a man's fortune there; any strange beast makes a man's fortune there. They won't give a penny to help a crippled beggar, but they'll pay ten to see a dead Indian. He has legs like a man, and his fins are like arms! Warm, I swear! Now I'll let my opinion go and hold it no longer: this is no fish but an islander who has recently been struck by a thunderbolt. Oh no, the storm has come again! My best plan is to crawl under his cloak; there's no other shelter around here. Misery introduces a man to strange bedfellows. I'll hide here till the last of the storm has passed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter STEPHANO singing; a bottle in his hand","id":379},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["I shall no more to sea, to sea,","Here shall I die a-shore:—","This is a very scurvy tune to sing at a man's funeral:","Well, here's my comfort.","[Drinks]","The master, the swabber, the boatswain, and I,","The gunner, and his mate,","Lov'd Mall, Meg, and Marian, and Margery,","But none of us car'd for Kate:","For she had a tongue with a tang,","Would cry to a sailor 'Go hang!'","She lov'd not the savour of tar nor of pitch,","Yet a tailor might scratch her wher-e'er she did itch.","Then to sea, boys, and let her go hang.","This is a scurvy tune too: but here's my comfort.","[Drinks]"],"id":380,"m":"I'll go to sea no more, no more; here I'll die ashore. This is a very lousy tune to sing at a man's funeral. Well, here's my comfort. The captain, the deck-hand, the boatswain and I, the gunner and his mate, loved Mall, Meg, Marian and Margery, but none of us cared for Kate. For she had a sharp tongue and would shout at a sailor, 'Go hang!' She didn't like the smell of tar or pitch, but a tailor could scratch her wherever she itched. So off to sea, boys, and let her go hang! This is a lousy tune too, but here's my comfort."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Do not torment me: O!"],"id":381,"m":"Don't torment me! Oh!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["What's the matter? Have we devils here? Do you put tricks upon us with","savages and men of Ind? Ha! I have not 'scaped drowning, to be afeard","now of your four legs; for it hath been said, As proper a man as ever","went on four legs cannot make him give ground: and it shall be said so","again, while Stephano breathes at 's nostrils."],"id":382,"m":"What's the matter? Do we have devils here? Are you playing tricks on us with savages and Indians? Ha! I haven't escaped drowning to be scared now of your four legs. As the saying goes, the finest man who ever walked on four legs can't make him give ground, and it will be said again as long as Stephano breathes through his nose."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["The spirit torments me: O!"],"id":383,"m":"The spirit is tormenting me! Oh!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["This is some monster of the isle with four legs, who hath got, as I","take it, an ague. Where the devil should he learn our language? I will","give him some relief, if it be but for that; if I can recover him and","keep him tame and get to Naples with him, he's a present for any","emperor that ever trod on neat's-leather."],"id":384,"m":"This is some monster of the island with four legs, who has caught a fever, I think. Where the devil did he learn our language? I'll give him some relief, if only for that. If I can cure him and tame him and get him to Naples, he'll be a present fit for any emperor who ever walked in leather shoes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Do not torment me, prithee; I'll bring my wood","home faster."],"id":385,"m":"Don't torment me, please! I'll bring my wood home faster."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["He's in his fit now and does not talk after the","wisest. He shall taste of my bottle: if he have never","drunk wine afore, it will go near to remove his fit. If","I can recover him, and keep him tame, I will not take","too much for him: he shall pay for him that hath him,","and that soundly."],"id":386,"m":"He's having his fit now and isn't talking very sensibly. He shall taste my bottle. If he's never drunk wine before, it will almost cure his fit. If I can cure him and tame him, no price will be too high for him. Whoever gets him will pay for him, and pay well."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Thou dost me yet but little hurt; thou wilt anon,","I know it by thy trembling: now Prosper works upon thee."],"id":387,"m":"You're not hurting me much yet, but you will soon, I can tell by your trembling. Now Prospero is working on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Come on your ways: open your mouth; here is","that which will give language to you, cat. Open your","mouth: this will shake your shaking, I can tell you, and","that soundly [gives CALIBAN a drink]: you cannot tell who's your","friend: open your chaps again."],"id":388,"m":"Come along now, open your mouth. Here's something that will make you talk, cat. Open your mouth. This will shake off your shaking, I can tell you, and properly. You can't tell who your friend is. Open your jaws again."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["I should know that voice: it should be—but he is","drowned; and these are devils. O! defend me."],"id":389,"m":"I should know that voice. It must be... but he's drowned, and these are devils. Oh, protect me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Four legs and two voices; a most delicate monster!","His forward voice now is to speak well of his","friend; his backward voice is to utter foul speeches, and","to detract. If all the wine in my bottle will recover","him, I will help his ague. Come. Amen! I will pour some","in thy other mouth."],"id":390,"m":"Four legs and two voices: a really fine monster! His front voice is for speaking well of his friend; his back voice is for saying foul things and slander. If all the wine in my bottle will cure him, I'll treat his fever. Come on. Amen! I'll pour some into your other mouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Stephano!"],"id":391,"m":"Stephano!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Doth thy other mouth call me? Mercy! mercy!","This is a devil, and no monster: I will leave him: I","have no long spoon."],"id":392,"m":"Is your other mouth calling me? Mercy! Mercy! This is a devil, not a monster. I'll leave him: I don't have a long spoon to sup with the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Stephano!—If thou beest Stephano, touch me, and","speak to me; for I am Trinculo:—be not afeared—thy good","friend Trinculo."],"id":393,"m":"Stephano! If you're Stephano, touch me and speak to me, because I'm Trinculo. Don't be scared: your good friend Trinculo."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["If thou beest Trinculo, come forth. I'll pull","thee by the lesser legs: if any be Trinculo's legs, these","are they. Thou art very Trinculo indeed! How cam'st thou","to be the siege of this moon-calf? Can he vent Trinculos?"],"id":394,"m":"If you're Trinculo, come out. I'll pull you out by the smaller legs. If any of these are Trinculo's legs, these are. You really are Trinculo! How did you come to be this moon-calf's droppings? Can he poop out Trinculos?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["I took him to be kill'd with a thunderstroke.","But art thou not drown'd, Stephano? I hope now thou are","not drown'd. Is the storm overblown? I hid me under the","dead moon-calf's gaberdine for fear of the storm. And","art thou living, Stephano? O Stephano, two Neapolitans","'scaped!"],"id":395,"m":"I thought he'd been killed by a thunderbolt. But aren't you drowned, Stephano? I hope now you're not drowned. Has the storm blown over? I hid under the dead moon-calf's cloak because I was afraid of the storm. And are you alive, Stephano? Oh, Stephano, two men of Naples escaped!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Prithee, do not turn me about: my stomach is not constant."],"id":396,"m":"Please don't spin me round. My stomach is unsteady."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["[Aside] These be fine things, an if they be not sprites.","That's a brave god, and bears celestial liquor;","I will kneel to him."],"id":397,"m":"These are fine things, if they aren't spirits. That's a splendid god, and he carries heavenly drink. I'll kneel to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["How didst thou 'scape? How cam'st thou hither? swear","by this bottle how thou cam'st hither—I escaped upon","a butt of sack, which the sailors heaved overboard, by","this bottle! which I made of the bark of a tree, with","mine own hands, since I was cast ashore."],"id":398,"m":"How did you escape? How did you get here? Swear on this bottle how you got here. I escaped on a barrel of sherry that the sailors threw overboard, I swear by this bottle, which I made out of tree bark with my own hands after I was washed ashore."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I'll swear upon that bottle to be thy true","subject, for the liquor is not earthly."],"id":399,"m":"I'll swear on that bottle to be your loyal subject, because that drink isn't from this earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Here: swear then how thou escapedst."],"id":400,"m":"Here, swear how you escaped, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Swum ashore, man, like a duck: I can swim like","a duck, I'll be sworn."],"id":401,"m":"Swam ashore, man, like a duck. I can swim like a duck, I swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["[Passing the bottle] Here, kiss the book [gives","TRINCULO a drink]. Though thou canst swim like a","duck, thou art made like a goose."],"id":402,"m":"Here, kiss the book. Even if you can swim like a duck, you're built like a goose."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["O Stephano! hast any more of this?"],"id":403,"m":"Oh, Stephano, have you got any more of this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["The whole butt, man: my cellar is in a rock by","the seaside, where my wine is hid. How now, moon-calf!","How does thine ague?"],"id":404,"m":"The whole barrel, man. My cellar is in a rock by the sea, where my wine is hidden. Well, moon-calf! How's your fever?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Hast thou not dropped from heaven?"],"id":405,"m":"Didn't you drop from heaven?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Out o' the moon, I do assure thee: I was the Man","in the Moon, when time was."],"id":406,"m":"Out of the moon, I assure you. I was the Man in the Moon once upon a time."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I have seen thee in her, and I do adore thee, my","mistress showed me thee, and thy dog and thy bush."],"id":407,"m":"I've seen you in her, and I worship you. My mistress showed you to me, and your dog and your bush."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Come, swear to that; kiss the book; I will","furnish it anon with new contents; swear."],"id":408,"m":"Come on, swear to that. Kiss the book. I'll fill it up again soon. Swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["By this good light, this is a very shallow","monster.—I afeard of him!—A very weak monster.","—The Man i' the Moon! A most poor credulous","monster!—Well drawn, monster, in good sooth!"],"id":409,"m":"By this good daylight, this is a very simple-minded monster. Me, afraid of him? A very feeble monster. The Man in the Moon! A very poor, gullible monster! That was a good swig, monster, honestly!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I'll show thee every fertile inch o' the island;","And I will kiss thy foot. I prithee, be my god."],"id":410,"m":"I'll show you every fertile inch of the island, and I'll kiss your foot. Please, be my god."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["By this light, a most perfidious and drunken","monster: when his god's asleep, he'll rob his bottle."],"id":411,"m":"By this light, a most treacherous and drunken monster! When his god's asleep, he'll steal his bottle."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I'll kiss thy foot: I'll swear myself thy subject."],"id":412,"m":"I'll kiss your foot. I'll swear to be your subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Come on, then; down, and swear."],"id":413,"m":"Come on, then. Down, and swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["I shall laugh myself to death at this puppy-headed","monster. A most scurvy monster! I could find in","my heart to beat him,—"],"id":414,"m":"I'll laugh myself to death at this puppy-faced monster. A very lousy monster! I could find it in my heart to beat him..."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Come, kiss."],"id":415,"m":"Come on, kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["But that the poor monster's in drink: an","abominable monster!"],"id":416,"m":"...except that the poor monster is drunk. A disgusting monster!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I'll show thee the best springs; I'll pluck thee","berries;","I'll fish for thee, and get thee wood enough.","A plague upon the tyrant that I serve!","I'll bear him no more sticks, but follow thee,","Thou wondrous man."],"id":417,"m":"I'll show you the best springs. I'll pick berries for you. I'll fish for you and get you plenty of wood. A plague on the tyrant I serve! I won't carry him any more sticks, but follow you, you wonderful man."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["A most ridiculous monster, to make a wonder of","a poor drunkard!"],"id":418,"m":"A ridiculous monster, to make a marvel out of a poor drunk!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I prithee, let me bring thee where crabs grow;","And I with my long nails will dig thee pig-nuts;","Show thee a jay's nest, and instruct thee how","To snare the nimble marmozet; I'll bring thee","To clust'ring filberts, and sometimes I'll get thee","Young scamels from the rock. Wilt thou go with me?"],"id":419,"m":"Please, let me take you to where crab apples grow, and I'll dig up earth-nuts for you with my long nails. I'll show you a jay's nest and teach you how to trap the quick little monkey. I'll take you to clusters of hazelnuts, and sometimes I'll get you young seabirds from the rocks. Will you come with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["I prithee now, lead the way without any more","talking—Trinculo, the king and all our company else","being drowned, we will inherit here.—Here, bear my","bottle.—Fellow Trinculo, we'll fill him by and by","again."],"id":420,"m":"Now please lead the way without any more talking. Trinculo, since the King and all the rest of our company are drowned, we'll take over here. Here, carry my bottle. Fellow Trinculo, we'll fill it up again soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Farewell, master; farewell, farewell! [Sings drunkenly]"],"id":421,"m":"Goodbye, master! Goodbye, goodbye!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["A howling monster, a drunken monster."],"id":422,"m":"A howling monster, a drunken monster!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["No more dams I'll make for fish;","Nor fetch in firing","At requiring,","Nor scrape trenchering, nor wash dish;","'Ban 'Ban, Ca—Caliban,","Has a new master—Get a new man.","Freedom, high-day! high-day, freedom! freedom,","high-day, freedom!"],"id":423,"m":"I won't make dams for fish any more, or fetch firewood when I'm told, or scrape plates or wash dishes. 'Ban, 'Ban, Ca-Caliban has a new master: get a new servant! Freedom, hooray! Hooray, freedom! Freedom, hooray, freedom!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["O brave monster! lead the way."],"id":424,"m":"Oh, splendid monster! Lead the way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":425},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Prospero's cell.","id":426},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FERDINAND, bearing a log.","id":427},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["There be some sports are painful, and their labour","Delight in them sets off: some kinds of baseness","Are nobly undergone, and most poor matters","Point to rich ends. This my mean task","Would be as heavy to me as odious; but","The mistress which I serve quickens what's dead,","And makes my labours pleasures: O! she is","Ten times more gentle than her father's crabbed,","And he's compos'd of harshness. I must remove","Some thousands of these logs, and pile them up,","Upon a sore injunction: my sweet mistress","Weeps when she sees me work, and says such baseness","Had never like executor. I forget:","But these sweet thoughts do even refresh my labours,","Most busy, least when I do it."],"id":428,"m":"Some pastimes are hard work, but the pleasure in them makes up for the effort. Some kinds of lowly work can be done nobly, and many humble tasks lead to rich rewards. This menial job of mine would be as heavy as it is hateful to me, but the lady I serve brings dead things to life and turns my labours into pleasures. Oh, she is ten times kinder than her father is bad-tempered, and he is made of harshness. I have to move some thousands of these logs and pile them up, under a strict order. My sweet lady weeps when she sees me work and says such lowly work never had such a noble worker. I'm forgetting my job, but these sweet thoughts refresh my labours, and I'm busiest when I'm least working."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MIRANDA: and PROSPERO behind.","id":429},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Alas! now pray you,","Work not so hard: I would the lightning had","Burnt up those logs that you are enjoin'd to pile!","Pray, set it down and rest you: when this burns,","'Twill weep for having wearied you. My father","Is hard at study; pray, now, rest yourself:","He's safe for these three hours."],"id":430,"m":"Oh, please, don't work so hard! I wish lightning had burnt up those logs you have been ordered to pile! Please put that down and rest. When this burns it will weep sap for having tired you. My father is busy studying. Please rest now: he's safe out of the way for the next three hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["O most dear mistress,","The sun will set, before I shall discharge","What I must strive to do."],"id":431,"m":"Oh, dearest lady, the sun will set before I finish what I have to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["If you'll sit down,","I'll bear your logs the while. Pray give me that;","I'll carry it to the pile."],"id":432,"m":"If you'll sit down, I'll carry your logs in the meantime. Please give me that; I'll take it to the pile."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["No, precious creature:","I had rather crack my sinews, break my back,","Than you should such dishonour undergo,","While I sit lazy by."],"id":433,"m":"No, precious creature. I would rather tear my muscles and break my back than have you suffer such dishonour while I sit by lazily."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["It would become me","As well as it does you: and I should do it","With much more ease; for my good will is to it,","And yours it is against."],"id":434,"m":"It would suit me as well as it suits you, and I would do it much more easily, because my heart is in it and yours is against it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] Poor worm! thou art infected:","This visitation shows it."],"id":435,"m":"Poor creature! You've caught the infection. This visit proves it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["You look wearily."],"id":436,"m":"You look tired."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["No, noble mistress; 'tis fresh morning with me","When you are by at night. I do beseech you—","Chiefly that I might set it in my prayers—","What is your name?"],"id":437,"m":"No, noble lady. It's fresh morning for me when you're near, even at night. I beg you, mainly so that I can put it in my prayers, what is your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Miranda—O my father!","I have broke your hest to say so."],"id":438,"m":"Miranda. Oh, my father! I've broken your order by telling him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Admir'd Miranda!","Indeed, the top of admiration; worth","What's dearest to the world! Full many a lady","I have ey'd with best regard, and many a time","The harmony of their tongues hath into bondage","Brought my too diligent ear: for several virtues","Have I lik'd several women; never any","With so full soul but some defect in her","Did quarrel with the noblest grace she ow'd,","And put it to the foil: but you, O you!","So perfect and so peerless, are created","Of every creature's best."],"id":439,"m":"Admired Miranda! Truly the height of admiration, worth more than the most precious things in the world! I have looked on many ladies with the greatest admiration, and many times the music of their voices has captured my too eager ear. I have liked different women for different virtues, but never one so wholeheartedly that some flaw in her didn't fight with her finest quality and spoil it. But you, oh you, so perfect and without equal, are made of the best of every creature."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["I do not know","One of my sex; no woman's face remember,","Save, from my glass, mine own; nor have I seen","More that I may call men than you, good friend,","And my dear father: how features are abroad,","I am skill-less of; but, by my modesty,—","The jewel in my dower,—I would not wish","Any companion in the world but you;","Nor can imagination form a shape,","Besides yourself, to like of. But I prattle","Something too wildly, and my father's precepts","I therein do forget."],"id":440,"m":"I don't know a single woman. I don't remember any woman's face except my own, from my mirror. And I haven't seen anyone I could call a man except you, good friend, and my dear father. What people look like elsewhere I have no idea. But by my modesty, the jewel of my dowry, I wouldn't wish for any companion in the world but you, and my imagination can't create any shape except yours to love. But I'm chattering too freely, and forgetting my father's instructions."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["I am, in my condition,","A prince, Miranda; I do think, a king;—","I would not so!—and would no more endure","This wooden slavery than to suffer","The flesh-fly blow my mouth.—Hear my soul speak:—","The very instant that I saw you, did","My heart fly to your service; there resides,","To make me slave to it; and for your sake","Am I this patient log-man."],"id":441,"m":"By rank, Miranda, I am a prince, and I think a king (I wish it weren't so!). I would no more put up with this slavery of carrying wood than let a blowfly lay eggs in my mouth. Hear my soul speak. The very moment I saw you, my heart flew to serve you. It lives there, making me its slave, and for your sake I am this patient log-carrier."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Do you love me?"],"id":442,"m":"Do you love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["O heaven! O earth! bear witness to this sound,","And crown what I profess with kind event,","If I speak true: if hollowly, invert","What best is boded me to mischief! I,","Beyond all limit of what else i' the world,","Do love, prize, honour you."],"id":443,"m":"Oh heaven, oh earth, bear witness to what I say! Crown my words with a happy outcome if I speak truly; if falsely, turn the best that's in store for me to disaster! I love, value and honour you beyond everything else in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["I am a fool","To weep at what I am glad of."],"id":444,"m":"I'm a fool to cry at something I'm glad about."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] Fair encounter","Of two most rare affections! Heavens rain grace","On that which breeds between them!"],"id":445,"m":"A lovely meeting of two rare loves! May heaven rain blessings on what grows between them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Wherefore weep you?"],"id":446,"m":"Why are you crying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["At mine unworthiness, that dare not offer","What I desire to give; and much less take","What I shall die to want. But this is trifling;","And all the more it seeks to hide itself,","The bigger bulk it shows. Hence, bashful cunning!","And prompt me, plain and holy innocence!","I am your wife, if you will marry me;","If not, I'll die your maid: to be your fellow","You may deny me; but I'll be your servant,","Whether you will or no."],"id":447,"m":"Because I'm unworthy, and don't dare offer what I want to give, much less take what I'll die without. But this is nonsense: the more my love tries to hide itself, the bigger it shows. Away, shy cleverness! Guide me, plain and holy innocence! I am your wife, if you'll marry me. If not, I'll die a virgin devoted to you. You may refuse to make me your partner, but I'll be your servant, whether you want me or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["My mistress, dearest;","And I thus humble ever."],"id":448,"m":"My mistress, dearest, and I will always be as humble as this before you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["My husband, then?"],"id":449,"m":"My husband, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Ay, with a heart as willing","As bondage e'er of freedom: here's my hand."],"id":450,"m":"Yes, with a heart as willing as a slave's is for freedom. Here's my hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["And mine, with my heart in't: and now farewell","Till half an hour hence."],"id":451,"m":"And here's mine, with my heart in it. And now goodbye until half an hour from now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["A thousand thousand!"],"id":452,"m":"A thousand thousand goodbyes!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt FERDINAND and MIRANDA severally.","id":453},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["So glad of this as they, I cannot be,","Who are surpris'd withal; but my rejoicing","At nothing can be more. I'll to my book;","For yet, ere supper time, must I perform","Much business appertaining."],"id":454,"m":"I can't be as glad about this as they are, since it has taken them by surprise, but nothing could make me happier. I'll go to my book, because before supper I still have a lot of work to do for this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":455},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Another part of the island.","id":456},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CALIBAN, with a bottle, STEPHANO, and TRINCULO.","id":457},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Tell not me:—when the butt is out we will drink","water; not a drop before: therefore bear up, and board","'em.—Servant-monster, drink to me."],"id":458,"m":"Don't tell me. When the barrel's empty we'll drink water, not a drop before. So hold steady and attack. Servant-monster, drink to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Servant-monster! The folly of this island! They","say there's but five upon this isle; we are three of","them; if th' other two be brained like us, the state","totters."],"id":459,"m":"Servant-monster! This island is crazy! They say there are only five people on this island. We are three of them. If the other two have brains like ours, the state is tottering."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Drink, servant-monster, when I bid thee: thy","eyes are almost set in thy head."],"id":460,"m":"Drink, servant-monster, when I tell you to. Your eyes are almost fixed in your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Where should they be set else? He were a brave","monster indeed, if they were set in his tail."],"id":461,"m":"Where else would they be fixed? He'd be a fine monster indeed if they were set in his tail."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["My man-monster hath drown'd his tongue in","sack: for my part, the sea cannot drown me; I swam, ere","I could recover the shore, five-and-thirty leagues, off","and on, by this light. Thou shalt be my lieutenant,","monster, or my standard."],"id":462,"m":"My man-monster has drowned his tongue in sherry. As for me, the sea can't drown me. I swam thirty-five leagues, on and off, before I could reach the shore, I swear by this light. You'll be my lieutenant, monster, or my flag-bearer."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Your lieutenant, if you list; he's no standard."],"id":463,"m":"Your lieutenant, if you like. He can't stand up straight like a flag."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["We'll not run, Monsieur monster."],"id":464,"m":"We won't run away, Monsieur Monster."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Nor go neither: but you'll lie like dogs, and","yet say nothing neither."],"id":465,"m":"Or walk either. You'll lie there like dogs and say nothing, too."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Moon-calf, speak once in thy life, if thou beest","a good moon-calf."],"id":466,"m":"Moon-calf, speak once in your life, if you're a good moon-calf."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["How does thy honour? Let me lick thy shoe.","I'll not serve him: he is not valiant."],"id":467,"m":"How is your honour? Let me lick your shoe. I won't serve him: he's not brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Thou liest, most ignorant monster: I am in case","to justle a constable. Why, thou deboshed fish thou,","was there ever man a coward that hath drunk so much sack","as I to-day? Wilt thou tell a monstrous lie, being but","half fish and half a monster?"],"id":468,"m":"You're lying, you ignorant monster. I'm ready to take on a policeman. Why, you debauched fish, was there ever a man who was a coward after drinking as much sherry as I have today? Will you tell a monstrous lie, when you're only half a fish and half a monster?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Lo, how he mocks me! wilt thou let him, my lord?"],"id":469,"m":"Look how he mocks me! Will you let him, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["'Lord' quoth he!—That a monster should be such","a natural!"],"id":470,"m":"'Lord', he says! That a monster should be such an idiot!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Lo, lo again! bite him to death, I prithee."],"id":471,"m":"Look, look, again! Bite him to death, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Trinculo, keep a good tongue in your head: if","you prove a mutineer, the next tree! The poor monster's","my subject, and he shall not suffer indignity."],"id":472,"m":"Trinculo, keep a civil tongue in your head. If you turn rebel, you'll hang from the next tree! The poor monster is my subject, and he won't be insulted."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I thank my noble lord. Wilt thou be pleas'd to","hearken once again to the suit I made to thee?"],"id":473,"m":"I thank my noble lord. Will you be pleased to listen again to the request I made to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Marry will I; kneel, and repeat it: I will stand,","and so shall Trinculo."],"id":474,"m":"Indeed I will. Kneel and repeat it. I'll stand, and so will Trinculo."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ARIEL, invisible","id":475},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["As I told thee before, I am subject to a tyrant,","sorcerer, that by his cunning hath cheated me of the","island."],"id":476,"m":"As I told you before, I am the subject of a tyrant, a sorcerer, who cheated me out of the island by his cunning."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Thou liest."],"id":477,"m":"You're lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Thou liest, thou jesting monkey, thou;","I would my valiant master would destroy thee;","I do not lie."],"id":478,"m":"You're lying, you jesting monkey, you! I wish my brave master would destroy you. I'm not lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Trinculo, if you trouble him any more in his tale,","by this hand, I will supplant some of your teeth."],"id":479,"m":"Trinculo, if you interrupt his story any more, by this hand, I'll knock out some of your teeth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Why, I said nothing."],"id":480,"m":"Why, I didn't say anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Mum, then, and no more.—[To CALIBAN] Proceed."],"id":481,"m":"Quiet, then, and no more. Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I say, by sorcery he got this isle;","From me he got it: if thy greatness will ,","Revenge it on him,—for I know, thou dar'st;","But this thing dare not,—"],"id":482,"m":"I say he got this island by sorcery. He took it from me. If your greatness will take revenge on him for it, since I know you dare, but this thing doesn't dare..."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["That's most certain."],"id":483,"m":"That's quite certain."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Thou shalt be lord of it and I'll serve thee."],"id":484,"m":"You'll be lord of it, and I'll serve you."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["How now shall this be compassed? Canst thou","bring me to the party?"],"id":485,"m":"Now how can this be done? Can you take me to the man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Yea, yea, my lord: I'll yield him thee asleep,","Where thou may'st knock a nail into his head."],"id":486,"m":"Yes, yes, my lord. I'll hand him over to you asleep, where you can knock a nail into his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Thou liest: thou canst not."],"id":487,"m":"You're lying: you can't."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["What a pied ninny's this! Thou scurvy patch!—","I do beseech thy greatness, give him blows,","And take his bottle from him: when that's gone","He shall drink nought but brine; for I'll not show him","Where the quick freshes are."],"id":488,"m":"What a motley fool this is! You lousy clown! I beg your greatness, beat him and take his bottle away. When that's gone he'll drink nothing but salt water, because I won't show him where the fresh springs are."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Trinculo, run into no further danger: interrupt the","monster one word further and, by this hand, I'll turn","my mercy out o' doors, and make a stock-fish of thee."],"id":489,"m":"Trinculo, don't put yourself in any more danger. Interrupt the monster one more word and, by this hand, I'll throw my mercy out of doors and beat you like dried cod."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Why, what did I? I did nothing. I'll go farther off."],"id":490,"m":"Why, what did I do? I didn't do anything. I'll move further away."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Didst thou not say he lied?"],"id":491,"m":"Didn't you say he was lying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Thou liest."],"id":492,"m":"You're lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Do I so? Take thou that. [Strikes TRINCULO.] As you","like this, give me the lie another time."],"id":493,"m":"Am I? Take that! If you like that, call me a liar again."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["I did not give the lie:—out o' your wits and","hearing too?—A pox o' your bottle! this can sack and","drinking do.—A murrain on your monster, and the devil","take your fingers!"],"id":494,"m":"I didn't call you a liar. Have you lost your mind and your hearing too? A plague on your bottle! This is what sherry and drinking do. A cattle disease on your monster, and the devil take your fingers!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Ha, ha, ha!"],"id":495,"m":"Ha, ha, ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Now, forward with your tale.—Prithee stand","further off."],"id":496,"m":"Now, get on with your story. Please stand further off."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Beat him enough: after a little time, I'll beat","him too."],"id":497,"m":"Beat him plenty. After a while, I'll beat him too."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Stand farther.—Come, proceed."],"id":498,"m":"Stand further off. Come on, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Why, as I told thee, 'tis a custom with him","I' th' afternoon to sleep: there thou may'st brain him,","Having first seiz'd his books; or with a log","Batter his skull, or paunch him with a stake,","Or cut his wezand with thy knife. Remember","First to possess his books; for without them","He's but a sot, as I am, nor hath not","One spirit to command: they all do hate him","As rootedly as I. Burn but his books;","He has brave utensils,—for so he calls them,—","Which, when he has a house, he'll deck withal:","And that most deeply to consider is","The beauty of his daughter; he himself","Calls her a nonpareil: I never saw a woman","But only Sycorax my dam and she;","But she as far surpasseth Sycorax","As great'st does least."],"id":499,"m":"Well, as I told you, it's his habit to sleep in the afternoon. There you can smash his brains in, after first seizing his books. Or batter his skull with a log, or stab him in the belly with a stake, or cut his windpipe with your knife. Remember to take his books first, because without them he's just a fool like me and hasn't a single spirit to command. They all hate him as deeply as I do. Just burn his books. He has fine furnishings, as he calls them, to decorate a house with when he has one. And what you should think about most is his daughter's beauty. He himself calls her a wonder without equal. I've never seen a woman except my mother Sycorax and her, but she is as far beyond Sycorax as the greatest is beyond the least."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Is it so brave a lass?"],"id":500,"m":"Is she such a fine girl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Ay, lord: she will become thy bed, I warrant,","And bring thee forth brave brood."],"id":501,"m":"Yes, lord. She'll suit your bed, I promise, and give you fine children."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Monster, I will kill this man; his daughter and I","will be king and queen,—save our graces!—and Trinculo","and thyself shall be viceroys. Dost thou like the plot,","Trinculo?"],"id":502,"m":"Monster, I will kill this man. His daughter and I will be king and queen (God save our majesties!), and Trinculo and you will be my deputies. Do you like the plan, Trinculo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Excellent."],"id":503,"m":"Excellent."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Give me thy hand: I am sorry I beat thee; but","while thou livest, keep a good tongue in thy head."],"id":504,"m":"Give me your hand. I'm sorry I hit you, but as long as you live, keep a civil tongue in your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Within this half hour will he be asleep;","Wilt thou destroy him then?"],"id":505,"m":"Within this half hour he'll be asleep. Will you kill him then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Ay, on mine honour."],"id":506,"m":"Yes, on my honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["This will I tell my master."],"id":507,"m":"I'll tell my master this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Thou mak'st me merry: I am full of pleasure.","Let us be jocund: will you troll the catch","You taught me but while-ere?"],"id":508,"m":"You make me merry. I'm full of joy. Let's be cheerful. Will you sing the round you taught me just now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["At thy request, monster, I will do reason, any","reason. Come on, Trinculo, let us sing.","[Sings]","Flout 'em and scout 'em; and scout 'em and flout 'em:","Thought is free."],"id":509,"m":"Since you ask, monster, I'll do anything reasonable, anything. Come on, Trinculo, let's sing. Mock them and jeer them, jeer them and mock them: thoughts are free."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["That's not the tune."],"id":510,"m":"That's not the tune."},{"t":"sd","text":"ARIEL plays the tune on a Tabor and Pipe.","id":511},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["What is this same?"],"id":512,"m":"What's this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["This is the tune of our catch, played by the","picture of Nobody."],"id":513,"m":"This is the tune of our song, played by the picture of Nobody."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["If thou beest a man, show thyself in thy","likeness: if thou beest a devil, take't as thou list."],"id":514,"m":"If you're a man, show yourself in your true shape. If you're a devil, do as you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["O, forgive me my sins!"],"id":515,"m":"Oh, forgive me my sins!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["He that dies pays all debts: I defy thee.—Mercy","upon us!"],"id":516,"m":"A man who dies pays all his debts. I defy you. Mercy on us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Art thou afeard?"],"id":517,"m":"Are you afraid?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["No, monster, not I."],"id":518,"m":"No, monster, not me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Be not afeard: the isle is full of noises,","Sounds, and sweet airs, that give delight, and hurt not.","Sometimes a thousand twangling instruments","Will hum about mine ears; and sometimes voices,","That, if I then had wak'd after long sleep,","Will make me sleep again: and then, in dreaming,","The clouds methought would open and show riches","Ready to drop upon me; that, when I wak'd,","I cried to dream again."],"id":519,"m":"Don't be afraid. The island is full of noises, sounds and sweet tunes that give delight and do no harm. Sometimes a thousand twanging instruments hum around my ears, and sometimes voices that, if I had just woken from a long sleep, would make me sleep again. And then, while dreaming, I thought the clouds would open and show riches ready to drop on me, so that when I woke I cried to dream again."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["This will prove a brave kingdom to me, where I","shall have my music for nothing."],"id":520,"m":"This will be a fine kingdom for me, where I'll have my music for free."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["When Prospero is destroyed."],"id":521,"m":"When Prospero is destroyed."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["That shall be by and by: I remember the story."],"id":522,"m":"That will be soon. I remember the plan."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["The sound is going away: let's follow it, and","after do our work."],"id":523,"m":"The sound is moving away. Let's follow it, and do our job afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Lead, monster: we'll follow.—I would I could see","this taborer! he lays it on. Wilt come?"],"id":524,"m":"Lead on, monster. We'll follow. I wish I could see this drummer! He's really going at it. Are you coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["I'll follow, Stephano."],"id":525,"m":"I'll follow, Stephano."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":526},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Another part of the island.","id":527},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ALONSO, SEBASTIAN, ANTONIO, GONZALO, ADRIAN, FRANCISCO, and OTHERS.","id":528},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["By'r lakin, I can go no further, sir;","My old bones ache: here's a maze trod, indeed,","Through forth-rights and meanders! By your patience,","I needs must rest me."],"id":529,"m":"By Our Lady, I can't go any further, sir. My old bones ache. We've walked through a real maze of straight paths and winding ones! With your permission, I must rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Old lord, I cannot blame thee,","Who am myself attach'd with weariness","To th' dulling of my spirits: sit down, and rest.","Even here I will put off my hope, and keep it","No longer for my flatterer: he is drown'd","Whom thus we stray to find; and the sea mocks","Our frustrate search on land. Well, let him go."],"id":530,"m":"Old lord, I can't blame you, since I'm worn out myself, to the point of numbness. Sit down and rest. Right here I'll give up my hope and keep it no longer to flatter me. The one we wander about looking for is drowned, and the sea mocks our useless search on land. Well, let him go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["[Aside to SEBASTIAN] I am right glad that he's","so out of hope.","Do not, for one repulse, forgo the purpose","That you resolv'd to effect."],"id":531,"m":"I'm very glad he's lost hope. Don't give up the plan you decided to carry out just because of one setback."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["[Aside to ANTONIO] The next advantage","Will we take throughly."],"id":532,"m":"We'll take the next opportunity thoroughly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["[Aside to SEBASTIAN] Let it be to-night;","For, now they are oppress'd with travel, they","Will not, nor cannot, use such vigilance","As when they are fresh."],"id":533,"m":"Let it be tonight. Now that they're worn out with walking, they won't and can't be as watchful as when they're fresh."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["[Aside to ANTONIO] I say, to-night: no more."],"id":534,"m":"Tonight, I say. No more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Solemn and strange music: and PROSPERO above, invisible. Enter several strange Shapes, bringing in a banquet: they dance about it with gentle actions of salutation; and inviting the KING, &c., to eat, they depart.","id":535},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["What harmony is this? my good friends, hark!"],"id":536,"m":"What music is this? My good friends, listen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Marvellous sweet music!"],"id":537,"m":"Wonderfully sweet music!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Give us kind keepers, heavens! What were these?"],"id":538,"m":"Heaven, give us kind guardian angels! What were those?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A living drollery. Now I will believe","That there are unicorns; that in Arabia","There is one tree, the phoenix' throne; one phoenix","At this hour reigning there."],"id":539,"m":"A live puppet show. Now I'll believe that there are unicorns, and that in Arabia there is one tree that's the phoenix's throne, with one phoenix reigning there right now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I'll believe both;","And what does else want credit, come to me,","And I'll be sworn 'tis true: travellers ne'er did lie,","Though fools at home condemn them."],"id":540,"m":"I'll believe both. And anything else that's hard to believe, bring it to me and I'll swear it's true. Travellers never lied, though fools at home condemn them."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["If in Naples","I should report this now, would they believe me?","If I should say, I saw such islanders,—","For, certes, these are people of the island,—","Who, though, they are of monstrous shape, yet, note,","Their manners are more gentle-kind than of","Our human generation you shall find","Many, nay, almost any."],"id":541,"m":"If I reported this in Naples now, would they believe me? If I said I'd seen islanders like these (because surely these are people of the island), who, though they are monstrous in shape, have manners, notice, more gentle and kind than you'll find in many of our human race, in fact almost any."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] Honest lord,","Thou hast said well; for some of you there present","Are worse than devils."],"id":542,"m":"Honest lord, you've said well, because some of you standing there are worse than devils."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I cannot too much muse","Such shapes, such gesture, and such sound, expressing,—","Although they want the use of tongue,—a kind","Of excellent dumb discourse."],"id":543,"m":"I can't marvel enough at such shapes, such gestures and such sounds, expressing, even though they can't speak, a kind of excellent silent conversation."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] Praise in departing."],"id":544,"m":"Save your praise until the end."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCISCO","lines":["They vanish'd strangely."],"id":545,"m":"They vanished strangely."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["No matter, since","They have left their viands behind; for we have stomachs.—","Will't please you taste of what is here?"],"id":546,"m":"No matter, since they've left their food behind, and we're hungry. Would you like to taste some of this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Not I."],"id":547,"m":"Not I."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Faith, sir, you need not fear. When we were boys,","Who would believe that there were mountaineers","Dewlapp'd like bulls, whose throats had hanging at them","Wallets of flesh? or that there were such men","Whose heads stood in their breasts? which now we find","Each putter-out of five for one will bring us","Good warrant of."],"id":548,"m":"Honestly, sir, you needn't be afraid. When we were boys, who would have believed that there were mountain men with folds of skin hanging from their throats like bulls, like bags of flesh? Or that there were men whose heads were in their chests? Yet now every traveller who bets five to one on coming home safely brings us proof of these things."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I will stand to, and feed,","Although my last; no matter, since I feel","The best is past.—Brother, my lord the duke,","Stand to and do as we."],"id":549,"m":"I will step up and eat, even if it's my last meal. It doesn't matter, since I feel the best of my life is over. Brother, my lord the Duke, step up and do as we do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Thunder and lightning. Enter ARIEL, like a harpy; claps his wings upon the table; and, with a quaint device, the banquet vanishes","id":550},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["You are three men of sin, whom Destiny,","That hath to instrument this lower world","And what is in't,—the never-surfeited sea","Hath caused to belch up you; and on this island","Where man doth not inhabit; you 'mongst men","Being most unfit to live. I have made you mad:"],"id":551,"m":"You are three sinful men. Destiny, which uses this lower world and everything in it as its instrument, has made the never-satisfied sea belch you up, and onto this island where no man lives, since you are the most unfit of all men to live. I have made you mad."},{"t":"sd","text":"Seeing ALONSO, SEBASTIAN, &c., draw their swords","id":552},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","cont":1,"lines":["And even with such-like valour men hang and drown","Their proper selves. You fools! I and my fellows","Are ministers of fate: the elements","Of whom your swords are temper'd may as well","Wound the loud winds, or with bemock'd-at stabs","Kill the still-closing waters, as diminish","One dowle that's in my plume; my fellow-ministers","Are like invulnerable. If you could hurt,","Your swords are now too massy for your strengths,","And will not be uplifted. But, remember—","For that's my business to you,—that you three","From Milan did supplant good Prospero;","Expos'd unto the sea, which hath requit it,","Him, and his innocent child: for which foul deed","The powers, delaying, not forgetting, have","Incens'd the seas and shores, yea, all the creatures,","Against your peace. Thee of thy son, Alonso,","They have bereft; and do pronounce, by me","Lingering perdition,—worse than any death","Can be at once,—shall step by step attend","You and your ways; whose wraths to guard you from—","Which here, in this most desolate isle, else falls","Upon your heads,—is nothing but heart-sorrow,","And a clear life ensuing."],"id":553,"m":"And it is with this kind of courage that men hang and drown themselves. You fools! My fellows and I are agents of fate. The metal your swords are made of could as easily wound the loud winds, or kill the waters that always close up again with stabs they laugh at, as cut one fibre of my feathers. My fellow agents are just as invulnerable. Even if you could hurt us, your swords are now too heavy for your strength, and you can't lift them. But remember, because that's my business with you, that you three overthrew good Prospero in Milan. You put him and his innocent child out on the sea, which has now paid you back. For that foul deed the heavenly powers, which delay but do not forget, have stirred up the seas and shores, and every creature, against your peace. Alonso, they have taken your son from you, and through me they declare that a slow ruin, worse than any sudden death, will follow you step by step wherever you go. The only protection from their anger, which will otherwise fall on your heads on this desolate island, is true repentance and a pure life from now on."},{"t":"sd","text":"He vanishes in thunder: then, to soft music, enter the Shapes again, and dance, with mocks and mows, and carry out the table","id":554},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] Bravely the figure of this harpy hast thou","Perform'd, my Ariel; a grace it had, devouring;","Of my instruction hast thou nothing bated","In what thou hadst to say: so, with good life","And observation strange, my meaner ministers","Their several kinds have done. My high charms work,","And these mine enemies are all knit up","In their distractions; they now are in my power;","And in these fits I leave them, while I visit","Young Ferdinand,—whom they suppose is drown'd,—","And his and mine lov'd darling."],"id":555,"m":"You played the part of this harpy splendidly, my Ariel. You had a devouring grace. You left out nothing of what I told you to say. And my lesser spirits have played their different parts with lifelike skill and wonderful care. My powerful spells are working, and these enemies of mine are all tied up in their madness. Now they're in my power. I'll leave them in these fits while I visit young Ferdinand, whom they think is drowned, and his and my beloved darling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit above","id":556},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I' the name of something holy, sir, why stand you","In this strange stare?"],"id":557,"m":"In the name of all that's holy, sir, why are you standing there staring like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["O, it is monstrous! monstrous!","Methought the billows spoke, and told me of it;","The winds did sing it to me; and the thunder,","That deep and dreadful organ-pipe, pronounc'd","The name of Prosper: it did bass my trespass.","Therefore my son i' th' ooze is bedded; and","I'll seek him deeper than e'er plummet sounded,","And with him there lie mudded."],"id":558,"m":"Oh, it's monstrous, monstrous! I thought the waves spoke and told me of it. The winds sang it to me, and the thunder, that deep and dreadful organ pipe, pronounced the name of Prospero. It sang out my crime in a bass voice. That's why my son lies buried in the mud of the sea bed, and I'll look for him deeper than any plumb line ever sank, and lie there with him in the mud."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":559},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["But one fiend at a time,","I'll fight their legions o'er."],"id":560,"m":"If they come one fiend at a time, I'll fight their whole armies."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I'll be thy second."],"id":561,"m":"I'll be your second."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt SEBASTIAN and ANTONIO","id":562},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["All three of them are desperate: their great guilt,","Like poison given to work a great time after,","Now 'gins to bite the spirits. I do beseech you","That are of suppler joints, follow them swiftly","And hinder them from what this ecstasy","May now provoke them to."],"id":563,"m":"All three of them are desperate. Their great guilt, like a poison designed to work long afterwards, is starting to bite their souls. I beg you, who have more flexible joints, follow them quickly and stop them doing what this madness may drive them to."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIAN","lines":["Follow, I pray you."],"id":564,"m":"Follow, please."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":565},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Prospero's cell.","id":566},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PROSPERO, FERDINAND, and MIRANDA","id":567},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["If I have too austerely punish'd you,","Your compensation makes amends: for","Have given you here a third of mine own life,","Or that for which I live; who once again","I tender to thy hand: all thy vexations","Were but my trials of thy love, and thou","Hast strangely stood the test: here, afore Heaven,","I ratify this my rich gift. O Ferdinand!","Do not smile at me that I boast her off,","For thou shalt find she will outstrip all praise,","And make it halt behind her."],"id":568,"m":"If I have punished you too harshly, your reward makes up for it, because I have given you here a third of my own life, or rather the thing I live for. I hand her over to you once again. All your hardships were only my tests of your love, and you have passed them wonderfully. Here, before heaven, I confirm this rich gift of mine. Oh Ferdinand, don't smile at me for boasting about her, because you'll find she outruns all praise and leaves it limping behind her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["I do believe it","Against an oracle."],"id":569,"m":"I would believe that even if an oracle said otherwise."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Then, as my gift and thine own acquisition","Worthily purchas'd, take my daughter: but","If thou dost break her virgin knot before","All sanctimonious ceremonies may","With full and holy rite be minister'd,","No sweet aspersion shall the heavens let fall","To make this contract grow; but barren hate,","Sour-ey'd disdain, and discord, shall bestrew","The union of your bed with weeds so loathly","That you shall hate it both: therefore take heed,","As Hymen's lamps shall light you."],"id":570,"m":"Then take my daughter, as my gift and as your own prize, which you have worthily won. But if you break her virginity before all the holy ceremonies have been performed with full and sacred rites, the heavens will let no sweet blessing fall to make this marriage grow. Instead barren hatred, sour-eyed contempt and quarrelling will scatter such hateful weeds over your marriage bed that you will both loathe it. So be careful, as you hope the god of marriage will light your way."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["As I hope","For quiet days, fair issue, and long life,","With such love as 'tis now, the murkiest den,","The most opportune place, the strong'st suggestion","Our worser genius can, shall never melt","Mine honour into lust, to take away","The edge of that day's celebration,","When I shall think, or Phoebus' steeds are founder'd,","Or Night kept chain'd below."],"id":571,"m":"As I hope for peaceful days, fine children and a long life, with love like ours now, not the darkest cave, the most convenient place, nor the strongest temptation my worse angel can offer will ever melt my honour into lust and blunt the joy of our wedding day, when I'll think either that the sun god's horses have gone lame or that Night is chained underground."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Fairly spoke:","Sit, then, and talk with her, she is thine own.","What, Ariel! my industrious servant, Ariel!"],"id":572,"m":"Nicely said. Sit, then, and talk with her: she is yours. Ariel! My hard-working servant, Ariel!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ARIEL","id":573},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["What would my potent master? here I am."],"id":574,"m":"What does my powerful master want? Here I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Thou and thy meaner fellows your last service","Did worthily perform; and I must use you","In such another trick. Go bring the rabble,","O'er whom I give thee power, here to this place;","Incite them to quick motion; for I must","Bestow upon the eyes of this young couple","Some vanity of mine art: it is my promise,","And they expect it from me."],"id":575,"m":"You and your lesser companions did your last job well, and I must use you in another trick like it. Go and bring the crowd of spirits I've given you power over to this place. Make them move quickly, because I must show the eyes of this young couple some little display of my art. I promised it, and they expect it of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Presently?"],"id":576,"m":"Right now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Ay, with a twink."],"id":577,"m":"Yes, in the blink of an eye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Before you can say 'Come' and 'Go,'","And breathe twice; and cry 'so, so,'","Each one, tripping on his toe,","Will be here with mop and mow.","Do you love me, master? no?"],"id":578,"m":"Before you can say 'come' and 'go', and breathe twice, and cry 'so, so', each one, tripping on tiptoe, will be here pulling faces. Do you love me, master? No?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Dearly, my delicate Ariel. Do not approach","Till thou dost hear me call."],"id":579,"m":"Dearly, my delicate Ariel. Don't come near until you hear me call."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Well, I conceive."],"id":580,"m":"Well, I understand."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":581},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Look, thou be true; do not give dalliance","Too much the rein: the strongest oaths are straw","To th' fire i' the blood: be more abstemious,","Or else good night your vow!"],"id":582,"m":"Make sure you keep your word. Don't give your flirting too much rein. The strongest oaths are straw to the fire in the blood. Hold back more, or else goodbye to your vow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["I warrant you, sir;","The white-cold virgin snow upon my heart","Abates the ardour of my liver."],"id":583,"m":"I promise you, sir, the cold white virgin snow of her on my heart cools the heat of my passion."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Well.—","Now come, my Ariel! bring a corollary,","Rather than want a spirit: appear, and pertly.","No tongue! all eyes! be silent."],"id":584,"m":"Good. Now come, my Ariel! Bring one spirit too many rather than one too few. Appear, and be quick. No talking! All eyes! Be silent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Soft music","id":585},{"t":"sd","text":"A Masque. Enter IRIS","id":586},{"t":"sp","who":"IRIS","lines":["Ceres, most bounteous lady, thy rich leas","Of wheat, rye, barley, vetches, oats, and peas;","Thy turfy mountains, where live nibbling sheep,","And flat meads thatch'd with stover, them to keep;","Thy banks with pioned and twilled brims,","Which spongy April at thy hest betrims,","To make cold nymphs chaste crowns; and thy broom groves,","Whose shadow the dismissed bachelor loves,","Being lass-lorn: thy pole-clipt vineyard;","And thy sea-marge, sterile and rocky-hard,","Where thou thyself dost air: the Queen o' the sky,","Whose watery arch and messenger am I,","Bids thee leave these; and with her sovereign grace,","Here on this grass-plot, in this very place,","To come and sport; her peacocks fly amain:","Approach, rich Ceres, her to entertain."],"id":587,"m":"Ceres, most generous lady, your rich fields of wheat, rye, barley, vetch, oats and peas; your grassy hills where nibbling sheep live, and flat meadows covered with hay to feed them; your riverbanks with dug-out and woven edges, which rainy April decorates with flowers at your command to make chaste crowns for cold nymphs; your groves of broom, whose shade the rejected young man loves when he has lost his girl; your vineyard with its staked vines; and your seashore, barren and rocky, where you yourself take the air: the Queen of the sky, whose rainbow and messenger I am, tells you to leave all these and, by her royal favour, come and play here on this patch of grass, in this very place. Her peacocks are flying fast. Come near, rich Ceres, to welcome her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CERES","id":588},{"t":"sp","who":"CERES","lines":["Hail, many-colour'd messenger, that ne'er","Dost disobey the wife of Jupiter;","Who with thy saffron wings upon my flowers","Diffusest honey drops, refreshing showers:","And with each end of thy blue bow dost crown","My bosky acres and my unshrubb'd down,","Rich scarf to my proud earth; why hath thy queen","Summon'd me hither to this short-grass'd green?"],"id":589,"m":"Greetings, many-coloured messenger, who never disobeys the wife of Jupiter. With your saffron wings you scatter honey drops and refreshing showers on my flowers, and with each end of your blue bow you crown my wooded acres and my bare hills, a rich scarf for my proud earth. Why has your queen called me here to this short-grassed lawn?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IRIS","lines":["A contract of true love to celebrate,","And some donation freely to estate","On the blest lovers."],"id":590,"m":"To celebrate a contract of true love, and to give a gift freely to the blessed lovers."},{"t":"sp","who":"CERES","lines":["Tell me, heavenly bow,","If Venus or her son, as thou dost know,","Do now attend the queen? Since they did plot","The means that dusky Dis my daughter got,","Her and her blind boy's scandal'd company","I have forsworn."],"id":591,"m":"Tell me, heavenly rainbow, as far as you know, are Venus or her son now with the Queen? Since they plotted the way gloomy Pluto got my daughter, I have sworn to avoid her company and her blind boy's disgraceful company."},{"t":"sp","who":"IRIS","lines":["Of her society","Be not afraid. I met her deity","Cutting the clouds towards Paphos and her son","Dove-drawn with her. Here thought they to have done","Some wanton charm upon this man and maid,","Whose vows are, that no bed-rite shall be paid","Till Hymen's torch be lighted; but in vain.","Mars's hot minion is return'd again;","Her waspish-headed son has broke his arrows,","Swears he will shoot no more, but play with sparrows,","And be a boy right out."],"id":592,"m":"Don't be afraid of meeting her. I met the goddess cutting through the clouds towards Paphos, with her son, in her chariot drawn by doves. They planned to cast some lustful spell on this man and girl, who have vowed that no wedding-night rites will be paid until the torch of the marriage god is lit. But it was useless. Mars's hot lover has gone back home. Her hot-tempered son has broken his arrows, swears he'll shoot no more but play with sparrows, and be a plain boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CERES","lines":["Highest Queen of State,","Great Juno comes; I know her by her gait."],"id":593,"m":"The highest Queen of all, great Juno, is coming. I know her by her walk."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter JUNO.","id":594},{"t":"sp","who":"JUNO","lines":["How does my bounteous sister? Go with me","To bless this twain, that they may prosperous be,","And honour'd in their issue."],"id":595,"m":"How is my generous sister? Come with me to bless this couple, so that they may prosper and be honoured in their children."},{"t":"sp","who":"JUNO","lines":["Honour, riches, marriage-blessing,","Long continuance, and increasing,","Hourly joys be still upon you!","Juno sings her blessings on you."],"id":596,"m":"Honour, riches, the blessing of marriage, long life and growing families, may joys be with you every hour! Juno sings her blessings on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CERES","lines":["Earth's increase, foison plenty,","Barns and garners never empty;","Vines with clust'ring bunches growing;","Plants with goodly burden bowing;","Spring come to you at the farthest,","In the very end of harvest!","Scarcity and want shall shun you;","Ceres' blessing so is on you."],"id":597,"m":"The earth's harvest and plenty of produce, barns and granaries never empty, vines growing with clusters of grapes, plants bending under their fine loads! May spring come back to you at the latest as soon as harvest ends! Scarcity and need will avoid you. This is Ceres' blessing on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["This is a most majestic vision, and","Harmonious charmingly; may I be bold","To think these spirits?"],"id":598,"m":"This is a most majestic vision, and charmingly harmonious. May I be so bold as to think these are spirits?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Spirits, which by mine art","I have from their confines call'd to enact","My present fancies."],"id":599,"m":"Spirits, which I have called by my art from where they are kept to act out my present fancies."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Let me live here ever:","So rare a wonder'd father and a wise,","Makes this place Paradise."],"id":600,"m":"Let me live here for ever. Such a rare, wonder-working and wise father makes this place Paradise."},{"t":"sd","text":"JUNO and CERES whisper, and send IRIS on employment.","id":601},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Sweet now, silence!","Juno and Ceres whisper seriously,","There's something else to do: hush, and be mute,","Or else our spell is marr'd."],"id":602,"m":"Sweet one, quiet now! Juno and Ceres are whispering seriously. There's something more to come. Hush and be silent, or our spell will be spoiled."},{"t":"sp","who":"IRIS","lines":["You nymphs, call'd Naiads, of the windring brooks,","With your sedg'd crowns and ever-harmless looks,","Leave your crisp channels, and on this green land","Answer your summons: Juno does command.","Come, temperate nymphs, and help to celebrate","A contract of true love: be not too late."],"id":603,"m":"You nymphs, called Naiads, of the winding streams, with your crowns of reeds and your ever-innocent looks, leave your rippling channels and answer your summons on this green land. Juno commands it. Come, chaste nymphs, and help to celebrate a contract of true love. Don't be late."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain NYMPHS","id":604},{"t":"sp","who":"IRIS","cont":1,"lines":["You sun-burn'd sicklemen, of August weary,","Come hither from the furrow, and be merry:","Make holiday: your rye-straw hats put on,","And these fresh nymphs encounter every one","In country footing."],"id":605,"m":"You sunburnt harvesters, tired of August, come here from the furrows and be merry. Take a holiday. Put on your straw hats and join these fresh nymphs, every one of you, in a country dance."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain Reapers, properly habited: they join with the Nymphs in a graceful dance; towards the end whereof PROSPERO starts suddenly, and speaks; after which, to a strange, hollow, and confused noise, they heavily vanish.","id":606},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside] I had forgot that foul conspiracy","Of the beast Caliban and his confederates","Against my life: the minute of their plot","Is almost come. [To the Spirits.] Well done! avoid; no","more!"],"id":607,"m":"I had forgotten that foul plot of the beast Caliban and his accomplices against my life. The time set for their plot has almost come. Well done! Go away! No more!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["This is strange: your father's in some passion","That works him strongly."],"id":608,"m":"This is strange. Your father is in some passion that is shaking him badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Never till this day","Saw I him touch'd with anger so distemper'd."],"id":609,"m":"I have never seen him so moved by such violent anger until today."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["You do look, my son, in a mov'd sort,","As if you were dismay'd: be cheerful, sir:","Our revels now are ended. These our actors,","As I foretold you, were all spirits and","Are melted into air, into thin air:","And, like the baseless fabric of this vision,","The cloud-capp'd towers, the gorgeous palaces,","The solemn temples, the great globe itself,","Yea, all which it inherit, shall dissolve","And, like this insubstantial pageant faded,","Leave not a rack behind. We are such stuff","As dreams are made on, and our little life","Is rounded with a sleep.—Sir, I am vex'd:","Bear with my weakness; my old brain is troubled.","Be not disturb'd with my infirmity.","If you be pleas'd, retire into my cell","And there repose: a turn or two I'll walk,","To still my beating mind."],"id":610,"m":"You look upset, my son, as if you were alarmed. Cheer up, sir. Our entertainment is over now. These actors of ours, as I told you, were all spirits and have melted into air, into thin air. And, like the baseless structure of this vision, the cloud-topped towers, the splendid palaces, the solemn temples, the great globe itself, yes, and everyone who lives on it, will dissolve, and, just as this insubstantial show has faded, leave not a wisp of cloud behind. We are made of the same stuff as dreams, and our little life is surrounded by a sleep. Sir, I am troubled. Bear with my weakness: my old brain is disturbed. Don't be worried by my frailty. If you like, go into my cave and rest there. I'll walk around a little to calm my racing mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND AND MIRANDA","lines":["We wish your peace."],"id":611,"m":"We wish you peace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":612},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Come, with a thought.—[To them.] I thank thee:","Ariel, come!"],"id":613,"m":"Come as quick as thought. I thank you. Ariel, come!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ARIEL.","id":614},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Thy thoughts I cleave to. What's thy pleasure?"],"id":615,"m":"I cling to your thoughts. What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Spirit,","We must prepare to meet with Caliban."],"id":616,"m":"Spirit, we must get ready to meet Caliban."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Ay, my commander; when I presented Ceres,","I thought to have told thee of it: but I fear'd","Lest I might anger thee."],"id":617,"m":"Yes, my commander. When I played Ceres I meant to tell you about it, but I was afraid I might make you angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Say again, where didst thou leave these varlets?"],"id":618,"m":"Tell me again, where did you leave these rogues?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I told you, sir, they were red-hot with drinking;","So full of valour that they smote the air","For breathing in their faces; beat the ground","For kissing of their feet; yet always bending","Towards their project. Then I beat my tabor;","At which, like unback'd colts, they prick'd their ears,","Advanc'd their eyelids, lifted up their noses","As they smelt music: so I charm'd their ears,","That calf-like they my lowing follow'd through","Tooth'd briers, sharp furzes, pricking goss and thorns,","Which enter'd their frail shins: at last I left them","I' the filthy-mantled pool beyond your cell,","There dancing up to the chins, that the foul lake","O'erstunk their feet."],"id":619,"m":"I told you, sir, they were red-hot with drinking, so full of courage that they struck the air for breathing in their faces and beat the ground for kissing their feet, yet always heading towards their plan. Then I beat my drum, and at that, like unbroken colts, they pricked up their ears, opened their eyes wide and lifted their noses as if they could smell music. I charmed their ears so that they followed my mooing like calves, through toothed briars, sharp gorse, prickly furze and thorns, which pierced their tender shins. At last I left them in the scum-covered pond beyond your cave, dancing there up to their chins, so that the stinking pool smelled worse than their feet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["This was well done, my bird.","Thy shape invisible retain thou still:","The trumpery in my house, go bring it hither","For stale to catch these thieves."],"id":620,"m":"That was well done, my bird. Keep your invisible shape. Go and bring the showy clothes from my house here, as bait to catch these thieves."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I go, I go."],"id":621,"m":"I'm going, I'm going."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":622},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["A devil, a born devil, on whose nature","Nurture can never stick; on whom my pains,","Humanely taken, all, all lost, quite lost;","And as with age his body uglier grows,","So his mind cankers. I will plague them all,","Even to roaring."],"id":623,"m":"A devil, a born devil, whose nature can never be improved by upbringing. All my efforts, taken out of kindness, are all lost, completely lost. And as his body grows uglier with age, so his mind rots. I will torment them all, until they roar."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ARIEL, loaden with glistering apparel, &c.","id":624},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Come, hang them on this line."],"id":625,"m":"Come on, hang them on this line."},{"t":"sd","text":"PROSPERO and ARIEL remain invisible. Enter CALIBAN, STEPHANO, and TRINCULO, all wet","id":626},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Pray you, tread softly, that the blind mole may not","Hear a foot fall: we now are near his cell."],"id":627,"m":"Please, walk softly, so that even the blind mole can't hear a footstep. We're near his cave now."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Monster, your fairy, which you say is a harmless","fairy, has done little better than played the","Jack with us."],"id":628,"m":"Monster, your fairy, which you say is a harmless fairy, has done little more than play the fool with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Monster, I do smell all horse-piss; at which my","nose is in great indignation."],"id":629,"m":"Monster, I smell all of horse piss, and my nose is very offended by it."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["So is mine.—Do you hear, monster? If I should","take a displeasure against you, look you,—"],"id":630,"m":"So is mine. Do you hear, monster? If I took a dislike to you, look out..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Thou wert but a lost monster."],"id":631,"m":"You'd be a lost monster."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Good my lord, give me thy favour still:","Be patient, for the prize I'll bring thee to","Shall hoodwink this mischance: therefore speak softly;","All's hush'd as midnight yet."],"id":632,"m":"My good lord, keep favouring me. Be patient, because the prize I'll lead you to will make you forget this bad luck. So speak quietly. Everything's still as hushed as midnight."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Ay, but to lose our bottles in the pool!—"],"id":633,"m":"Yes, but to lose our bottles in the pond!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["There is not only disgrace and dishonour in","that, monster, but an infinite loss."],"id":634,"m":"There's not only shame and dishonour in that, monster, but an infinite loss."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["That's more to me than my wetting: yet this is","your harmless fairy, monster."],"id":635,"m":"That matters more to me than my soaking. And yet this is your harmless fairy, monster."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["I will fetch off my bottle, though I be o'er","ears for my labour."],"id":636,"m":"I'll get my bottle back, even if I'm in over my ears for my trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Prithee, my king, be quiet. Seest thou here,","This is the mouth o' the cell: no noise, and enter.","Do that good mischief which may make this island","Thine own for ever, and I, thy Caliban,","For aye thy foot-licker."],"id":637,"m":"Please, my king, be quiet. Do you see this? This is the mouth of the cave. No noise, and go in. Do that good wickedness that will make this island yours for ever, and me, your Caliban, your foot-licker for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Give me thy hand: I do begin to have bloody","thoughts."],"id":638,"m":"Give me your hand. I'm starting to have bloody thoughts."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["O King Stephano! O peer! O worthy Stephano!","Look what a wardrobe here is for thee!"],"id":639,"m":"Oh, King Stephano! Oh, lord! Oh, worthy Stephano! Look what a wardrobe there is here for you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Let it alone, thou fool; it is but trash."],"id":640,"m":"Leave it alone, you fool. It's only rubbish."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["O, ho, monster! we know what belongs to a","frippery.—O King Stephano!"],"id":641,"m":"Oh, ho, monster! We know what belongs in a second-hand clothes shop. Oh, King Stephano!"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Put off that gown, Trinculo; by this hand, I'll","have that gown."],"id":642,"m":"Take off that gown, Trinculo. By this hand, I'll have that gown."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Thy Grace shall have it."],"id":643,"m":"Your Grace shall have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["The dropsy drown this fool! What do you mean","To dote thus on such luggage? Let's along,","And do the murder first. If he awake,","From toe to crown he'll fill our skins with pinches;","Make us strange stuff."],"id":644,"m":"May the dropsy drown this fool! What do you mean by drooling over such junk? Let's go and do the murder first. If he wakes, he'll fill our skins with pinches from head to toe and turn us into strange stuff."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Be you quiet, monster.—Mistress line, is not","this my jerkin? Now is the jerkin under the line: now,","jerkin, you are like to lose your hair, and prove a bald","jerkin."],"id":645,"m":"You be quiet, monster. Mistress Line, isn't this my jacket? Now the jacket is under the line. Now, jacket, you're likely to lose your hair and turn out to be a bald jacket."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Do, do: we steal by line and level, an't like","your Grace."],"id":646,"m":"Go on, go on. We steal by the plumb line and the level, if it please your Grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["I thank thee for that jest: here's a garment","for't: wit shall not go unrewarded while I am king of","this country: 'Steal by line and level,' is an excellent","pass of pate: there's another garment for't."],"id":647,"m":"Thank you for that joke. Here's a garment for it. Wit won't go unrewarded while I'm king of this country. 'Steal by line and level' is an excellent piece of wit. There's another garment for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["Monster, come, put some lime upon your fingers,","and away with the rest."],"id":648,"m":"Monster, come on, put some glue on your fingers and take the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I will have none on't. We shall lose our time,","And all be turn'd to barnacles, or to apes","With foreheads villainous low."],"id":649,"m":"I'll have none of it. We'll waste our time and all be turned into barnacle geese, or into apes with horribly low foreheads."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Monster, lay-to your fingers: help to bear this","away where my hogshead of wine is, or I'll turn you out","of my kingdom. Go to; carry this."],"id":650,"m":"Monster, put your fingers to work. Help carry this to where my barrel of wine is, or I'll throw you out of my kingdom. Come on, carry this."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["And this."],"id":651,"m":"And this."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Ay, and this."],"id":652,"m":"Yes, and this."},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise of hunters heard. Enter divers Spirits, in shape of hounds, and hunt them about; PROSPERO and ARIEL setting them on","id":653},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Hey, Mountain, hey!"],"id":654,"m":"Hey, Mountain, hey!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Silver! there it goes, Silver!"],"id":655,"m":"Silver! There he goes, Silver!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Fury, Fury! There, Tyrant, there! hark, hark!"],"id":656,"m":"Fury, Fury! There, Tyrant, there! Listen, listen!"},{"t":"sd","text":"CALIBAN, STEPHANO, and TRINCULO are driven out.","id":657},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Go, charge my goblins that they grind their joints","With dry convulsions; shorten up their sinews","With aged cramps, and more pinch-spotted make them","Than pard, or cat o' mountain."],"id":658,"m":"Go, order my goblins to grind their joints with dry convulsions, shrink their muscles with old people's cramps, and make them more spotted with pinches than a leopard or a wildcat."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Hark, they roar."],"id":659,"m":"Listen, they're roaring."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Let them be hunted soundly. At this hour","Lies at my mercy all mine enemies;","Shortly shall all my labours end, and thou","Shalt have the air at freedom; for a little","Follow, and do me service."],"id":660,"m":"Let them be hunted thoroughly. At this moment all my enemies are at my mercy. Soon all my work will be finished, and you will have the freedom of the air. For a little longer, follow me and serve me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":661},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Prospero's cell.","id":662},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PROSPERO in his magic robes; and ARIEL.","id":663},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Now does my project gather to a head:","My charms crack not; my spirits obey, and time","Goes upright with his carriage. How's the day?"],"id":664,"m":"Now my plan is coming to its climax. My spells hold, my spirits obey, and time walks upright under its load. What time is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["On the sixth hour; at which time, my lord,","You said our work should cease."],"id":665,"m":"Nearly six o'clock, the time, my lord, when you said our work would end."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["I did say so,","When first I rais'd the tempest. Say, my spirit,","How fares the King and 's followers?"],"id":666,"m":"I did say so, when I first raised the storm. Tell me, my spirit, how are the King and his followers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Confin'd together","In the same fashion as you gave in charge;","Just as you left them: all prisoners, sir,","In the line-grove which weather-fends your cell;","They cannot budge till your release. The king,","His brother, and yours, abide all three distracted,","And the remainder mourning over them,","Brim full of sorrow and dismay; but chiefly","Him you term'd, sir, 'the good old lord, Gonzalo':","His tears run down his beard, like winter's drops","From eaves of reeds; your charm so strongly works them,","That if you now beheld them, your affections","Would become tender."],"id":667,"m":"Imprisoned together just as you ordered, exactly as you left them. They're all prisoners, sir, in the grove of lime trees that shelters your cave from the weather. They can't move until you release them. The King, his brother and yours are all three out of their minds, and the rest are mourning over them, brimming with sorrow and alarm, but most of all the one you called, sir, 'the good old lord, Gonzalo'. His tears run down his beard like winter rain from a thatched roof. Your spell works on them so strongly that if you saw them now, your feelings would soften."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Dost thou think so, spirit?"],"id":668,"m":"Do you think so, spirit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Mine would, sir, were I human."],"id":669,"m":"Mine would, sir, if I were human."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["And mine shall.","Hast thou, which art but air, a touch, a feeling","Of their afflictions, and shall not myself,","One of their kind, that relish all as sharply,","Passion as they, be kindlier mov'd than thou art?","Though with their high wrongs I am struck to the quick,","Yet with my nobler reason 'gainst my fury","Do I take part: the rarer action is","In virtue than in vengeance: they being penitent,","The sole drift of my purpose doth extend","Not a frown further. Go release them, Ariel.","My charms I'll break, their senses I'll restore,","And they shall be themselves."],"id":670,"m":"And mine will. If you, who are only air, have a sense and feeling of their sufferings, shouldn't I, one of their own kind, who feel everything as sharply and suffer as they do, be more kindly moved than you? Although their great wrongs have cut me to the quick, I take the side of my nobler reason against my fury. It is a rarer act to forgive than to take revenge. Since they are sorry, my purpose goes not one frown further. Go and release them, Ariel. I'll break my spells and restore their senses, and they'll be themselves again."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I'll fetch them, sir."],"id":671,"m":"I'll fetch them, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":672},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Ye elves of hills, brooks, standing lakes, and groves;","And ye that on the sands with printless foot","Do chase the ebbing Neptune, and do fly him","When he comes back; you demi-puppets that","By moonshine do the green sour ringlets make,","Whereof the ewe not bites; and you whose pastime","Is to make midnight mushrooms, that rejoice","To hear the solemn curfew; by whose aid,—","Weak masters though ye be,—I have bedimm'd","The noontide sun, call'd forth the mutinous winds,","And 'twixt the green sea and the azur'd vault","Set roaring war: to the dread rattling thunder","Have I given fire, and rifted Jove's stout oak","With his own bolt: the strong-bas'd promontory","Have I made shake; and by the spurs pluck'd up","The pine and cedar: graves at my command","Have wak'd their sleepers, op'd, and let them forth","By my so potent art. But this rough magic","I here abjure; and, when I have requir'd","Some heavenly music,—which even now I do,—","To work mine end upon their senses that","This airy charm is for, I'll break my staff,","Bury it certain fathoms in the earth,","And deeper than did ever plummet sound","I'll drown my book."],"id":673,"m":"You elves of the hills, the brooks, the still lakes and the woods, and you who chase the ebbing sea on the sands without leaving a footprint and run from it when it comes back; you tiny puppet-like fairies who make the sour green rings in the grass by moonlight, which the ewes won't eat; and you whose hobby is to make mushrooms grow at midnight, who love to hear the solemn evening bell: with your help, weak as you are, I have darkened the midday sun, called up the rebellious winds, and set a roaring war between the green sea and the blue sky. I have set fire to the dreadful rattling thunder and split Jove's sturdy oak with his own lightning bolt. I have made the firmly rooted headland shake and pulled up pines and cedars by their roots. At my command graves have woken their sleepers, opened, and let them out, by my powerful art. But I here give up this rough magic. And when I have called for some heavenly music, which I'm doing now, to work my purpose on the senses of those this airy spell is meant for, I'll break my staff, bury it several fathoms deep in the earth, and drown my book deeper than any plumb line ever sank."},{"t":"sd","text":"Solemn music","id":674},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ARIEL: after him, ALONSO, with frantic gesture, attended by GONZALO; SEBASTIAN and ANTONIO in like manner, attended by ADRIAN and FRANCISCO: they all enter the circle which PROSPERO had made, and there stand charmed: which PROSPERO observing, speaks.","id":675},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["A solemn air, and the best comforter","To an unsettled fancy, cure thy brains,","Now useless, boil'd within thy skull! There stand,","For you are spell-stopp'd.","Holy Gonzalo, honourable man,","Mine eyes, even sociable to the show of thine,","Fall fellowly drops. The charm dissolves apace;","And as the morning steals upon the night,","Melting the darkness, so their rising senses","Begin to chase the ignorant fumes that mantle","Their clearer reason.—O good Gonzalo!","My true preserver, and a loyal sir","To him thou follow'st, I will pay thy graces","Home, both in word and deed.—Most cruelly","Didst thou, Alonso, use me and my daughter:","Thy brother was a furtherer in the act;—","Thou'rt pinch'd for't now, Sebastian.—Flesh and blood,","You, brother mine, that entertain'd ambition,","Expell'd remorse and nature, who, with Sebastian,—","Whose inward pinches therefore are most strong,—","Would here have kill'd your king; I do forgive thee,","Unnatural though thou art! Their understanding","Begins to swell, and the approaching tide","Will shortly fill the reasonable shores","That now lie foul and muddy. Not one of them","That yet looks on me, or would know me.—Ariel,","Fetch me the hat and rapier in my cell:—"],"id":676,"m":"May a solemn tune, the best comfort for a disturbed mind, cure your brains, which are useless now and boiling inside your skull! Stand there, because you are frozen by a spell. Holy Gonzalo, honourable man, my eyes, in sympathy with the sight of yours, shed tears to keep them company. The spell is dissolving fast, and as the morning creeps up on the night and melts the darkness, so their returning senses begin to chase away the fog of confusion that covers their clearer reason. Oh, good Gonzalo, my true saviour and a loyal servant to the master you follow, I will repay your kindnesses in full, in words and deeds. Alonso, you treated me and my daughter most cruelly. Your brother helped you do it. You're tormented for it now, Sebastian. And you, my own flesh and blood, my brother, who welcomed ambition and drove out pity and natural feeling, who, with Sebastian (whose inner torments are therefore the strongest), would have killed your king here: I forgive you, unnatural as you are! Their understanding is starting to rise, and the coming tide will soon fill the shores of their reason, which now lie foul and muddy. Not one of them is yet looking at me or would recognise me. Ariel, fetch me the hat and sword from my cave."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit ARIEL","id":677},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["I will discase me, and myself present,","As I was sometime Milan.—Quickly, spirit;","Thou shalt ere long be free."],"id":678,"m":"I'll take off these robes and present myself as I used to be when I was Duke of Milan. Quickly, spirit. You'll soon be free."},{"t":"sd","text":"ARIEL re-enters, singing, and helps to attire PROSPERO.","id":679},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["Where the bee sucks, there suck I:","In a cowslip's bell I lie;","There I couch when owls do cry.","On the bat's back I do fly","After summer merrily:","Merrily, merrily shall I live now","Under the blossom that hangs on the bough."],"id":680,"m":"Where the bee sucks nectar, there I suck. I lie in a cowslip's bell, and rest there when owls cry. I fly on a bat's back merrily, following the summer. Merrily, merrily shall I live now, under the blossom that hangs on the branch."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Why, that's my dainty Ariel! I shall miss thee;","But yet thou shalt have freedom;—so, so, so.—","To the king's ship, invisible as thou art:","There shalt thou find the mariners asleep","Under the hatches; the master and the boatswain","Being awake, enforce them to this place,","And presently, I prithee."],"id":681,"m":"That's my dainty Ariel! I'll miss you, but you'll still have your freedom. So, so, so. Go to the King's ship, invisible as you are. There you'll find the sailors asleep below deck. When the captain and the boatswain are awake, make them come here, and at once, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["I drink the air before me, and return","Or ere your pulse twice beat."],"id":682,"m":"I'll drink the air in front of me and be back before your pulse beats twice."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":683},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["All torment, trouble, wonder and amazement","Inhabits here. Some heavenly power guide us","Out of this fearful country!"],"id":684,"m":"Every torment, trouble, wonder and amazement lives here. May some heavenly power guide us out of this terrifying country!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Behold, sir king,","The wronged Duke of Milan, Prospero.","For more assurance that a living prince","Does now speak to thee, I embrace thy body;","And to thee and thy company I bid","A hearty welcome."],"id":685,"m":"Look, sir King, the wronged Duke of Milan, Prospero. To assure you further that a living prince is speaking to you, I embrace you. And to you and your companions I give a hearty welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Whe'er thou be'st he or no,","Or some enchanted trifle to abuse me,","As late I have been, I not know: thy pulse","Beats, as of flesh and blood; and, since I saw thee,","Th' affliction of my mind amends, with which,","I fear, a madness held me: this must crave,—","An if this be at all—a most strange story.","Thy dukedom I resign, and do entreat","Thou pardon me my wrongs.—But how should Prospero","Be living and be here?"],"id":686,"m":"Whether you are him or not, or some enchanted thing to deceive me, as I have been deceived lately, I don't know. Your pulse beats like flesh and blood, and since I saw you, the disorder of my mind, which I'm afraid held me in madness, has got better. This must need, if it is real at all, a very strange story. I give back your dukedom and beg you to pardon my wrongs. But how can Prospero be alive and be here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["First, noble friend,","Let me embrace thine age; whose honour cannot","Be measur'd or confin'd."],"id":687,"m":"First, noble friend, let me embrace you, old man, whose honour cannot be measured or limited."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Whether this be","Or be not, I'll not swear."],"id":688,"m":"Whether this is real or not, I won't swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["You do yet taste","Some subtleties o' the isle, that will not let you","Believe things certain.—Welcome, my friends all:—","[Aside to SEBASTIAN and ANTONIO] But you, my brace of","lords, were I so minded,","I here could pluck his highness' frown upon you,","And justify you traitors: at this time","I will tell no tales."],"id":689,"m":"You are still feeling some of the island's illusions, which won't let you believe things that are certain. Welcome, all my friends! But you, my pair of lords, if I wanted to, I could bring down his Highness's anger on you right here and prove you traitors. For now I'll tell no tales."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["[Aside] The devil speaks in him."],"id":690,"m":"The devil is speaking through him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["No.","For you, most wicked sir, whom to call brother","Would even infect my mouth, I do forgive","Thy rankest fault; all of them; and require","My dukedom of thee, which, perforce, I know","Thou must restore."],"id":691,"m":"No. As for you, most wicked sir, whom it would infect my mouth to call brother, I forgive your foulest crime, all of them, and I demand my dukedom back from you, which I know you have no choice but to return."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["If thou beest Prospero,","Give us particulars of thy preservation;","How thou hast met us here, whom three hours since","Were wrack'd upon this shore; where I have lost,—","How sharp the point of this remembrance is!—","My dear son Ferdinand."],"id":692,"m":"If you are Prospero, tell us the details of how you survived, how you've met us here, who were wrecked on this shore three hours ago, where I have lost (how sharply this memory stabs me!) my dear son Ferdinand."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["I am woe for't, sir."],"id":693,"m":"I'm sorry for it, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Irreparable is the loss, and patience","Says it is past her cure."],"id":694,"m":"The loss can't be repaired, and patience says it's beyond her power to cure."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["I rather think","You have not sought her help; of whose soft grace,","For the like loss I have her sovereign aid,","And rest myself content."],"id":695,"m":"I think rather that you haven't asked for her help. By her gentle mercy I have her powerful aid for a loss just like yours, and I am content."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["You the like loss!"],"id":696,"m":"You, a loss like mine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["As great to me, as late; and, supportable","To make the dear loss, have I means much weaker","Than you may call to comfort you, for I","Have lost my daughter."],"id":697,"m":"As great to me, and as recent. And to make such a painful loss bearable I have far weaker comforts than you can call on, because I have lost my daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["A daughter?","O heavens! that they were living both in Naples,","The king and queen there! That they were, I wish","Myself were mudded in that oozy bed","Where my son lies. When did you lose your daughter?"],"id":698,"m":"A daughter? Oh heavens, if only they were both alive in Naples, as king and queen there! If only they were, I'd wish myself buried in that muddy bed where my son lies. When did you lose your daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["In this last tempest. I perceive, these lords","At this encounter do so much admire","That they devour their reason, and scarce think","Their eyes do offices of truth, their words","Are natural breath; but, howsoe'er you have","Been justled from your senses, know for certain","That I am Prospero, and that very duke","Which was thrust forth of Milan; who most strangely","Upon this shore, where you were wrack'd, was landed","To be the lord on't. No more yet of this;","For 'tis a chronicle of day by day,","Not a relation for a breakfast nor","Befitting this first meeting. Welcome, sir:","This cell's my court: here have I few attendants","And subjects none abroad: pray you, look in.","My dukedom since you have given me again,","I will requite you with as good a thing;","At least bring forth a wonder, to content ye","As much as me my dukedom."],"id":699,"m":"In this last storm. I can see that these lords are so amazed at this meeting that they swallow their reason and hardly believe that their eyes are telling the truth or that their words are natural breath. But however much you have been shaken out of your senses, know for certain that I am Prospero, the very duke who was thrown out of Milan, and who, most strangely, landed on this shore where you were wrecked, to be its lord. No more of this yet, because it's a story to be told day by day, not one for over breakfast, nor suitable for this first meeting. Welcome, sir. This cave is my court. Here I have few servants, and no subjects outside. Please, look in. Since you have given me back my dukedom, I'll repay you with something just as good, or at least show you a wonder that will please you as much as my dukedom pleases me."},{"t":"sd","text":"The entrance of the Cell opens, and discovers FERDINAND and MIRANDA playing at chess.","id":700},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Sweet lord, you play me false."],"id":701,"m":"Sweet lord, you're cheating me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["No, my dearest love,","I would not for the world."],"id":702,"m":"No, my dearest love, I wouldn't for the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["Yes, for a score of kingdoms you should wrangle,","And I would call it fair play."],"id":703,"m":"Yes, you would argue over twenty kingdoms, and I would call it fair play."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["If this prove","A vision of the island, one dear son","Shall I twice lose."],"id":704,"m":"If this turns out to be another illusion of the island, I'll have lost one dear son twice."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A most high miracle!"],"id":705,"m":"A miracle of the highest kind!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Though the seas threaten, they are merciful:","I have curs'd them without cause.","[Kneels to ALONSO.]"],"id":706,"m":"Though the seas threaten, they are merciful. I cursed them without reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Now all the blessings","Of a glad father compass thee about!","Arise, and say how thou cam'st here."],"id":707,"m":"Now may all the blessings of a happy father surround you! Stand up and tell me how you came here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MIRANDA","lines":["O, wonder!","How many goodly creatures are there here!","How beauteous mankind is! O brave new world","That has such people in't!"],"id":708,"m":"Oh, wonder! How many fine creatures there are here! How beautiful mankind is! Oh, splendid new world that has such people in it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["'Tis new to thee."],"id":709,"m":"It's new to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["What is this maid, with whom thou wast at play?","Your eld'st acquaintance cannot be three hours:","Is she the goddess that hath sever'd us,","And brought us thus together?"],"id":710,"m":"Who is this girl you were playing with? You can't have known her for more than three hours. Is she the goddess who separated us and has brought us together like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FERDINAND","lines":["Sir, she is mortal;","But by immortal Providence she's mine.","I chose her when I could not ask my father","For his advice, nor thought I had one. She","Is daughter to this famous Duke of Milan,","Of whom so often I have heard renown,","But never saw before; of whom I have","Receiv'd a second life: and second father","This lady makes him to me."],"id":711,"m":"Sir, she is mortal, but by immortal Providence she is mine. I chose her when I couldn't ask my father for his advice, and didn't think I had a father. She is the daughter of this famous Duke of Milan, whose fame I have heard of so often but whom I never saw before. From him I have received a second life, and this lady makes him a second father to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I am hers:","But, O! how oddly will it sound that I","Must ask my child forgiveness!"],"id":712,"m":"And I am a second father to her. But oh, how strange it will sound that I must ask my child to forgive me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["There, sir, stop:","Let us not burden our remembrances with","A heaviness that's gone."],"id":713,"m":"Stop there, sir. Let's not weigh down our memories with a sadness that's over."},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["I have inly wept,","Or should have spoke ere this. Look down, you gods,","And on this couple drop a blessed crown;","For it is you that have chalk'd forth the way","Which brought us hither."],"id":714,"m":"I have been weeping inside, or I would have spoken before now. Look down, you gods, and drop a blessed crown on this couple, because it was you who marked out the path that brought us here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I say, Amen, Gonzalo!"],"id":715,"m":"I say amen, Gonzalo!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Was Milan thrust from Milan, that his issue","Should become kings of Naples? O, rejoice","Beyond a common joy, and set it down","With gold on lasting pillars. In one voyage","Did Claribel her husband find at Tunis,","And Ferdinand, her brother, found a wife","Where he himself was lost; Prospero his dukedom","In a poor isle; and all of us ourselves,","When no man was his own."],"id":716,"m":"Was the Duke of Milan thrown out of Milan so that his children could become kings of Naples? Oh, rejoice beyond ordinary joy, and write it in gold on lasting pillars! On one voyage Claribel found her husband at Tunis, and Ferdinand, her brother, found a wife where he himself was lost; Prospero found his dukedom on a poor island; and all of us found ourselves, when none of us was in his right mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["[To FERDINAND and MIRANDA] Give me your hands:","Let grief and sorrow still embrace his heart","That doth not wish you joy!"],"id":717,"m":"Give me your hands. May grief and sorrow always grip the heart of anyone who doesn't wish you joy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","lines":["Be it so. Amen!"],"id":718,"m":"So be it! Amen!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ARIEL, with the Master and Boatswain amazedly following.","id":719},{"t":"sp","who":"GONZALO","cont":1,"lines":["O look, sir! look, sir! Here are more of us.","I prophesied, if a gallows were on land,","This fellow could not drown.—Now, blasphemy,","That swear'st grace o'erboard, not an oath on shore?","Hast thou no mouth by land? What is the news?"],"id":720,"m":"Oh look, sir! Look, sir! Here are more of us! I predicted that if there was a gallows on land, this fellow couldn't drown. Now, you blasphemer, who swore heaven's grace overboard, not one oath on shore? Have you no mouth on land? What's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["The best news is that we have safely found","Our king and company: the next, our ship,—","Which but three glasses since we gave out split,—","Is tight and yare, and bravely rigg'd as when","We first put out to sea."],"id":721,"m":"The best news is that we have found our King and his company safe. The next is that our ship, which only three hours ago we said was wrecked, is watertight, ready and as finely rigged as when we first put out to sea."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["[Aside to PROSPERO] Sir, all this service","Have I done since I went."],"id":722,"m":"Sir, I have done all this since I left."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside to ARIEL] My tricksy spirit!"],"id":723,"m":"My clever spirit!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["These are not natural events; they strengthen","From strange to stranger—Say, how came you hither?"],"id":724,"m":"These are not natural events. They grow stranger and stranger. Tell me, how did you get here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOATSWAIN","lines":["If I did think, sir, I were well awake,","I'd strive to tell you. We were dead of sleep,","And,—how, we know not,—all clapp'd under hatches,","Where, but even now, with strange and several noises","Of roaring, shrieking, howling, jingling chains,","And mo diversity of sounds, all horrible,","We were awak'd; straightway, at liberty:","Where we, in all her trim, freshly beheld","Our royal, good, and gallant ship; our master","Cap'ring to eye her: on a trice, so please you,","Even in a dream, were we divided from them,","And were brought moping hither."],"id":725,"m":"If I thought I was wide awake, sir, I'd try to tell you. We were fast asleep and, we don't know how, all shut up below deck. Just now we were woken by strange and different noises of roaring, shrieking, howling, jangling chains and more kinds of sounds, all horrible, and at once we were free. There we saw our royal, good and gallant ship in perfect condition, and our captain dancing with joy to see her. In an instant, if you please, as if in a dream, we were separated from the others and brought here in a daze."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARIEL","lines":["[Aside to PROSPERO] Was't well done?"],"id":726,"m":"Did I do it well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["[Aside to ARIEL] Bravely, my diligence. Thou","shalt be free."],"id":727,"m":"Splendidly, my busy one. You'll be free."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["This is as strange a maze as e'er men trod;","And there is in this business more than nature","Was ever conduct of: some oracle","Must rectify our knowledge."],"id":728,"m":"This is as strange a maze as men ever walked, and there is more in this business than nature ever brought about. Some oracle must explain it to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Sir, my liege,","Do not infest your mind with beating on","The strangeness of this business: at pick'd leisure,","Which shall be shortly, single I'll resolve you,—","Which to you shall seem probable—of every","These happen'd accidents; till when, be cheerful","And think of each thing well.—[Aside to ARIEL] Come","hither, spirit;","Set Caliban and his companions free;","Untie the spell. [Exit ARIEL] How fares my gracious sir?","There are yet missing of your company","Some few odd lads that you remember not."],"id":729,"m":"Sir, my lord, don't trouble your mind by hammering on the strangeness of this business. At a chosen time, which will be soon, I'll explain to you in private every one of these events, in a way that will seem believable to you. Until then, be cheerful and think well of everything. Come here, spirit. Set Caliban and his companions free. Undo the spell. How are you, my gracious sir? There are still a few odd fellows of your company missing whom you've forgotten."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ARIEL, driving in CALIBAN, STEPHANO, and TRINCULO, in their stolen apparel.","id":730},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["Every man shift for all the rest, and let no man","take care for himself, for all is but fortune.—Coragio!","bully-monster, Coragio!"],"id":731,"m":"Every man look after all the rest, and let no man care about himself, because everything is just luck. Courage, fine monster, courage!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["If these be true spies which I wear in my head,","here's a goodly sight."],"id":732,"m":"If these eyes in my head are telling the truth, here's a fine sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["O Setebos, these be brave spirits indeed.","How fine my master is! I am afraid","He will chastise me."],"id":733,"m":"Oh Setebos, these are splendid spirits indeed! How fine my master looks! I'm afraid he'll punish me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Ha, ha!","What things are these, my lord Antonio?","Will money buy them?"],"id":734,"m":"Ha, ha! What are these things, my lord Antonio? Can money buy them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Very like; one of them","Is a plain fish, and, no doubt, marketable."],"id":735,"m":"Very likely. One of them is plainly a fish, and no doubt sellable."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Mark but the badges of these men, my lords,","Then say if they be true.—This mis-shapen knave—","His mother was a witch; and one so strong","That could control the moon, make flows and ebbs,","And deal in her command without her power.","These three have robb'd me; and this demi-devil,—","For he's a bastard one,—had plotted with them","To take my life: two of these fellows you","Must know and own; this thing of darkness I","Acknowledge mine."],"id":736,"m":"Just look at these men's uniforms, my lords, and then say if they're honest. This misshapen rogue's mother was a witch, and such a strong one that she could control the moon, make tides flow and ebb, and use the moon's power without its authority. These three have robbed me, and this half-devil (for he's a bastard one) had plotted with them to take my life. Two of these fellows you must know and claim as yours. This thing of darkness I admit is mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["I shall be pinch'd to death."],"id":737,"m":"I'll be pinched to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Is not this Stephano, my drunken butler?"],"id":738,"m":"Isn't this Stephano, my drunken butler?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["He is drunk now: where had he wine?"],"id":739,"m":"He's drunk now. Where did he get wine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["And Trinculo is reeling-ripe: where should they","Find this grand liquor that hath gilded them?","How cam'st thou in this pickle?"],"id":740,"m":"And Trinculo is staggering drunk. Where did they find this marvellous liquor that has flushed them gold? How did you get into this pickle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRINCULO","lines":["I have been in such a pickle since I saw you","last that, I fear me, will never out of my bones. I","shall not fear fly-blowing."],"id":741,"m":"I've been in such a pickle since I last saw you that I'm afraid it will never get out of my bones. I won't have to worry about flies laying eggs in me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Why, how now, Stephano!"],"id":742,"m":"Well, what's this, Stephano?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["O! touch me not: I am not Stephano, but a cramp."],"id":743,"m":"Oh, don't touch me! I'm not Stephano, I'm one big cramp."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["You'd be king o' the isle, sirrah?"],"id":744,"m":"You wanted to be king of the island, did you, fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEPHANO","lines":["I should have been a sore one, then."],"id":745,"m":"I'd have been a sore one, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["This is as strange a thing as e'er I look'd on.","[Pointing to CALIBAN]"],"id":746,"m":"This is as strange a thing as I ever looked at."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["He is as disproportioned in his manners","As in his shape.—Go, sirrah, to my cell;","Take with you your companions: as you look","To have my pardon, trim it handsomely."],"id":747,"m":"He is as misshapen in his behaviour as in his body. Go to my cave, fellow, and take your companions with you. If you hope for my pardon, tidy it up nicely."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALIBAN","lines":["Ay, that I will; and I'll be wise hereafter,","And seek for grace. What a thrice-double ass","Was I, to take this drunkard for a god,","And worship this dull fool!"],"id":748,"m":"Yes, I will. And I'll be wise from now on and seek forgiveness. What a triple-double ass I was to take this drunk for a god and worship this stupid fool!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Go to; away!"],"id":749,"m":"Go on, off with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["Hence, and bestow your luggage where you found it."],"id":750,"m":"Go, and put that junk back where you found it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Or stole it, rather."],"id":751,"m":"Or stole it, rather."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CALIBAN, STEPHANO, and TRINCULO.","id":752},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["Sir, I invite your Highness and your train","To my poor cell, where you shall take your rest","For this one night; which—part of it—I'll waste","With such discourse as, I not doubt, shall make it","Go quick away; the story of my life","And the particular accidents gone by","Since I came to this isle: and in the morn","I'll bring you to your ship, and so to Naples,","Where I have hope to see the nuptial","Of these our dear-belov'd solemnized;","And thence retire me to my Milan, where","Every third thought shall be my grave."],"id":753,"m":"Sir, I invite your Highness and your followers to my poor cave, where you will rest for this one night. I'll spend part of it with talk that I'm sure will make it pass quickly: the story of my life and all the events that have happened since I came to this island. And in the morning I'll take you to your ship and so to Naples, where I hope to see the wedding of these dearly loved young people celebrated. And from there I'll retire to my Milan, where every third thought will be of my grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALONSO","lines":["I long To hear the story of your life, which must","Take the ear strangely."],"id":754,"m":"I long to hear the story of your life, which must be strange to hear."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","lines":["I'll deliver all;","And promise you calm seas, auspicious gales,","And sail so expeditious that shall catch","Your royal fleet far off.—[Aside to ARIEL] My Ariel,","chick,","That is thy charge: then to the elements","Be free, and fare thou well!—Please you, draw near."],"id":755,"m":"I'll tell you everything, and I promise you calm seas, favourable winds and sailing so fast that you'll catch up with your royal fleet far ahead. My Ariel, my chick, that is your job. Then be free to the elements, and farewell! Please, come in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":756},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Epilogue.","id":757},{"t":"sp","who":"PROSPERO","cont":1,"lines":["Now my charms are all o'erthrown,","And what strength I have's mine own;","Which is most faint; now 'tis true,","I must be here confin'd by you,","Or sent to Naples. Let me not,","Since I have my dukedom got,","And pardon'd the deceiver, dwell","In this bare island by your spell:","But release me from my bands","With the help of your good hands.","Gentle breath of yours my sails","Must fill, or else my project fails,","Which was to please. Now I want","Spirits to enforce, art to enchant;","And my ending is despair,","Unless I be reliev'd by prayer,","Which pierces so that it assaults","Mercy itself, and frees all faults.","As you from crimes would pardon'd be,","Let your indulgence set me free."],"id":758,"m":"Now my spells are all overthrown, and what strength I have is my own, which is very weak. Now it's true that I must either be kept here by you or sent to Naples. Since I have got my dukedom back and pardoned the man who deceived me, don't let me stay on this bare island by your spell, but release me from my bonds with the help of your good hands. Your kind breath must fill my sails, or else my plan fails, which was to please you. Now I have no spirits to command and no magic to enchant, and my ending will be despair unless I am helped by prayer, which pierces so deeply that it storms mercy itself and frees all faults. As you would be pardoned for your sins, let your kindness set me free."}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Prospero","group":"The island","desc":"The rightful Duke of Milan, overthrown by his brother twelve years ago and set adrift with his baby daughter. On the island he has become a powerful magician, served by the spirit Ariel and the slave Caliban. He raises the storm to bring his enemies into his power, arranges his daughter's match, then chooses forgiveness, frees Ariel, gives up his magic and asks the audience to set him free."},{"name":"Miranda","group":"The island","desc":"Prospero's daughter, about fifteen. She was not yet three when they were cast away, and her only memory of Milan is a dream-like sense of four or five women who looked after her. Tender-hearted (she suffers with the drowning sailors), honest and direct: she proposes to Ferdinand herself. Her 'O brave new world' greets the very men who wronged her father."},{"name":"Ariel","group":"The island","desc":"An 'airy spirit' Prospero freed from the pine tree where the witch Sycorax had shut him for twelve years, because he was too delicate to carry out her foul orders. He performs the storm, the songs, the harpy and the masque, and longs for the freedom he is promised: a year off his service, and then release within two days. Quick, playful and, at the key moment, the voice of pity."},{"name":"Caliban","group":"The island","desc":"Son of the witch Sycorax, who ruled the island before Prospero. Prospero says his father was the devil. At first Prospero treated him kindly and taught him language, and Caliban showed him the island's fresh springs and fertile ground; he was enslaved after he tried to rape Miranda. He curses Prospero, worships the drunk Stephano, plots murder, yet speaks some of the play's most beautiful lines about the island. Ends promising to 'seek for grace'."},{"name":"Ferdinand","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"Son of King Alonso, heir to Naples. Believes his father drowned. Falls in love with Miranda at first sight, patiently carries logs to prove himself, and is betrothed to her. Found playing chess with her in the final scene."},{"name":"Alonso","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"King of Naples. Twelve years ago he helped Antonio overthrow Prospero. Grief for his son, whom he thinks drowned, and Ariel's accusation as the harpy bring him to guilt and repentance. He returns Milan to Prospero and blesses his son's marriage."},{"name":"Antonio","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"Prospero's younger brother, who seized the dukedom of Milan. To win Alonso's help he agreed to pay Naples a yearly tribute and make Milan subject to it. Cynical and clever, he tempts Sebastian to murder Alonso on the island. Prospero forgives him, but Antonio never answers or shows remorse."},{"name":"Sebastian","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"Alonso's brother. Mocks Gonzalo with Antonio and is easily talked into a plot to kill the sleeping king and take the crown of Naples. Unrepentant at the end apart from one sneering aside."},{"name":"Gonzalo","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"An honest old counsellor. Put in charge of setting Prospero adrift, he secretly gave him food, water, clothes and books when he was set adrift. On the island he tries to cheer Alonso, imagines an ideal commonwealth, is mocked by Antonio and Sebastian, and in the last scene sums up how everyone 'found' themselves."},{"name":"Stephano","group":"The comic plot","desc":"Alonso's drunken butler. Washed ashore on a barrel of wine, he is worshipped by Caliban, declares himself king of the island and agrees to murder Prospero, but is distracted by fine clothes and hunted off by spirit dogs."},{"name":"Trinculo","group":"The comic plot","desc":"Alonso's jester. Shelters from the storm under Caliban's cloak, mocks the 'monster', and is beaten by Stephano when Ariel's invisible voice makes it seem he is calling him a liar."},{"name":"Adrian","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"A lord in Alonso's party who praises the island's sweet air and is mocked for it by Antonio and Sebastian."},{"name":"Francisco","group":"The court of Naples","desc":"A lord in Alonso's party who tries to comfort the King with a vivid account of Ferdinand swimming strongly to shore."},{"name":"Boatswain","group":"The ship","desc":"The ship's officer who orders the courtiers below deck in the storm: 'What cares these roarers for the name of king?' He reappears, safe, in the final scene."},{"name":"Iris","group":"The masque","desc":"Goddess of the rainbow and Juno's messenger, played by one of Prospero's spirits. She opens the betrothal masque and summons nymphs and reapers to dance."},{"name":"Ceres","group":"The masque","desc":"Goddess of harvest, played by Ariel (he says 'when I presented Ceres'). She blesses the couple with full barns and plenty, and refuses to meet Venus and Cupid."},{"name":"Juno","group":"The masque","desc":"Queen of the gods and goddess of marriage, played by a spirit. She sings a blessing of honour, riches and children on Ferdinand and Miranda."},{"name":"Sycorax","group":"Offstage","desc":"A witch from Algiers, Caliban's mother, now dead. Banished to the island while pregnant, she imprisoned Ariel in a pine. Prospero describes her as his dark opposite."},{"name":"Claribel","group":"Offstage","desc":"Alonso's daughter, just married to the King of Tunis. The fleet was sailing home from her wedding when the storm struck."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"A ship carrying the King of Naples and his court is caught in a violent storm. The boatswain orders the noble passengers out of the way, telling them their titles mean nothing to the waves. Gonzalo jokes that the boatswain looks born to be hanged, not drowned. Antonio and Sebastian curse the crew. The ship seems to split, and everyone prepares to die.","key":["Rank means nothing in the face of nature: the boatswain's 'What cares these roarers for the name of king?'","Gonzalo's humour and patience against Antonio and Sebastian's insults show their characters at once.","We assume the wreck is real; the next scene reveals it is magic."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who orders the King's party to stay below deck?","o":["The Master","The Boatswain","Gonzalo","Ferdinand"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Gonzalo feel comforted by the boatswain?","o":["He is a skilled sailor who has weathered many storms at sea","He looks born to be hanged, so cannot drown","He prays loudly","He is the King's cousin"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who curses the boatswain most rudely?","o":["Alonso and his son Ferdinand","Antonio and Sebastian","Gonzalo and old Adrian","The mariners"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Boatswain tell Gonzalo to do with his authority?","o":["Use it to command the storm to be quiet","Use it to order the sailors to row harder","Use it to keep the King safe below deck","Use it to send for help from the shore"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Gonzalo say he would give for dry land?","o":["A thousand furlongs of sea for an acre of barren ground","All of the gold in Naples for a single safe harbour","His title and lands for one more day of calm weather","Every jewel on board for a sight of the Milan coast"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"On the island, Miranda begs her father to calm the storm. Prospero assures her no one has been harmed, then tells her their story: twelve years ago his brother Antonio, helped by the King of Naples, seized his dukedom of Milan and set them adrift; kind Gonzalo gave them supplies and books. Miranda sleeps. Ariel reports how he performed the storm and scattered the survivors, while the rest of the fleet sails sadly home believing the King drowned, and asks for his promised freedom; Prospero angrily reminds him how he rescued him from the witch Sycorax, then promises freedom in two days. Prospero and Miranda visit Caliban, who claims the island was his and curses them; Prospero threatens him with cramps. Ariel's songs lead Ferdinand to Miranda, and they fall in love at first sight. Prospero pretends to suspect Ferdinand as a spy, charms his sword and sets him to slave labour.","key":["Prospero's long story gives the play's back-story and shows his sense of injustice.","Ariel and Caliban are two very different servants: one longs for freedom, the other for his lost island.","Ferdinand and Miranda fall in love exactly as Prospero planned; he makes it hard 'lest too light winning make the prize light'."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long ago were Prospero and Miranda driven from Milan?","o":["Three years","Twelve years","Twenty years","Two years"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who gave Prospero his books?","o":["Antonio","Alonso","Gonzalo","Ariel"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where had Sycorax imprisoned Ariel?","o":["In a hollow oak tree","In a cloven pine","In a cave by the sea","In the sea, under a rock"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Prospero say Caliban is kept confined?","o":["He stole food","He tried to violate Miranda","He freed Ariel","He refused to learn"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What did Antonio promise the King of Naples in return for his help?","o":["Miranda's hand in marriage to the King's son, Ferdinand, when she came of age","A yearly tribute, with Milan made subject to Naples","Prospero's books","The island"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Elsewhere on the island, Gonzalo tries to cheer Alonso, who believes his son Ferdinand is drowned. Antonio and Sebastian mock Gonzalo's every word. Francisco says he saw Ferdinand swim towards shore. Sebastian blames Alonso for marrying his daughter Claribel to the King of Tunis. Gonzalo describes the ideal commonwealth he would create on the island. Ariel's music puts everyone to sleep except Antonio and Sebastian. Antonio persuades Sebastian to kill his sleeping brother and become King of Naples, as Antonio himself became Duke of Milan. As they draw their swords, Ariel wakes Gonzalo, and the plotters invent a story about roaring lions.","key":["Antonio repeats his original crime by tempting Sebastian to kill a brother.","Gonzalo's dream of a perfect commonwealth questions how power and property should work.","Ariel's intervention shows Prospero watching and controlling everything."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom has Alonso's daughter Claribel just married?","o":["The Duke of Milan","The King of Tunis","Ferdinand","The Prince of Algiers"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What would Gonzalo's ideal commonwealth have no need of?","o":["Food grown by the sweat of men","Trade, magistrates and work","Women and wedding vows","Music"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What excuse do Antonio and Sebastian give for their drawn swords?","o":["They heard lions roaring","They saw a ghost","They were fighting each other","They saw Caliban"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"Why does Sebastian blame Alonso for Ferdinand's loss?","o":["He refused to turn the ship back to Naples in time","He married Claribel to an African against advice","He made Ferdinand sail on the most dangerous ship","He sent Ferdinand ahead alone to look for land"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How does Antonio persuade Sebastian to kill Alonso?","o":["He says that the King has insulted Sebastian","He promises to share the crown of Naples equally","He says the gods have ordered it in a dream","He points out his own success in taking Milan"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Caliban, carrying wood and cursing Prospero, hides under his cloak when he sees Trinculo, thinking he is a tormenting spirit. Trinculo, fearing another storm, crawls under the cloak too. The drunken Stephano finds what looks like a four-legged monster and gives it wine. He pulls out Trinculo and the two friends rejoice. Caliban decides Stephano is a god with 'celestial liquor', kneels to him and promises to show him the island. He sings drunkenly of his freedom from Prospero.","key":["Caliban swaps one master for another and calls it freedom.","The comic plot mirrors the serious ones: servants plotting to be kings.","Trinculo's joke about people paying to see a 'dead Indian' links Caliban to the European fascination with the New World."],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Stephano get ashore?","o":["He swam ashore like a duck all the way","On a barrel of sack (wine)","In the ship's boat with the crew","Ariel carried him over the waves"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Caliban worship Stephano?","o":["He has magic books","He thinks his wine is heavenly","He is the King","He frees Ariel"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Caliban promise Stephano?","o":["Gold and jewels from the caves of the island","To show him the island and serve him","Miranda's hand","A ship home"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does Trinculo think he could do with Caliban in England?","o":["Sell him as a servant to a great lord","Give him to the King as a strange gift","Show him for money as a strange fish","Teach him to speak and to read English"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Caliban promise to do for Stephano's food?","o":["Hunt deer with a bow in the forests","Dig for pignuts and find him fresh springs","Cook fish over a fire on the beach","Steal fruit from Prospero's garden"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Ferdinand carries logs for Prospero, glad to do it for Miranda's sake. Miranda offers to carry them for him. She tells him her name, against her father's order. They declare their love, and Miranda offers to be his wife, or his servant if he will not marry her. They agree to marry. Prospero, watching unseen, is delighted.","key":["Ferdinand turns hard labour into a test of love.","Miranda speaks plainly and proposes herself, breaking with her father's instructions.","Prospero's hidden watching shows the romance is real but also part of his plan."],"quiz":[{"q":"What task has Prospero given Ferdinand?","o":["Fishing for their supper","Carrying and piling logs","Guarding Prospero's cell at night","Building a boat"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What order does Miranda break?","o":["Not to leave the cell while her father studied","Not to tell Ferdinand her name","Not to sing to the young prince at his work","Not to touch the logs or help him in any way"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Miranda say she will be if Ferdinand will not marry her?","o":["A nun","His servant","A queen","A spirit"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Ferdinand not mind his hard labour?","o":["Prospero has promised to free him soon","He is used to such work in his own home","He thinks his father will soon rescue him","Thinking of Miranda makes it a pleasure"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How many women's faces has Miranda seen?","o":["None but her own, in the glass","Only those of her mother's maids","Many, in pictures in the cell","Two, her own and her mother's"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Stephano, Trinculo and Caliban are drunk. Caliban asks Stephano to kill Prospero. The invisible Ariel keeps saying 'Thou liest', so Stephano thinks Trinculo is interrupting and beats him. Caliban explains the plan: kill Prospero while he sleeps in the afternoon, but seize his books first; Stephano can then take Miranda and be king. Ariel plays their tune on a pipe and drum, frightening Stephano and Trinculo. Caliban comforts them with a speech about the island's sweet noises. They follow the music.","key":["The murder plot parodies Antonio's earlier plotting.","Caliban knows the books are the source of Prospero's power.","'The isle is full of noises' gives the 'monster' the play's most lyrical speech."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Ariel say to cause trouble?","o":["'Beware, beware!'","'Thou liest'","'Follow me'","'Awake!'"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What must the plotters seize first, according to Caliban?","o":["The bottle of sack","Prospero's books","Miranda","Ariel's magic pipe"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Caliban say the isle is full of?","o":["Spirits, devils and goblins","Noises, sounds and sweet airs","Gold and jewels hidden in caves","Fresh springs, berries and fish"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Caliban say about Prospero's books?","o":["They are full of lies and should be read aloud","They hold the secret of the island's treasure","They were stolen from Caliban's mother Sycorax","Without them he is only a sot like Caliban"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Stephano make Trinculo do after the beating?","o":["Kneel and beg pardon","Stand further off","Sing a catch alone","Hold the bottle"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Worn out from searching, the King's party stops to rest, and Alonso decides his son must be dead. Antonio and Sebastian agree to kill him that night. Strange shapes bring in a banquet and invite them to eat, then Ariel appears as a harpy, makes the banquet vanish and accuses Alonso, Antonio and Sebastian of overthrowing Prospero. He tells Alonso his son's death is his punishment and that only sorrow and a clean life can save him. Alonso, horrified, runs off to drown himself; Antonio and Sebastian go to fight the spirits. Gonzalo sends the others after them.","key":["The harpy speech names the crime directly and calls for repentance.","Alonso responds with guilt; Antonio and Sebastian only with swords.","Prospero, watching, declares that his enemies are now 'in my power'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What form does Ariel take to accuse the lords?","o":["A sea nymph","A harpy","A hound","A ghost"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What happens to the banquet?","o":["They eat it","It vanishes","Caliban steals it","It is poisoned"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Alonso set off to do?","o":["Kill Prospero","Drown himself to lie with his son","Find the ship","Pray"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why are Antonio and Sebastian glad the King is tired?","o":["They want to go back to the ship","They need time to rest themselves","They hope he will give up searching","They plan to kill him that night"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Gonzalo say about the three men of sin?","o":["They are bewitched by a strange spirit","They have gone to find the King's son","They are angry with the harpy's words","Their guilt is now biting their spirits"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Prospero gives Miranda to Ferdinand, warning him not to break her virginity before the wedding. He has his spirits perform a masque: Iris, Ceres and Juno bless the couple with prosperity and children, and nymphs and reapers dance. Suddenly Prospero remembers Caliban's plot and stops the show, telling Ferdinand that 'our revels now are ended' and all life is like a dream. Ariel describes leading the drunken plotters through briars into a stinking pond. Prospero hangs fine clothes as bait; Stephano and Trinculo grab them despite Caliban's protests, and spirits in the shape of hounds chase them away.","key":["The masque celebrates chaste marriage, fertility and harmony.","'We are such stuff as dreams are made on': Prospero's reflection on illusion and mortality.","The plotters are defeated by their own greed for flashy clothes."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which goddesses appear in the masque?","o":["Venus, Diana and Minerva","Iris, Ceres and Juno","Athena, Hera and Aphrodite","Flora and Pomona"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Prospero suddenly end the masque?","o":["Miranda faints at the sight of the goddesses","He remembers Caliban's plot","Ariel interrupts","Ferdinand laughs"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What distracts Stephano and Trinculo from murder?","o":["Wine from Stephano's barrel","Fine clothes hung on a line","Music from the air","Miranda"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Prospero say Miranda is to Ferdinand?","o":["A third of his own life, a gift of his own","A prize Ferdinand has paid for in gold","A servant who will cook and clean for him","A wife who will one day rule all of Milan"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Prospero punish the plotters at the end?","o":["He turns them into stones by the sea","He sets spirits like hounds on them","He locks them up in a pine-tree","He sends them back to the ship"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Ariel tells Prospero the prisoners are distracted with grief and that he would pity them if he were human. Prospero decides that 'the rarer action is in virtue than in vengeance'. He renounces his magic, promising to break his staff and drown his book. The charmed lords are brought in; Prospero reveals himself as Duke of Milan, forgives them, and takes back his dukedom. He shows Alonso Ferdinand and Miranda playing chess; father and son are reunited, and Miranda marvels at the 'brave new world'. The boatswain reports the ship is safe. Caliban, Stephano and Trinculo are driven in; Prospero acknowledges Caliban as his. Prospero invites everyone to his cell to hear his story, and sets Ariel free.","key":["Prompted by Ariel, Prospero chooses forgiveness over revenge.","He gives up his magic before returning to ordinary life in Milan.","Not everyone is reconciled: Antonio stays silent."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Prospero say is the 'rarer action'?","o":["Vengeance on his enemies","Virtue (forgiveness)","Magic that can raise the dead","Silence and solitude"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What are Ferdinand and Miranda doing when they are revealed?","o":["Dancing","Playing chess","Sleeping","Praying"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Prospero promise to do with his book?","o":["Burn it","Drown it","Give it to Ariel","Bury it in Milan"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"Who makes Prospero choose mercy?","o":["Ariel, who says he would pity them","Miranda, who begs him to forgive","Gonzalo, who weeps for his King","Ferdinand, who asks for his father"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Prospero say of Caliban at the end?","o":["He must stay on the island as a slave","He will go to Milan as Prospero's fool","This thing of darkness I acknowledge mine","He is a devil who can never be taught"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Alone on stage, Prospero speaks directly to the audience. His magic is gone and he is as weak as any man. He asks to be released from the island by their applause, and for their 'indulgence' to pardon him, as they would be pardoned themselves.","key":["The audience now holds the power Prospero has given up.","Applause and prayer replace magic.","Many readers hear Shakespeare's own farewell to the stage."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Prospero ask the audience for?","o":["Money for the players","Applause and pardon","Silence while he leaves the stage","A ship to sail home to Milan"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What has Prospero lost?","o":["His only daughter Miranda","His charms (magic)","His dukedom of Milan for ever","His memory of the past"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Prospero say will happen without the audience's help?","o":["His project, which was to please, will fail","He will have to stay on the stage until dawn","His daughter will never reach Naples to marry","The ship will sink again on the journey home"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Prospero say will fill his sails?","o":["The wind that Ariel sends him","The audience's gentle breath","The prayers of all the sailors","The spells he has left behind"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Prospero say his ending will be without prayer?","o":["Death","Despair","Silence","Exile"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"}]}},"notes":{"15":"The storm ignores rank: the first of many questions about who really has power.","19":"Gonzalo jokes even in danger. The proverb 'born to be hanged, never drowned' runs through the play.","47":"Miranda's compassion is her first trait, and it anticipates the pity that turns Prospero to mercy.","48":"The storm is a performance: terror without injury.","54":"Prospero's cloak is his magic; taking it off makes him a father telling a story.","60":"A famous image of memory as a deep, dark gulf.","68":"Prospero admits he neglected government for books. Some readers see him as partly to blame.","70":"Antonio grew like ivy round a tree, smothering it.","77":"Miranda's quick answer: one family can produce both good and wicked children.","82":"They were set adrift to die without anyone seeing a murder.","84":"The infant Miranda's smile kept her father going.","86":"Gonzalo's kindness provided the books that became Prospero's power.","92":"Prospero believes his fortunes depend on acting now, under a lucky star.","100":"Ferdinand's cry as he leaps overboard. Often quoted out of context.","106":"The Bermudas: a link to the 1609 shipwreck reports that inspired the play.","110":"Ariel's first complaint introduces the theme of service and freedom.","119":"Ariel, invisible, sets the plotters against each other with two words.","125":"Ariel's imprisonment in the tree. Prospero threatens the same punishment in an oak.","150":"Prospero taught Caliban the names of the sun and moon: an echo of Genesis.","152":"Caliban shows no shame about his attempted rape, which complicates sympathy for him.","154":"Caliban turns his education against his teacher.","164":"Ariel's song turns death into transformation: a 'sea-change'.","178":"Love at first sight, as Prospero planned.","181":"Prospero makes the courtship difficult so Ferdinand will value Miranda.","192":"Prospero teases Miranda: she has nothing to compare Ferdinand with.","195":"Love makes captivity bearable: the first of several kinds of 'prison' in the play.","204":"Gonzalo's practical comfort is wise but badly timed.","206":"Sebastian and Antonio's mockery begins: their wit has no kindness in it.","288":"Gonzalo's ideal commonwealth draws on Montaigne's essay on cannibals.","295":"Antonio spots the contradiction: Gonzalo wants no rulers but would be king.","324":"Antonio tempts Sebastian just as he was once tempted to take Milan.","342":"Antonio uses theatre language: everything so far is just the introduction to their crime.","350":"Antonio feels no conscience at all.","356":"Ariel's song in Gonzalo's ear stops the murder.","378":"Native people taken from the Americas were displayed in England as curiosities.","397":"Caliban swaps Prospero for Stephano, a far worse master.","423":"Caliban celebrates 'freedom' by finding a new master.","428":"Ferdinand's labour feels light because he thinks of Miranda.","447":"Miranda proposes: strikingly bold for a woman in a play of this time.","499":"Caliban knows that Prospero's power comes from his books.","519":"Caliban's lyrical side: he hears the island's music more deeply than anyone.","551":"Ariel as harpy delivers judgment like a prophet.","553":"Repentance and a changed life are the only escape.","558":"Alonso hears his own guilt in the thunder.","563":"Gonzalo sees guilt finally taking effect on the three sinners.","570":"Prospero insists on chastity before marriage: the masque celebrates lawful, fruitful love.","610":"Perhaps the play's most famous line: human life is as fleeting as a dream.","623":"The nature versus nurture debate in one line.","640":"Caliban, focused on the murder, sees through the bait better than his masters.","669":"Ariel, a spirit, teaches his master compassion.","670":"The turning point of the play: Prospero chooses mercy.","673":"Prospero renounces his power before returning to the human world.","708":"Miranda's wonder; Prospero's reply ''Tis new to thee' adds irony.","736":"Prospero accepts responsibility for Caliban, whatever that means.","748":"Caliban's last words suggest he can learn and change.","753":"Prospero looks ahead to old age and death.","758":"The Epilogue hands the power to the audience."},"gloss":{"boatswain":"ship's officer in charge of the deck crew","yarely":"quickly, nimbly","yare":"quick, ready","bestir":"get moving","topsail":"the sail above the main sail","boson":"boatswain","roarers":"roaring waves","hap":"happen","gallows":"fit for hanging","topmast":"the upper part of a ship's mast","maincourse":"mainsail","pox":"plague (a curse)","whoreson":"son of a whore (insult)","unstanched":"unstopped, leaky","courses":"main sails","furlongs":"eighth-parts of a mile","furze":"gorse, a prickly shrub","heath":"heather","fain":"gladly","welkin":"sky","fraughting":"forming the cargo","perdition":"ruin","betid":"happened","bootless":"useless","inquisition":"inquiry","abysm":"abyss","teen":"grief, trouble","perfidious":"treacherous","signories":"states ruled by lords","liberal arts":"learning","rapt":"absorbed, carried away","trash":"hold back (a hound)","verdure":"green life, vigour","sans":"without","prerogative":"royal privilege","confederates":"allies, accomplices","coronet":"small crown worn by a duke","inveterate":"long-standing","extirpate":"root out, remove","levied":"raised (an army)","wench":"girl (affectionate here)","bark":"small ship","cherubin":"angel","holp":"helped","steaded":"been useful","prescience":"foreknowledge","zenith":"highest point of fortune","auspicious":"favourable","dulness":"drowsiness","quality":"fellow spirits","beak":"prow, front of the ship","waist":"middle of the ship","boresprit":"bowsprit, the spar at the ship's front","precursors":"forerunners","Neptune":"Roman god of the sea","trident":"three-pronged spear","coil":"uproar","brine":"salt water","glasses":"hours (measured by hourglass)","bate":"reduce, cut off","Argier":"Algiers","hests":"commands","unmitigable":"which could not be calmed","whelp":"pup","urchins":"goblins in hedgehog shape","fen":"marsh","dam":"mother","sty":"pen like a pig","stripes":"lashes of a whip","abhorred":"hateful","quaint":"clever, dainty","featly":"nimbly","whist":"hushed","sprites":"spirits","burden":"chorus, refrain","Chanticleer":"the rooster","fathom":"six feet deep","knell":"funeral bell","ditty":"song","owes":"owns","advance":"raise","canker":"a worm that eats flowers","vouchsafe":"grant","surety":"guarantee","nerves":"sinews","ward":"defensive posture","visitor":"one who comforts the sick","temperance":"mild climate (also a girl's name)","tawny":"brown","paragon":"model of perfection","Dido":"Queen of Carthage, deserted by Aeneas","Aeneas":"Trojan hero who left Dido","doublet":"close-fitting jacket","importun'd":"begged","plantation":"colonising","docks":"weeds","mallows":"weeds","contraries":"opposites","traffic":"trade","magistrate":"official","tilth":"ploughed land","bourn":"boundary","foison":"plenty","engine":"weapon of war","bat-fowling":"catching birds at night with lights","sudden":"quick","drollery":"puppet show","phoenix":"mythical bird reborn from its ashes","viands":"food","dewlapp'd":"with folds of skin at the throat","harpy":"monster with a woman's face and a bird's wings and claws","dowle":"tiny feather","massy":"heavy","bereft":"robbed","ecstasy":"madness","plummet":"weight on a line for measuring depth","gaberdine":"cloak","Poor-John":"dried salted fish","doit":"tiny coin","moon-calf":"monster, misshapen creature","ague":"fever","neat's-leather":"cowhide","siege":"excrement","sack":"sweet white wine, sherry","butt":"large barrel","trenchering":"wooden plates","firing":"firewood","marmozet":"small monkey","filberts":"hazelnuts","scamels":"probably seabirds or shellfish","pig-nuts":"edible roots","crabs":"crab apples","high-day":"hooray","injunction":"order","executor":"doer, worker","bondage":"slavery","bulk":"size","nonpareil":"one without equal","brave":"fine, splendid","jocund":"merry","troll":"sing heartily","catch":"round song","while-ere":"a short while ago","tabor":"small drum","taborer":"drummer","standard":"flag-bearer","deboshed":"debauched, drunken","natural":"idiot","pied":"multicoloured (like a jester's costume)","patch":"fool","murrain":"cattle plague","wezand":"windpipe","paunch":"stab in the belly","sot":"fool","brained":"with brains like","stock-fish":"dried cod (beaten before cooking)","by'r lakin":"by Our Lady","forth-rights":"straight paths","meanders":"winding paths","attach'd":"seized","frustrate":"frustrated","throughly":"thoroughly","unicorns":"mythical horned horses","certes":"certainly","muse":"wonder at","flat-long":"with the flat of the sword","austerely":"harshly","sanctimonious":"holy","aspersion":"sprinkling (of blessing)","Hymen":"god of marriage","Phoebus":"the sun god","abstemious":"self-restrained","corollary":"one extra","pertly":"briskly","leas":"meadows","vetches":"plants grown for fodder","stover":"winter fodder","pioned":"dug","broom":"a yellow-flowered shrub","Dis":"Pluto, god of the underworld","Paphos":"home of Venus in Cyprus","minion":"darling, lover","garners":"granaries","Naiads":"water nymphs","sedg'd":"made of reeds","sicklemen":"harvesters","rack":"wisp of cloud","inherit":"occupy, own","pageant":"show","varlets":"rogues","unback'd":"never ridden","goss":"gorse","trumpery":"showy rubbish","stale":"bait, decoy","frippery":"second-hand clothes shop","jerkin":"leather jacket","hogshead":"large barrel","barnacles":"barnacle geese","dropsy":"illness with swelling","pard":"leopard","cat o' mountain":"wildcat","line-grove":"grove of lime trees","eaves":"edge of a roof","demi-puppets":"tiny spirits","ringlets":"fairy rings","curfew":"evening bell","azur'd":"blue","rifted":"split","promontory":"headland","spurs":"roots","abjure":"renounce","discase":"undress","rapier":"sword","cowslip":"a small yellow flower","subtleties":"illusions, fancy dishes","brace":"pair","perforce":"necessarily","requite":"repay","inly":"inwardly","chalk'd":"marked out","trim":"condition","cap'ring":"dancing with joy","moping":"dazed","tricksy":"clever","rectify":"correct","infest":"trouble","Coragio":"courage!","bully-monster":"fine monster","badges":"uniforms showing whose servant one is","reeling-ripe":"staggering drunk","gilded":"flushed (with drink)","pickle":"mess; also pickling brine","fly-blowing":"flies laying eggs in meat","sirrah":"term used to inferiors","nuptial":"wedding","solemnized":"celebrated","indulgence":"kindness, pardon (and applause)","Setebos":"a god of the Patagonians","Milan":"the Duke of Milan (by title)","Naples":"the King of Naples (by title)","art":"magic","charms":"spells","prithee":"please","hag-seed":"child of a witch","hither":"here","thence":"from there","anon":"soon","ere":"before","afeard":"afraid","yond":"over there"}};
const PARTS={"47":[[0,8,"If your magic, my dearest father, has made the wild waters roar like this, calm them. It looks as if the sky would pour down stinking tar, except that the sea, rising up to the face of the heavens, puts out its fire. Oh, I have suffered with those I saw suffering! A fine ship, which surely had some noble people in it, smashed to pieces. Oh, their cry hit my very heart! Poor souls, they died."],[9,12,"If I had been a powerful god, I would have sunk the sea into the earth before it could swallow the good ship like that, with all the souls it carried."]],"72":[[0,0,"Please listen to me."],[1,18,"I neglected worldly matters and gave myself to solitude and the improvement of my mind, with studies that, if they hadn't kept me so withdrawn, would have been worth more than anything the people value. This woke an evil nature in my treacherous brother. My trust, like a good parent with a bad child, produced in him a falseness as great as my trust, which truly had no limit, a confidence without bounds. Made a lord in this way, not only with my income but with whatever else my power could demand, he was like a man who tells a lie so often that he cheats his own memory into believing it. He came to believe that he really was the Duke, because he was standing in for me and acting out the outward show of royalty with all its privileges. So his ambition grew. Do you hear me?"]],"78":[[0,2,"Now hear the terms. This King of Naples, a long-standing enemy of mine, listened to my brother's request."],[3,12,"It was that, in return for homage and who knows how much tribute, the King should immediately throw me and my family out of the dukedom and give fair Milan, with all its honours, to my brother. So a treacherous army was raised, and one midnight, chosen for the purpose, Antonio opened the gates of Milan, and in the dead of darkness his agents hurried me and you, crying, out of the city."]],"82":[[0,4,"A good question, girl: my story invites it. My dear, they didn't dare, because my people loved me so much, and they didn't want to leave such a bloody stain on their business. Instead they painted over their foul aims with fairer colours."],[5,12,"In short, they hurried us onto a ship and carried us some miles out to sea, where they had got ready a rotten wreck of a boat, with no rigging, ropes, sail or mast: even the rats had instinctively left it. There they put us, to cry to the sea, which roared back at us, and to sigh to the winds, whose pity, sighing back, only did us loving harm."]],"92":[[0,7,"Know this much. By a very strange accident, generous Fortune, now my friend, has brought my enemies to this shore. And I can see by my foreknowledge that my highest point depends on a very lucky star: if I don't seize its influence now but let it pass, my fortunes will sink for ever after. No more questions now."],[8,9,"You're drowsy. It's a good sleepiness, so give in to it. I know you can't help it."]],"106":[[0,3,"The King's ship is safe in harbour, in the deep bay where you once called me up at midnight to fetch dew from the always stormy Bermudas. She's hidden there."],[4,11,"The sailors are all stowed below deck; with a spell added to their exhaustion I have left them asleep. As for the rest of the fleet, which I scattered, they have all met up again and are on the Mediterranean sea, sailing sadly home to Naples, believing they saw the King's ship wrecked and the King himself die."]],"125":[[0,2,"This hag with the blue-ringed eyes was brought here pregnant and left here by the sailors. You, my slave, as you describe yourself, were her servant then."],[3,15,"And because you were a spirit too delicate to carry out her coarse and hateful orders, and refused her great commands, she imprisoned you, with the help of her stronger servants and in her most relentless rage, in a split pine tree. Imprisoned in that crack you stayed in pain for twelve years. During that time she died and left you there, where you groaned as fast as the paddles of a mill wheel strike the water. At that time this island had no human form to honour it, except for the son she gave birth to here, a freckled pup born of a hag."]],"150":[[0,8,"I have to eat my dinner. This island is mine, through my mother Sycorax, and you're taking it from me. When you first came, you stroked me and made a fuss of me. You gave me water with berries in it, and taught me the names of the bigger light and the smaller one that burn by day and night. And then I loved you, and showed you all the features of the island: the fresh springs, the salt pits, the barren places and the fertile ones."],[9,14,"Curse me for doing it! May all the spells of Sycorax, toads, beetles, bats, fall on you! I am the only subject you have, and I used to be my own king. Now you pen me like a pig in this hard rock, while you keep the rest of the island from me."]],"161":[[0,5,"Come to these yellow sands, and then join hands. When you have bowed and kissed, and the wild waves are hushed, dance nimbly here and there, and, sweet spirits, sing the chorus."],[6,12,"Listen, listen! The watchdogs bark. Listen, listen! I hear the call of the strutting rooster."]],"164":[[0,5,"Your father lies thirty feet deep. His bones have turned to coral, and those are pearls that were his eyes. Nothing of him that might decay but has been changed by the sea into something rich and strange."],[6,8,"Sea nymphs ring his funeral bell every hour. Listen! Now I hear them: ding-dong, bell."]],"181":[[0,3,"Wait, sir, one more word. They're both in each other's power. But I must make this quick business difficult, in case winning too easily makes the prize seem cheap. One more word."],[4,8,"I order you to listen to me. You are claiming a title that isn't yours, and you've come to this island as a spy, to take it from me, its lord."]],"195":[[0,1,"So they are. My spirits are all tied up, as if in a dream."],[2,8,"My father's loss, the weakness I feel, the wreck of all my friends, and the threats of this man who has overpowered me are all light burdens, if only I may see this girl through my prison window once a day. Let free men use every other corner of the earth: I'll have space enough in such a prison."]],"293":[[0,5,"In my state I would do everything the opposite way from usual. I would allow no kind of trade, no title of magistrate. Reading and writing would be unknown. There would be no riches, no poverty and no servants. No contracts, no inheritance, no boundaries or fences, no ploughed fields or vineyards."],[6,9,"No use of metal, grain, wine or oil. No jobs: all men idle, all of them, and women too, but innocent and pure. No ruler..."]],"350":[[0,6,"Yes, sir, where is that? If it were a chilblain, it would make me wear a slipper. But I don't feel this god in my heart. If twenty consciences stood between me and Milan, they could turn to sugar and melt before they bothered me! Here lies your brother, no better than the earth he lies on, if he were what he now looks like, that is, dead."],[7,12,"With this obedient blade, three inches of it, I can put him to bed for ever, while you, doing the same, could close the eyes for good of this old leftover, this Sir Caution, so he can't condemn what we do. As for the rest, they'll take a hint as easily as a cat laps milk."],[13,14,"They'll say the time is right for any business we tell them suits the hour."]],"356":[[0,2,"My master knows by his magic the danger that you, his friend, are in, and sends me to keep you alive, or else his plan will fail."],[3,9,"While you lie here snoring, wide-eyed conspiracy is seizing its chance. If you care about your life, shake off sleep and beware. Wake up! Wake up!"]],"375":[[0,7,"May all the diseases the sun sucks up from bogs, swamps and marshes fall on Prospero and make him sick inch by inch! His spirits can hear me, and yet I have to curse. But they won't pinch me, scare me with goblins, throw me in the mud, or lead me off my path in the dark like a will-o'-the-wisp, unless he tells them to. But they're set on me for every little thing."],[8,13,"Sometimes they're like apes that pull faces and chatter at me and then bite me; then like hedgehogs that lie curled up in my barefoot path and stick their prickles up as I step. Sometimes I'm all wrapped in snakes, who hiss me mad with their forked tongues."]],"378":[[0,3,"There isn't a bush or shrub here to keep off any weather at all, and another storm is brewing: I can hear it singing in the wind. That black cloud over there, that huge one, looks like a filthy leather wine jug about to spill its drink."],[4,8,"If it thunders like it did before, I don't know where to hide my head. That cloud is bound to pour down by the bucketful. What have we here? A man or a fish? Dead or alive? A fish: he smells like a fish, a very old, fishy smell, a sort of stale dried cod."],[9,12,"A strange fish! If I were in England now, as I once was, and had a painting of this fish on a sign, every fool out on a holiday would pay a silver coin to see it. This monster would make a man's fortune there; any strange beast makes a man's fortune there."],[13,17,"They won't give a penny to help a crippled beggar, but they'll pay ten to see a dead Indian. He has legs like a man, and his fins are like arms! Warm, I swear! Now I'll let my opinion go and hold it no longer: this is no fish but an islander who has recently been struck by a thunderbolt."],[18,21,"Oh no, the storm has come again! My best plan is to crawl under his cloak; there's no other shelter around here. Misery introduces a man to strange bedfellows. I'll hide here till the last of the storm has passed."]],"380":[[0,3,"I'll go to sea no more, no more; here I'll die ashore. This is a very lousy tune to sing at a man's funeral. Well, here's my comfort."],[4,10,"The captain, the deck-hand, the boatswain and I, the gunner and his mate, loved Mall, Meg, Marian and Margery, but none of us cared for Kate. For she had a sharp tongue and would shout at a sailor, 'Go hang!"],[11,15,"She didn't like the smell of tar or pitch, but a tailor could scratch her wherever she itched. So off to sea, boys, and let her go hang! This is a lousy tune too, but here's my comfort."]],"428":[[0,12,"Some pastimes are hard work, but the pleasure in them makes up for the effort. Some kinds of lowly work can be done nobly, and many humble tasks lead to rich rewards. This menial job of mine would be as heavy as it is hateful to me, but the lady I serve brings dead things to life and turns my labours into pleasures. Oh, she is ten times kinder than her father is bad-tempered, and he is made of harshness. I have to move some thousands of these logs and pile them up, under a strict order. My sweet lady weeps when she sees me work and says such lowly work never had such a noble worker."],[13,14,"I'm forgetting my job, but these sweet thoughts refresh my labours, and I'm busiest when I'm least working."]],"439":[[0,9,"Admired Miranda! Truly the height of admiration, worth more than the most precious things in the world! I have looked on many ladies with the greatest admiration, and many times the music of their voices has captured my too eager ear. I have liked different women for different virtues, but never one so wholeheartedly that some flaw in her didn't fight with her finest quality and spoil it."],[10,11,"But you, oh you, so perfect and without equal, are made of the best of every creature."]],"447":[[0,4,"Because I'm unworthy, and don't dare offer what I want to give, much less take what I'll die without. But this is nonsense: the more my love tries to hide itself, the bigger it shows. Away, shy cleverness!"],[5,9,"Guide me, plain and holy innocence! I am your wife, if you'll marry me. If not, I'll die a virgin devoted to you. You may refuse to make me your partner, but I'll be your servant, whether you want me or not."]],"499":[[0,8,"Well, as I told you, it's his habit to sleep in the afternoon. There you can smash his brains in, after first seizing his books. Or batter his skull with a log, or stab him in the belly with a stake, or cut his windpipe with your knife. Remember to take his books first, because without them he's just a fool like me and hasn't a single spirit to command. They all hate him as deeply as I do. Just burn his books."],[9,10,"He has fine furnishings, as he calls them, to decorate a house with when he has one."],[11,16,"And what you should think about most is his daughter's beauty. He himself calls her a wonder without equal. I've never seen a woman except my mother Sycorax and her, but she is as far beyond Sycorax as the greatest is beyond the least."]],"519":[[0,1,"Don't be afraid. The island is full of noises, sounds and sweet tunes that give delight and do no harm."],[2,8,"Sometimes a thousand twanging instruments hum around my ears, and sometimes voices that, if I had just woken from a long sleep, would make me sleep again. And then, while dreaming, I thought the clouds would open and show riches ready to drop on me, so that when I woke I cried to dream again."]],"553":[[0,11,"And it is with this kind of courage that men hang and drown themselves. You fools! My fellows and I are agents of fate. The metal your swords are made of could as easily wound the loud winds, or kill the waters that always close up again with stabs they laugh at, as cut one fibre of my feathers. My fellow agents are just as invulnerable. Even if you could hurt us, your swords are now too heavy for your strength, and you can't lift them. But remember, because that's my business with you, that you three overthrew good Prospero in Milan."],[12,23,"You put him and his innocent child out on the sea, which has now paid you back. For that foul deed the heavenly powers, which delay but do not forget, have stirred up the seas and shores, and every creature, against your peace. Alonso, they have taken your son from you, and through me they declare that a slow ruin, worse than any sudden death, will follow you step by step wherever you go. The only protection from their anger, which will otherwise fall on your heads on this desolate island, is true repentance and a pure life from now on."]],"555":[[0,7,"You played the part of this harpy splendidly, my Ariel. You had a devouring grace. You left out nothing of what I told you to say. And my lesser spirits have played their different parts with lifelike skill and wonderful care. My powerful spells are working, and these enemies of mine are all tied up in their madness. Now they're in my power."],[8,10,"I'll leave them in these fits while I visit young Ferdinand, whom they think is drowned, and his and my beloved darling."]],"568":[[0,7,"If I have punished you too harshly, your reward makes up for it, because I have given you here a third of my own life, or rather the thing I live for. I hand her over to you once again. All your hardships were only my tests of your love, and you have passed them wonderfully. Here, before heaven, I confirm this rich gift of mine."],[8,10,"Oh Ferdinand, don't smile at me for boasting about her, because you'll find she outruns all praise and leaves it limping behind her."]],"592":[[0,6,"Don't be afraid of meeting her. I met the goddess cutting through the clouds towards Paphos, with her son, in her chariot drawn by doves. They planned to cast some lustful spell on this man and girl, who have vowed that no wedding-night rites will be paid until the torch of the marriage god is lit."],[7,10,"But it was useless. Mars's hot lover has gone back home. Her hot-tempered son has broken his arrows, swears he'll shoot no more but play with sparrows, and be a plain boy."]],"610":[[0,13,"You look upset, my son, as if you were alarmed. Cheer up, sir. Our entertainment is over now. These actors of ours, as I told you, were all spirits and have melted into air, into thin air. And, like the baseless structure of this vision, the cloud-topped towers, the splendid palaces, the solemn temples, the great globe itself, yes, and everyone who lives on it, will dissolve, and, just as this insubstantial show has faded, leave not a wisp of cloud behind. We are made of the same stuff as dreams, and our little life is surrounded by a sleep. Sir, I am troubled. Bear with my weakness: my old brain is disturbed."],[14,17,"Don't be worried by my frailty. If you like, go into my cave and rest there. I'll walk around a little to calm my racing mind."]],"619":[[0,4,"I told you, sir, they were red-hot with drinking, so full of courage that they struck the air for breathing in their faces and beat the ground for kissing their feet, yet always heading towards their plan."],[5,13,"Then I beat my drum, and at that, like unbroken colts, they pricked up their ears, opened their eyes wide and lifted their noses as if they could smell music. I charmed their ears so that they followed my mooing like calves, through toothed briars, sharp gorse, prickly furze and thorns, which pierced their tender shins. At last I left them in the scum-covered pond beyond your cave, dancing there up to their chins, so that the stinking pool smelled worse than their feet."]],"667":[[0,3,"Imprisoned together just as you ordered, exactly as you left them. They're all prisoners, sir, in the grove of lime trees that shelters your cave from the weather."],[4,8,"They can't move until you release them. The King, his brother and yours are all three out of their minds, and the rest are mourning over them, brimming with sorrow and alarm, but most of all the one you called, sir, 'the good old lord, Gonzalo'."],[9,12,"His tears run down his beard like winter rain from a thatched roof. Your spell works on them so strongly that if you saw them now, your feelings would soften."]],"670":[[0,4,"And mine will. If you, who are only air, have a sense and feeling of their sufferings, shouldn't I, one of their own kind, who feel everything as sharply and suffer as they do, be more kindly moved than you?"],[5,12,"Although their great wrongs have cut me to the quick, I take the side of my nobler reason against my fury. It is a rarer act to forgive than to take revenge. Since they are sorry, my purpose goes not one frown further. Go and release them, Ariel. I'll break my spells and restore their senses, and they'll be themselves again."]],"676":[[0,3,"May a solemn tune, the best comfort for a disturbed mind, cure your brains, which are useless now and boiling inside your skull! Stand there, because you are frozen by a spell."],[4,10,"Holy Gonzalo, honourable man, my eyes, in sympathy with the sight of yours, shed tears to keep them company. The spell is dissolving fast, and as the morning creeps up on the night and melts the darkness, so their returning senses begin to chase away the fog of confusion that covers their clearer reason."],[11,26,"Oh, good Gonzalo, my true saviour and a loyal servant to the master you follow, I will repay your kindnesses in full, in words and deeds. Alonso, you treated me and my daughter most cruelly. Your brother helped you do it. You're tormented for it now, Sebastian. And you, my own flesh and blood, my brother, who welcomed ambition and drove out pity and natural feeling, who, with Sebastian (whose inner torments are therefore the strongest), would have killed your king here: I forgive you, unnatural as you are! Their understanding is starting to rise, and the coming tide will soon fill the shores of their reason, which now lie foul and muddy. Not one of them is yet looking at me or would recognise me. Ariel, fetch me the hat and sword from my cave."]],"686":[[0,6,"Whether you are him or not, or some enchanted thing to deceive me, as I have been deceived lately, I don't know. Your pulse beats like flesh and blood, and since I saw you, the disorder of my mind, which I'm afraid held me in madness, has got better. This must need, if it is real at all, a very strange story."],[7,9,"I give back your dukedom and beg you to pardon my wrongs. But how can Prospero be alive and be here?"]],"699":[[0,14,"In this last storm. I can see that these lords are so amazed at this meeting that they swallow their reason and hardly believe that their eyes are telling the truth or that their words are natural breath. But however much you have been shaken out of your senses, know for certain that I am Prospero, the very duke who was thrown out of Milan, and who, most strangely, landed on this shore where you were wrecked, to be its lord. No more of this yet, because it's a story to be told day by day, not one for over breakfast, nor suitable for this first meeting. Welcome, sir. This cave is my court. Here I have few servants, and no subjects outside. Please, look in."],[15,18,"Since you have given me back my dukedom, I'll repay you with something just as good, or at least show you a wonder that will please you as much as my dukedom pleases me."]],"711":[[0,1,"Sir, she is mortal, but by immortal Providence she is mine."],[2,8,"I chose her when I couldn't ask my father for his advice, and didn't think I had a father. She is the daughter of this famous Duke of Milan, whose fame I have heard of so often but whom I never saw before. From him I have received a second life, and this lady makes him a second father to me."]],"725":[[0,6,"If I thought I was wide awake, sir, I'd try to tell you. We were fast asleep and, we don't know how, all shut up below deck. Just now we were woken by strange and different noises of roaring, shrieking, howling, jangling chains and more kinds of sounds, all horrible, and at once we were free."],[7,11,"There we saw our royal, good and gallant ship in perfect condition, and our captain dancing with joy to see her. In an instant, if you please, as if in a dream, we were separated from the others and brought here in a daze."]],"729":[[0,9,"Sir, my lord, don't trouble your mind by hammering on the strangeness of this business. At a chosen time, which will be soon, I'll explain to you in private every one of these events, in a way that will seem believable to you. Until then, be cheerful and think well of everything. Come here, spirit. Set Caliban and his companions free. Undo the spell. How are you, my gracious sir?"],[10,11,"There are still a few odd fellows of your company missing whom you've forgotten."]],"736":[[0,4,"Just look at these men's uniforms, my lords, and then say if they're honest. This misshapen rogue's mother was a witch, and such a strong one that she could control the moon, make tides flow and ebb, and use the moon's power without its authority."],[5,9,"These three have robbed me, and this half-devil (for he's a bastard one) had plotted with them to take my life. Two of these fellows you must know and claim as yours. This thing of darkness I admit is mine."]],"753":[[0,9,"Sir, I invite your Highness and your followers to my poor cave, where you will rest for this one night. I'll spend part of it with talk that I'm sure will make it pass quickly: the story of my life and all the events that have happened since I came to this island. And in the morning I'll take you to your ship and so to Naples, where I hope to see the wedding of these dearly loved young people celebrated."],[10,11,"And from there I'll retire to my Milan, where every third thought will be of my grave."]],"758":[[0,9,"Now my spells are all overthrown, and what strength I have is my own, which is very weak. Now it's true that I must either be kept here by you or sent to Naples. Since I have got my dukedom back and pardoned the man who deceived me, don't let me stay on this bare island by your spell, but release me from my bonds with the help of your good hands."],[10,17,"Your kind breath must fill my sails, or else my plan fails, which was to please you. Now I have no spirits to command and no magic to enchant, and my ending will be despair unless I am helped by prayer, which pierces so deeply that it storms mercy itself and frees all faults."],[18,19,"As you would be pardoned for your sins, let your kindness set me free."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"power":["Power and authority","Who rules, by what right, and what happens when rank meets the storm."],"magic":["Magic and art","Prospero's 'art' as power, as learning and as theatre; and the choice to give it up."],"freedom":["Freedom and service","Ariel, Caliban, Ferdinand and Prospero himself: masters, servants and prisoners."],"forgive":["Revenge and forgiveness","Prospero has his enemies in his power. Will he punish them or pardon them?"],"colony":["Colonisation and the island","Who owns the island? Caliban, Prospero, and the European 'brave new world'."],"nature":["Nature and nurture","Can education and care change a nature? Caliban, Miranda, Antonio."],"illusion":["Illusion and dreams","Storms that harm no one, vanishing banquets, a masque, and life itself 'such stuff as dreams are made on'."],"love":["Love and marriage","Ferdinand and Miranda's love at first sight, tested and blessed."],"betrayal":["Betrayal and guilt","Brothers who betray brothers, and the guilt that finally bites."]},"quotes":[{"id":"15","who":"BOATSWAIN","text":"When the sea is. Hence! What cares these roarers for the name of king? To cabin! silence! Trouble","themes":["power"],"note":"The boatswain: nature ignores titles."},{"id":"47","who":"MIRANDA","text":"Dashes the fire out. O! I have suffered With those that I saw suffer: a brave vessel,","themes":["forgive","love"],"note":"Miranda's compassion for strangers."},{"id":"60","who":"PROSPERO","text":"In the dark backward and abysm of time?","themes":["illusion"],"note":"Memory as a dark gulf of the past."},{"id":"77","who":"MIRANDA","text":"Good wombs have borne bad sons.","themes":["nature","betrayal"],"note":"Miranda on Antonio: family does not guarantee goodness."},{"id":"70","who":"PROSPERO","text":"The ivy which had hid my princely trunk,","themes":["betrayal","power"],"note":"Antonio smothered Prospero like ivy round a tree."},{"id":"68","who":"PROSPERO","text":"And rapt in secret studies. Thy false uncle—","themes":["magic","power"],"note":"Prospero neglected his dukedom for his books."},{"id":"74","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Absolute Milan. Me, poor man—my library Was dukedom large enough: of temporal royalties","themes":["magic","power"],"note":"Prospero's books mattered more to him than ruling."},{"id":"86","who":"PROSPERO","text":"I prize above my dukedom.","themes":["magic"],"note":"The books Gonzalo saved are worth more than Milan to Prospero."},{"id":"100","who":"ARIEL","text":"Was the first man that leapt; cried 'Hell is empty, And all the devils are here.'","themes":["illusion"],"note":"Ferdinand's terror in Ariel's storm."},{"id":"110","who":"ARIEL","text":"Let me remember thee what thou hast promis'd,","themes":["freedom"],"note":"Ariel asks for his promised liberty."},{"id":"119","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Thou liest, malignant thing! Hast thou forgot","themes":["power","freedom"],"note":"Prospero's anger at Ariel's request."},{"id":"129","who":"PROSPERO","text":"If thou more murmur'st, I will rend an oak And peg thee in his knotty entrails till","themes":["power","freedom"],"note":"Prospero threatens Ariel with Sycorax's punishment."},{"id":"150","who":"CALIBAN","text":"This island's mine, by Sycorax my mother,","themes":["colony","freedom"],"note":"Caliban's claim to the island."},{"id":"150","who":"CALIBAN","text":"Which first was mine own king; and here you sty me","themes":["colony","power"],"note":"Caliban was once his own ruler."},{"id":"151","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Filth as thou art, with human care, and lodg'd thee","themes":["nature","colony"],"note":"Prospero claims he treated Caliban kindly until the attack on Miranda."},{"id":"152","who":"CALIBAN","text":"Thou didst prevent me; I had peopl'd else This isle with Calibans.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Caliban shows no remorse."},{"id":"153","who":"PROSPERO","text":"A thing most brutish, I endow'd thy purposes With words that made them known: but thy vile race,","themes":["colony","nature"],"note":"Prospero taught Caliban language."},{"id":"154","who":"CALIBAN","text":"You taught me language, and my profit on't Is, I know how to curse: the red plague rid you,","themes":["colony","freedom"],"note":"Caliban turns language against his master."},{"id":"156","who":"CALIBAN","text":"I must obey. His art is of such power, It would control my dam's god, Setebos,","themes":["magic","power"],"note":"Caliban obeys only out of fear."},{"id":"164","who":"ARIEL","text":"Full fathom five thy father lies:","themes":["illusion","magic"],"note":"Ariel's song of death as transformation."},{"id":"164","who":"ARIEL","text":"Into something rich and strange.","themes":["illusion"],"note":"The 'sea-change' that runs through the play."},{"id":"178","who":"PROSPERO","text":"They have changed eyes;—delicate Ariel,","themes":["love","magic"],"note":"Love at first sight, as Prospero planned."},{"id":"181","who":"PROSPERO","text":"business I must uneasy make, lest too light winning Make the prize light. One word more:","themes":["love","power"],"note":"Prospero makes the courtship hard."},{"id":"195","who":"FERDINAND","text":"Might I but through my prison once a day Behold this maid: all corners else o' th' earth","themes":["love","freedom"],"note":"Love makes captivity bearable."},{"id":"288","who":"GONZALO","text":"Had I plantation of this isle, my lord,—","themes":["colony","power"],"note":"Gonzalo imagines his ideal state."},{"id":"296","who":"GONZALO","text":"All things in common nature should produce Without sweat or endeavour; treason, felony,","themes":["colony","nature"],"note":"Gonzalo's commonwealth without work or property."},{"id":"295","who":"ANTONIO","text":"The latter end of his commonwealth forgets the beginning.","themes":["power"],"note":"Antonio mocks the contradiction in Gonzalo's dream."},{"id":"324","who":"ANTONIO","text":"My strong imagination sees a crown Dropping upon thy head.","themes":["betrayal","power"],"note":"Antonio tempts Sebastian."},{"id":"342","who":"ANTONIO","text":"Whereof what's past is prologue, what to come","themes":["betrayal","illusion"],"note":"Antonio turns the past into a prelude to new crime."},{"id":"350","who":"ANTONIO","text":"'Twould put me to my slipper: but I feel not This deity in my bosom: twenty consciences","themes":["betrayal"],"note":"Antonio has no conscience."},{"id":"378","who":"TRINCULO","text":"his gaberdine; there is no other shelter hereabout: misery acquaints a man with strange bed-fellows. I will here shroud till the dregs of the","themes":["freedom"],"note":"Trinculo shelters under Caliban's cloak."},{"id":"378","who":"TRINCULO","text":"When they will not give a doit to relieve a lame beggar, they will lay out ten to see a dead Indian. Legg'd like a man, and his fins like","themes":["colony"],"note":"Europeans paying to stare at people from the Americas."},{"id":"397","who":"CALIBAN","text":"That's a brave god, and bears celestial liquor;","themes":["colony","freedom"],"note":"Caliban chooses a new master."},{"id":"423","who":"CALIBAN","text":"Freedom, high-day! high-day, freedom! freedom,","themes":["freedom","colony"],"note":"Caliban's drunken idea of freedom."},{"id":"428","who":"FERDINAND","text":"Upon a sore injunction: my sweet mistress Weeps when she sees me work, and says such baseness","themes":["love","freedom"],"note":"Ferdinand's labour of love."},{"id":"447","who":"MIRANDA","text":"I am your wife, if you will marry me;","themes":["love"],"note":"Miranda proposes."},{"id":"499","who":"CALIBAN","text":"Or cut his wezand with thy knife. Remember First to possess his books; for without them","themes":["magic","power"],"note":"Caliban knows where Prospero's power lies."},{"id":"519","who":"CALIBAN","text":"Be not afeard: the isle is full of noises,","themes":["colony","illusion"],"note":"Caliban's lyrical speech about the island."},{"id":"519","who":"CALIBAN","text":"I cried to dream again.","themes":["illusion","colony"],"note":"Caliban longs for the riches of his dreams."},{"id":"551","who":"ARIEL","text":"You are three men of sin, whom Destiny,","themes":["betrayal","forgive"],"note":"Ariel as harpy accuses the lords."},{"id":"553","who":"ARIEL","text":"Upon your heads,—is nothing but heart-sorrow, And a clear life ensuing.","themes":["forgive","betrayal"],"note":"Only repentance can save them."},{"id":"558","who":"ALONSO","text":"The name of Prosper: it did bass my trespass.","themes":["betrayal"],"note":"Alonso hears his guilt in the thunder."},{"id":"563","who":"GONZALO","text":"All three of them are desperate: their great guilt, Like poison given to work a great time after,","themes":["betrayal"],"note":"Gonzalo sees guilt taking hold."},{"id":"570","who":"PROSPERO","text":"If thou dost break her virgin knot before","themes":["love","power"],"note":"Prospero insists on chastity before marriage."},{"id":"610","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Our revels now are ended. These our actors,","themes":["illusion","magic"],"note":"The masque ends abruptly."},{"id":"610","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Leave not a rack behind. We are such stuff As dreams are made on, and our little life","themes":["illusion"],"note":"Life as a dream surrounded by sleep."},{"id":"623","who":"PROSPERO","text":"A devil, a born devil, on whose nature Nurture can never stick; on whom my pains,","themes":["nature","colony"],"note":"Prospero gives up on Caliban."},{"id":"669","who":"ARIEL","text":"Mine would, sir, were I human.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Ariel prompts Prospero's pity."},{"id":"670","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Do I take part: the rarer action is In virtue than in vengeance: they being penitent,","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Prospero chooses forgiveness."},{"id":"673","who":"PROSPERO","text":"By my so potent art. But this rough magic I here abjure; and, when I have requir'd","themes":["magic"],"note":"Prospero renounces his art."},{"id":"673","who":"PROSPERO","text":"I'll drown my book.","themes":["magic"],"note":"The final break with magic."},{"id":"676","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Would here have kill'd your king; I do forgive thee, Unnatural though thou art! Their understanding","themes":["forgive","betrayal"],"note":"Prospero forgives Antonio."},{"id":"708","who":"MIRANDA","text":"How beauteous mankind is! O brave new world That has such people in't!","themes":["colony","illusion","love"],"note":"Miranda's wonder at mankind."},{"id":"709","who":"PROSPERO","text":"'Tis new to thee.","themes":["illusion"],"note":"Prospero's dry reply."},{"id":"716","who":"GONZALO","text":"In a poor isle; and all of us ourselves, When no man was his own.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Gonzalo: everyone found themselves."},{"id":"736","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Must know and own; this thing of darkness I Acknowledge mine.","themes":["colony","nature"],"note":"Prospero accepts Caliban as his."},{"id":"748","who":"CALIBAN","text":"Ay, that I will; and I'll be wise hereafter, And seek for grace. What a thrice-double ass","themes":["nature","forgive"],"note":"Caliban's change of heart."},{"id":"753","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Every third thought shall be my grave.","themes":["magic"],"note":"Prospero looks towards death."},{"id":"758","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Now my charms are all o'erthrown,","themes":["magic","power"],"note":"The Epilogue: Prospero without his magic."},{"id":"758","who":"PROSPERO","text":"As you from crimes would pardon'd be, Let your indulgence set me free.","themes":["forgive","freedom"],"note":"The play ends asking for pardon and applause."},{"id":"198","who":"PROSPERO","text":"Thou shalt be as free As mountain winds; but then exactly do","themes":["freedom"],"note":"Prospero's promise to Ariel."},{"id":"755","who":"PROSPERO","text":"That is thy charge: then to the elements Be free, and fare thou well!—Please you, draw near.","themes":["freedom","magic"],"note":"Ariel's release."}]};
const EX={"intro":["A storm at sea: a ship carrying Alonso, King of Naples, his son Ferdinand, his brother Sebastian and Antonio, Duke of Milan, seems to be wrecked near a remote island.","On the island live Prospero, the rightful Duke of Milan, overthrown twelve years ago by his brother Antonio, and his daughter Miranda. Prospero is a magician, served by the spirit Ariel and the slave Caliban, and the storm is his doing.","Over a single afternoon Prospero brings everyone into his power. Will he take revenge?"],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The storm: rank means nothing to the waves","id":15},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Prospero tells Miranda how Antonio stole Milan","id":68},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Ariel asks for his freedom","id":110},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Caliban: 'This island's mine'","id":150},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Ferdinand and Miranda fall in love at first sight","id":178},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Gonzalo's ideal commonwealth","id":288},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Antonio tempts Sebastian to kill Alonso","id":324},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Ariel wakes Gonzalo and the murder is stopped","id":356},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Caliban takes Stephano as his god","id":397},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Ferdinand and Miranda promise to marry","id":447},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Caliban's plot to kill Prospero","id":499},{"s":"a3s2","t":"'The isle is full of noises'","id":519},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Ariel as a harpy accuses the 'three men of sin'","id":551},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The betrothal masque","id":587},{"s":"a4s1","t":"'Our revels now are ended'","id":610},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The plotters are hunted by spirit hounds","id":654},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Prospero chooses virtue over vengeance","id":670},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Prospero gives up his magic","id":673},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Reunion: Ferdinand and Miranda at chess","id":701},{"s":"a5s1","t":"'O brave new world'","id":708},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Epilogue: Prospero asks the audience to set him free","id":758}],"irony":{"119":"Ariel's invisible voice makes Trinculo seem to say what he never said.","123":"We and Miranda are hearing the story from Prospero only; we never hear Sycorax's or Ariel's own version.","238":"The courtiers see the same island in opposite ways: each sees what he is.","278":"Sebastian blames Alonso for losing Claribel, while he himself is about to plot his brother's death.","318":"Antonio and Sebastian offer to guard the King they are about to murder.","410":"Caliban repeats exactly what he did for Prospero, and what he says he regrets.","486":"Prospero, who 'sleeps' in the afternoon, knows every word of their plan.","544":"Prospero hints that the lords should save their praise until they see how the show ends.","640":"The 'monster' is the only one of the three who sees the clothes are a trap.","708":"Miranda calls 'brave' the very men who betrayed her father and tried to murder their king.","709":"Prospero knows exactly what these 'goodly creatures' have done.","734":"Sebastian and Antonio, who were happy to kill a king, joke about selling Caliban."},"devices":{"sea":"The sea and sea-change","music":"Music and sound","theatre":"Theatre and performance","nature":"Nature, plants and animals","sleep":"Sleep and dreams","bondage":"Bondage and freedom"},"dev":{"15":[["sea","The roaring waves become loud, rough men who care nothing for kings.","What cares these roarers for the name of king"]],"70":[["nature","Antonio as ivy smothering a tree: a parasite growing on the host.","The ivy which had hid my princely trunk"]],"82":[["sea","The boat is called a dead body: the plan was murder in disguise.","A rotten carcass of a boat"]],"125":[["bondage","Ariel trapped in a tree, like a soul imprisoned.","Into a cloven pine"]],"150":[["bondage","Caliban kept like a pig in a sty.","here you sty me In this hard rock"]],"164":[["sea","Death becomes beautiful change: bones to coral, eyes to pearls.","Full fathom five thy father lies"],["music","A song, not a speech: Ariel's music draws Ferdinand on.","Of his bones are coral made"]],"195":[["bondage","Love turns imprisonment into freedom.","Might I but through my prison once a day"]],"328":[["sleep","Antonio twists the sleeping king into an image of missed chances.","Thou let'st thy fortune sleep"]],"331":[["sea","Sebastian is still water; Antonio will teach him to flow like a tide.","I am standing water"]],"342":[["theatre","Antonio thinks like a playwright: the past is only the opening scene.","what's past is prologue"]],"356":[["sleep","Conspiracy keeps its eyes open while honest men sleep.","Open-ey'd Conspiracy"]],"378":[["nature","Trinculo cannot decide if Caliban is man, fish or beast.","A strange fish"]],"519":[["music","Caliban hears the island as music.","Be not afeard: the isle is full of noises"],["sleep","Caliban's dream of heaven-sent riches.","The clouds methought would open and show riches"]],"551":[["theatre","Ariel performs a harpy in a staged banquet: punishment as theatre.","You are three men of sin"]],"558":[["sea","Alonso imagines his son buried in the sea bed.","Therefore my son i' th' ooze is bedded"]],"610":[["theatre","The masque ends; the language of actors and stages describes the whole world.","Our revels now are ended"],["theatre","A possible pun on Shakespeare's own Globe theatre.","the great globe itself"],["sleep","Life as a short dream surrounded by the sleep of death.","our little life Is rounded with a sleep"]],"619":[["nature","The drunk plotters are like young horses never ridden.","like unback'd colts"]],"676":[["sea","Returning reason is a tide flowing over muddy shores.","the approaching tide Will shortly fill the reasonable shores"]],"680":[["music","Ariel's song of freedom, about to come true.","Where the bee sucks, there suck I"]],"758":[["bondage","In the Epilogue Prospero becomes the prisoner, the audience the magician.","Let me not, Since I have my dukedom got"]]},"stage":{"ship":"The ship","cell":"Prospero's cell","island":"The island","epilogue":"The stage"},"why":{"0":"Sets up the play's questions about power from the first scene.","1":"Miranda's pity prepares for the play's ending in mercy.","3":"Introduces nature against nurture in one line.","4":"Prospero's image of betrayal: the trusted brother as a parasite.","5":"Prospero's own neglect helped Antonio. Is he partly to blame?","9":"Freedom is Ariel's single desire, and drives his relationship with Prospero.","11":"Prospero's power over Ariel is backed by threats, like Sycorax's.","12":"The key line for colonial readings: the island had an owner before Prospero.","14":"Prospero's defence of how he treated Caliban.","17":"Language as a gift and a weapon: Caliban turns his education against his teacher.","19":"The play's most famous song, turning death into transformation.","21":"Love at first sight, but arranged by Prospero.","24":"Gonzalo's dream of a perfect state.","27":"Antonio repeats his own crime by tempting another brother.","29":"Antonio's lack of conscience makes him the one character untouched by the ending.","32":"Caliban trades one master for another.","35":"Miranda's directness: she proposes marriage herself.","37":"Caliban's most beautiful speech complicates the idea of him as a 'monster'.","39":"Ariel's judgment on the lords: the moral centre of Act 3.","40":"Repentance, not punishment, is the goal.","45":"Perhaps the most quoted lines in the play: all life is a passing show.","46":"Prospero's despair about Caliban: nature cannot be changed by nurture.","47":"The spirit teaches the man to pity.","48":"The turning point: forgiveness rather than revenge.","49":"Prospero gives up the magic that made him powerful.","52":"Miranda's wonder, undercut by who she is looking at.","54":"Gonzalo's summary of the play's happy ending.","55":"Prospero accepts some responsibility for Caliban.","56":"Caliban's last words suggest change is possible.","59":"The play ends with a request for forgiveness and applause."},"arc":{"Prospero's anger":[3,7,6,5,4,6,8,9,3,1],"Prospero's control":[9,10,10,9,10,10,10,9,7,0]},"arcnote":"Prospero's power over everyone on the island stays almost total until Act 5; then, as his anger turns to forgiveness, he gives his power away, last of all to the audience.","context":[["Date and first performance","Written about 1610–11. The first recorded performance was at court before King James I on 1 November 1611, and it was played again in 1613 during the celebrations for the marriage of the King's daughter Elizabeth."],["Printing","The Tempest was first printed in the First Folio of 1623, where it is the very first play. The text is unusually clean, with detailed stage directions."],["Shakespeare's last solo play","It is probably the last play Shakespeare wrote alone. Many readers have heard his own farewell to the theatre in Prospero's giving up of his magic, although there is no evidence he meant it that way."],["Late plays (romances)","With Pericles, Cymbeline and The Winter's Tale, it belongs to the 'late plays' or 'romances': stories of loss at sea, lost children, long separations, magic, and reconciliation between generations. The label came from later critics: the First Folio of 1623 prints The Tempest and The Winter's Tale among the comedies, Cymbeline among the tragedies, and leaves Pericles out."],["An original plot","Unusually for Shakespeare, no earlier story is known to supply the plot. He drew details from travel writing, Montaigne and Ovid, but the story itself seems to be his own."],["The Bermuda shipwreck","In 1609 a ship carrying colonists to Virginia, the Sea Venture, was wrecked on the Bermudas. Everyone survived and eventually reached Virginia. Accounts of the wreck, including William Strachey's letter, circulated in London, and details of Ariel's storm echo them."],["The New World and colonisation","England's colony at Jamestown, Virginia, was founded in 1607. Debates about the rights of native peoples, their 'civilising', and the riches of new lands lie behind Caliban, Gonzalo's commonwealth, and Trinculo's 'dead Indian'."],["Montaigne","Gonzalo's ideal commonwealth closely follows John Florio's 1603 translation of Montaigne's essay 'Of Cannibals', which praises the natural life of the peoples of the Americas over European 'civilisation'. Caliban's name may itself be a play on 'cannibal'."],["Ovid","Prospero's speech 'Ye elves of hills' borrows from Medea's invocation in Ovid's Metamorphoses (in Arthur Golding's translation). It is a surprise: Prospero's 'white' magic echoes a famous witch."],["Magic","Educated Renaissance people distinguished learned magic (studying the hidden powers of nature, often through books, like John Dee) from witchcraft, which made pacts with devils. King James had written a book against witches. Prospero insists his art is learned; Sycorax is a witch."],["The unities","Unusually for Shakespeare, the play keeps the classical 'unities': one main place (the island), one plot, and action that lasts about as long as the performance, roughly three to four hours. The only other Shakespeare play to do this is The Comedy of Errors."],["Masques","Court masques, with music, dance, costumes and gods descending, were fashionable at James I's court. Prospero's masque for Ferdinand and Miranda imitates one."],["Indoor theatre","Shakespeare's company had begun playing at the indoor Blackfriars theatre, with candlelight, music between acts and stage machinery: ideal for the play's songs, spirits and vanishing banquet."]],"critics":{"colony":[["Octave Mannoni, Prospero and Caliban (1950)","Paraphrased: Prospero and Caliban model the psychology of colonial rule, the master's need to dominate and the colonised person's dependence."],["Aimé Césaire, Une Tempête (1969)","A rewriting of the play from Caliban's point of view, in which Caliban is a black slave who rejects Prospero's language and authority."],["Paul Brown, 'This thing of darkness I acknowledge mine' (1985)","Paraphrased: the play both supports and exposes the language of colonisation; Caliban's beautiful speech and his claim to the island unsettle Prospero's story."]],"magic":[["Edward Dowden, Shakspere: His Mind and Art (1875)","Paraphrased: Prospero's serene renunciation of his art reflects Shakespeare himself, at the height of his powers, saying farewell to the stage."]],"nature":[["Frank Kermode, introduction to the Arden edition (1954)","Paraphrased: the play's central contrast is between nature and art (or nurture): Caliban stands for nature without grace, set against Prospero's learning and the courtiers' corrupted civilisation."]],"betrayal":[["Jan Kott, Shakespeare Our Contemporary (1964)","Paraphrased: the island repeats the history of Milan: brothers plot against brothers, servants against masters. The play is darker and more bitter than its fairy-tale surface."]],"power":[["Stephen Orgel, introduction to the Oxford edition (1987)","Paraphrased: Prospero's story is the only version of the past we hear, and his power depends on controlling that story; the absence of Miranda's mother is telling."]],"love":[["Ann Thompson, 'Miranda, where's your sister?' (1991)","Paraphrased: Miranda is the only woman on stage; her value lies in her chastity and her use to her father's political plans, which a feminist reading must question."]]},"insults":[{"id":25,"f":"A pox o' your throat, you bawling, blasphemous, incharitable dog","who":"Sebastian","n":"The noblemen curse a working man doing his job."},{"id":27,"f":"you whoreson, insolent noisemaker","who":"Antonio","n":"Antonio's contempt for those beneath him."},{"id":119,"f":"Thou liest, malignant thing","who":"Prospero","n":"Prospero turns on Ariel, his favourite servant."},{"id":125,"f":"A freckl'd whelp, hag-born","who":"Prospero","n":"Prospero describes Caliban as an animal from birth."},{"id":146,"f":"Thou poisonous slave, got by the devil himself","who":"Prospero","n":"Prospero's first words to Caliban."},{"id":139,"f":"Thou earth, thou","who":"Prospero","n":"Caliban is reduced to mere dirt."},{"id":148,"f":"As wicked dew as e'er my mother brush'd","who":"Caliban","n":"Caliban answers with a curse of his own."},{"id":153,"f":"Abhorred slave","who":"Prospero","n":"The hatred is mutual."},{"id":154,"f":"the red plague rid you","who":"Caliban","n":"Caliban's curse for being taught language."},{"id":206,"f":"He receives comfort like cold porridge","who":"Sebastian","n":"Mockery of grief."},{"id":350,"f":"This ancient morsel, this Sir Prudence","who":"Antonio","n":"Antonio sneers at Gonzalo while planning his death."},{"id":409,"f":"a very shallow monster","who":"Trinculo","n":"Trinculo mocks Caliban's gullibility."},{"id":488,"f":"What a pied ninny's this! Thou scurvy patch","who":"Caliban","n":"Caliban's view of the jester."},{"id":623,"f":"A devil, a born devil","who":"Prospero","n":"Prospero gives up on Caliban."},{"id":691,"f":"whom to call brother Would even infect my mouth","who":"Prospero","n":"Even in forgiving, Prospero cannot call Antonio brother."},{"id":736,"f":"this demi-devil","who":"Prospero","n":"Caliban as half-devil, half-human."}],"pronouns":[{"id":54,"f":"Wipe thou thine eyes; have comfort","n":"Prospero uses tender 'thou' to his daughter."},{"id":63,"f":"Sir, are not you my father","n":"Miranda uses respectful 'you' to her father."},{"id":114,"f":"I prithee, Remember I have done thee worthy service","n":"Ariel uses 'thee' to his master, a mark of their strange intimacy."},{"id":154,"f":"You taught me language","n":"Caliban switches to 'you' for both Prospero and Miranda: hostile distance."},{"id":440,"f":"I would not wish Any companion in the world but you","n":"Miranda and Ferdinand keep the polite 'you' even when declaring love."},{"id":676,"f":"I do forgive thee, Unnatural though thou art","n":"Prospero's 'thee' to Antonio is cold and commanding rather than loving."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Everyone drowns","The storm is magic and all survive","The ship reaches Naples"],"a1s2":["Prospero kills the King","Prospero's enemies are scattered on the island","Ferdinand escapes by boat"],"a2s1":["Antonio kills Alonso","The murder plot is stopped","Gonzalo joins the plot"],"a2s2":["Caliban kills Stephano","Caliban serves Stephano","Stephano frees Ariel"],"a3s1":["Prospero forbids the marriage","Prospero secretly approves","Miranda runs away"],"a3s2":["Caliban's plot succeeds","Ariel warns Prospero","Stephano gives up"],"a3s3":["The lords escape","The lords are brought to Prospero","The lords find Ferdinand"],"a4s1":["Prospero takes revenge","Prospero forgives his enemies","Prospero stays on the island"],"a5s1":["Prospero speaks to the audience","The play ends with a wedding on stage","Ariel stays with Prospero"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Ariel and Caliban","pts":["Ariel is air and fire; Caliban is earth and water.","Ariel serves willingly in hope of freedom; Caliban serves only out of fear.","Ariel is freed at the end; Caliban is 'acknowledged' and promises to seek grace."],"ids":[110,150]},{"t":"Prospero and Sycorax","pts":["Both are magicians exiled to the island with a child.","Prospero calls his power 'art'; Sycorax's is witchcraft.","Both imprison spirits: Sycorax in a pine, Prospero threatens an oak."],"ids":[119,129]},{"t":"Antonio and Gonzalo","pts":["Antonio betrayed Prospero; Gonzalo saved him.","Antonio mocks and plots; Gonzalo comforts and dreams of a better state.","Antonio is silent at the end; Gonzalo blesses everyone."],"ids":[324,288]},{"t":"Ferdinand and Caliban","pts":["Both carry logs for Prospero.","Ferdinand does it for love and accepts it; Caliban curses every load.","Ferdinand wins Miranda by patience; Caliban tried to take her by force."],"ids":[428,375]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Tempest"],["Written","About 1610–11; first printed as the opening play of the First Folio, 1623"],["Genre","Comedy, as the First Folio classes it (its opening play); modern critics group it with the late plays or 'romances': comedy with magic, loss, repentance and reconciliation"],["Setting","A ship at sea, then a remote island in the Mediterranean, between Tunis and Naples"],["Time","One afternoon, from about two o'clock until six; the action matches the length of a performance"],["Language","Mostly verse, often broken and dense; prose for Stephano and Trinculo; songs for Ariel and Caliban; rhyming couplets for the masque"],["Main plot","Prospero brings the men who overthrew him into his power, then forgives them and regains Milan"],["Second plot","Ferdinand and Miranda's love, tested and blessed"],["Murder plots","Antonio and Sebastian against Alonso; Caliban, Stephano and Trinculo against Prospero"],["Turning point","5.1: Ariel's pity moves Prospero to choose 'virtue' over 'vengeance'"],["Resolution","Prospero gives up his magic, reveals himself, sets Ariel free and plans to sail home"],["Key themes","Power and authority, magic and art, freedom and service, forgiveness, colonisation, nature and nurture, illusion"],["Famous for","'We are such stuff as dreams are made on', 'O brave new world', Ariel's songs and Caliban's 'isle is full of noises'"]],"analysis":{"PROSPERO":["Prospero controls almost everything that happens: the storm, where each survivor lands, who meets whom, even when people fall asleep. He is a playwright inside the play, and some of his spells (the banquet, the masque) are literally shows.","He is also a wronged man. His long story to Miranda in 1.2 shows how bitterly he still feels his brother's treachery, though it also reveals that he neglected his duties for his 'secret studies'.","His treatment of his servants is uncomfortable. He is affectionate to Ariel but threatens him with imprisonment in an oak; he calls Caliban filth and torments him with cramps. Modern audiences often find him a harsh master.","The turning point comes when Ariel says he would pity the prisoners 'were I human'. Prospero decides that 'the rarer action is in virtue than in vengeance', gives up his magic and forgives, though his words to Antonio remain cold.","In the Epilogue he is powerless and must ask the audience for applause and pardon. Many readers connect his farewell to magic with Shakespeare's farewell to the stage."],"MIRANDA":["Miranda has grown up seeing no man except her father and Caliban. Her first speech is full of pity for the sailors she thinks have drowned.","She is innocent but not weak. She speaks harshly to Caliban (in some editions the 'Abhorred slave' speech is hers), argues with her father on Ferdinand's behalf, and openly proposes marriage.","Her love for Ferdinand also serves Prospero's political plan: the match unites Milan and Naples. Some critics ask how much choice she really has.","Her 'O brave new world' expresses wonder at mankind but is ironic, since she is looking at the men who wronged her father."],"ARIEL":["Ariel belongs to the lighter elements: he flies, blazes as fire on the ship's masts, sings, turns invisible and changes shape at will. He performs the storm, the songs, the harpy and the masque.","His one desire is freedom. He reminds Prospero of his promise in 1.2 and keeps asking 'Do you love me, master?', which suggests both affection and anxiety.","Ariel is the voice of compassion at the crucial moment: his remark that he would pity the prisoners if he were human leads Prospero to forgive.","He is set free at the very end ('to the elements be free'), and the play gives him a song of the free life he is about to enjoy."],"CALIBAN":["Caliban is described as a 'monster', a 'fish', a 'moon-calf' and a 'thing of darkness', yet he speaks some of the most beautiful verse in the play.","He claims the island by inheritance from his mother Sycorax and remembers Prospero's early kindness. To many modern readers he represents colonised peoples, whose land and language were taken over.","He also tried to rape Miranda and shows no remorse; he plots murder and is easily fooled by Stephano's wine. The play does not let us see him simply as a victim.","At the end he calls himself an ass for worshipping a drunkard and promises to 'seek for grace', suggesting he can change, despite Prospero's claim that nurture never sticks on him."],"FERDINAND":["Ferdinand first appears weeping for his father. He is captivated by Ariel's music and by Miranda.","He accepts the log-carrying cheerfully, turning hard labour into a proof of love: a contrast to Caliban, who curses the same work.","He promises to respect Miranda's chastity before marriage, and at the end he is found playing chess with her, a game of skill and strategy fit for a future king and queen."],"ALONSO":["Alonso helped Antonio depose Prospero. On the island he is consumed by grief for Ferdinand, whom he believes drowned.","The harpy's accusation strikes him deeply: he hears his guilt in the thunder and wants to lie dead in the sea with his son.","Unlike Antonio and Sebastian, he repents. He returns Milan, asks pardon and blesses his son's marriage, which repairs the old division between the families."],"ANTONIO":["Antonio is the original villain: he stole his brother's dukedom and had him set adrift.","On the island he tempts Sebastian to kill Alonso, using the same logic that served him in Milan, and says he feels no conscience at all.","He is forgiven, but he never speaks to Prospero again, apart from one joke about selling Caliban. His silence leaves the ending open."],"GONZALO":["Gonzalo is the loyal counsellor who secretly helped Prospero and Miranda survive, supplying the books that made Prospero's magic possible.","He is mocked for his talkativeness, but he stays patient and kind. His imagined commonwealth questions what a good society would look like.","In the last scene he gives the play's verdict: on this voyage everyone has found what was lost, including themselves."]},"motifs":[["Storms and the sea","The play begins with a storm that destroys nothing, and the sea runs through it as a power that both drowns and transforms: Ariel's 'sea-change', Alonso's wish to lie in the ooze, Ferdinand's discovery that the seas are 'merciful'.",[0,19,20]],["Music and sound","Ariel's songs guide Ferdinand, wake Gonzalo and lead the plotters into a pond. Music charms, heals and punishes, and Caliban hears the whole island as music.",[19,37,38]],["Masters and servants","Almost every relationship involves service: Prospero and Ariel, Prospero and Caliban, Caliban and Stephano, Ferdinand as log-man, the boatswain and the courtiers. The play keeps asking what real freedom is.",[9,17,32,33]],["Sleep and dreams","Characters fall asleep on command, plots are hatched over sleeping men, and Prospero calls life a dream 'rounded with a sleep'.",[2,38,45]],["Theatre and illusion","The storm, the banquet, the harpy and the masque are all performances staged by Prospero. The language of acting ('prologue', 'revels', 'pageant') makes the island a stage.",[28,44,45]],["Books and learning","Prospero's books are the source of his power; Caliban knows they must be seized first; Prospero ends by drowning his book.",[6,7,36,50]]],"symbols2":[["Prospero's books","Learning, power and control. They cost him Milan and gave him the island; he gives them up to return to the human world."],["The staff and the cloak","Prospero's tools of magic. He takes off the cloak to be a father in 1.2 and breaks the staff when he renounces magic."],["The storm","Prospero's power and his anger, and also the chaos of the world that the play moves through towards harmony."],["The pine and the oak","Ariel's imprisonment in a pine and Prospero's threat of an oak: power that can trap a free spirit."],["The island","A place outside ordinary society that each character sees differently: a paradise, a desert, a kingdom to seize, a prison, a home."],["The chess game","Ferdinand and Miranda playing chess hints at courtly skill, strategy and the political marriage that unites Naples and Milan."]],"bigq":[["Is Prospero a good man?",["He was wronged and loves his daughter","He threatens Ariel and torments Caliban","He controls everyone, even his daughter's love","He chooses forgiveness, but coldly"],[10,11,48,51]],["Is Caliban a monster or a victim?",["He claims the island was his","He was taught, then enslaved","He tried to rape Miranda and plots murder","He speaks beautiful verse and promises to change"],[12,15,17,37,56]],["Does everyone really change at the end?",["Alonso repents","Caliban seeks grace","Antonio says nothing to his brother","Sebastian still sneers"],[40,51,54,56]],["Is the play about colonisation?",["Written as England began colonising America","Echoes of the Bermuda wreck and Montaigne","Caliban's claim to the island","Or is it a fairy tale about magic and family?"],[12,24,31,55]],["Why does Prospero give up his magic?",["To return to ordinary human life in Milan","Because magic made him neglect his duty before","Because forgiveness needs no power","As Shakespeare's farewell to the stage?"],[49,50,57,58]],["How free is anyone in the play?",["Ariel is freed, Caliban is not","Ferdinand calls his imprisonment freedom","Miranda's marriage serves her father's plan","Prospero himself needs the audience to free him"],[9,23,33,59]]],"symbols":{"The storm and the sea":"\\bsea\\b|\\bstorm|\\btempest|\\bwaves?\\b|\\bdrown","Books and art":"\\bbooks?\\b|\\bart\\b","Freedom and liberty":"\\bfree\\b|\\bfreedom|\\bliberty","Sleep and dreams":"\\bsleep|\\bdream","Music":"\\bmusic|\\bsong|\\bsing|\\bair\\b","Slave and master":"\\bslave|\\bmaster"},"stagerules":[["ship|sea","ship"],["cell","cell"],["epilogue","epilogue"],["island","island"]],"solo":{"375":1,"378":1,"428":1,"454":1,"623":1,"673":1,"758":1},"iago":[["a1s1","Raises a storm that wrecks his enemies' ship without harming anyone."],["a1s2","Has Ariel scatter the survivors around the island and leave Ferdinand alone."],["a1s2","Brings Ferdinand and Miranda together, then tests Ferdinand with hard labour."],["a2s1","Has Ariel put the lords to sleep, then wake Gonzalo to stop the murder."],["a3s1","Watches the lovers promise to marry, unseen."],["a3s2","Has the invisible Ariel spy on Caliban's plot and lead the plotters astray."],["a3s3","Stages the vanishing banquet and Ariel's harpy speech to drive the lords to guilt."],["a4s1","Blesses the betrothal with a masque and traps the thieves with fine clothes."],["a5s1","Chooses forgiveness, reveals himself, reunites father and son, and frees Ariel."]],"rel":{"1":[["ANTONIO","PROSPERO","hate"],["PROSPERO","MIRANDA","love"],["ARIEL","PROSPERO","trust"],["CALIBAN","PROSPERO","hate"],["FERDINAND","MIRANDA","love"],["PROSPERO","FERDINAND","deceive"]],"2":[["ANTONIO","SEBASTIAN","deceive"],["SEBASTIAN","ALONSO","hate"],["GONZALO","ALONSO","trust"],["ARIEL","GONZALO","trust"],["CALIBAN","STEPHANO","trust"],["TRINCULO","CALIBAN","hate"]],"3":[["FERDINAND","MIRANDA","love"],["CALIBAN","PROSPERO","hate"],["ARIEL","TRINCULO","deceive"],["STEPHANO","TRINCULO","hate"],["ARIEL","ALONSO","deceive"],["PROSPERO","ALONSO","hate"]],"4":[["PROSPERO","FERDINAND","trust"],["FERDINAND","MIRANDA","love"],["PROSPERO","CALIBAN","hate"],["ARIEL","STEPHANO","deceive"],["CALIBAN","STEPHANO","hate"],["ARIEL","PROSPERO","trust"]],"5":[["PROSPERO","ALONSO","trust"],["PROSPERO","ANTONIO","trust"],["ALONSO","FERDINAND","love"],["FERDINAND","MIRANDA","love"],["PROSPERO","ARIEL","love"],["GONZALO","PROSPERO","trust"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Prospero as a powerful figure?",[6,10,11,36,44,49,58]],["How is the relationship between Prospero and Caliban presented?",[12,14,16,17,46,55,56]],["How does Shakespeare explore ideas about freedom and imprisonment?",[9,11,23,33,60,61,59]],["Explore the theme of forgiveness in The Tempest.",[1,39,40,47,48,51,59]],["How does Shakespeare present the island as a place of illusion and wonder?",[19,20,37,38,44,45,52]],["How is Ariel presented, and how does he change Prospero?",[9,10,11,19,47,61]],["How is Miranda presented as a daughter and a lover?",[1,3,21,35,52,53]],["How does Shakespeare present betrayal and guilt?",[4,27,28,29,41,42]],["What does the play suggest about nature and nurture?",[3,14,16,17,46,56]],["How does the play explore questions of colonisation and ownership?",[12,13,24,25,31,32,55]],["How important is the Epilogue to the meaning of the play?",[49,50,57,58,59]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, embedded quotations","Analysed key words and images, not just the meaning","Linked to context (colonisation, magic, masques, the Bermuda wreck)","Tracked change across the whole play: beginning, middle, end","Considered the audience and dramatic effect (spectacle, music, illusion)","Mentioned an alternative reading or critic","Wrote a conclusion that answers the question","Checked spelling of names: Prospero, Miranda, Ariel, Caliban, Ferdinand, Alonso, Gonzalo, Sycorax"],"place":{"a1s1":"At sea","a1s2":"The island","a2s1":"The island","a2s2":"The island","a3s1":"The island","a3s2":"The island","a3s3":"The island","a4s1":"The island","a5s1":"The island","a5s2":"The island"},"models":[[11,"Prospero is presented as powerful, but his power often looks like control by threat.","\"I will rend an oak And peg thee in his knotty entrails\" is a violent threat that echoes Sycorax's imprisonment of Ariel in a pine.","The audience see that Prospero can be as harsh as the witch he despises.","Magic was feared and associated with witchcraft in Shakespeare's England, though learned magic was viewed differently.","Some readers argue his power is a means to justice, as he gives it up once he has restored order."],[17,"Prospero and Caliban's relationship shows a struggle over power, language and land.","\"You taught me language, and my profit on't Is, I know how to curse\" turns Prospero's gift into a weapon against him.","The audience feel the bitterness of the colonised, and Caliban gains some sympathy.","The play was written as Europeans were colonising the Americas.","Prospero's account of Caliban's attack on Miranda complicates sympathy for Caliban."],[33,"Freedom and imprisonment are central, as almost every character is controlled by someone.","Caliban's \"Freedom, high-day!\" is an excited chant, but he has only swapped one master for another.","The audience see the irony that his freedom is illusion.","Masters and servants were bound by strict obligations in Jacobean society.","Ariel's real freedom at the end contrasts with Caliban's, so the play explores different kinds of liberty."],[48,"Forgiveness is shown as a choice that requires overcoming the desire for revenge.","\"The rarer action is In virtue than in vengeance\" contrasts two abstract nouns, presenting forgiveness as the harder and nobler act.","The audience see the turning point of the play: Prospero chooses mercy.","Christian teaching encouraged forgiveness of enemies.","His forgiveness of Antonio is cold, so some readers question how complete it is."],[37,"The island is presented as a place of wonder that changes people.","Caliban's \"Be not afeard: the isle is full of noises\" reassures in gentle verse, surprising from a character often called a monster.","The audience see the island's magic through the eyes of its native, which deepens its mystery.","Travel writing about the New World described strange sounds and wonders.","Some critics see the island as a theatre and Prospero as a dramatist creating illusions."],[47,"Ariel is presented as a spirit who teaches Prospero compassion.","\"Mine would, sir, were I human\" is a short, quiet reply that implies Prospero should feel pity.","The audience see a spirit showing more humanity than its master.","Spirits were believed to serve magicians through learning and command.","Some readers see Ariel's reply as strategy to win freedom, not moral teaching."],[52,"Miranda is presented as innocent and loving, a daughter on the edge of adulthood.","\"O brave new world\" shows her wonder at seeing many people for the first time.","The audience feel the irony, as the people she admires include traitors.","Daughters were expected to obey fathers in marriage choices.","Prospero's dry reply \"'Tis new to thee\" shows age and experience against youth."],[42,"Betrayal and guilt are presented as a poison that eventually takes effect.","\"Their great guilt, Like poison given to work a great time after\" is a simile that makes guilt work slowly from within.","The audience see the traitors' punishment as internal, not just imposed by magic.","Usurping a rightful duke was a grave crime against order.","Antonio shows almost no guilt, which suggests not all wrongdoers repent."],[46,"The play questions whether nature or nurture shapes character.","Prospero's \"A devil, a born devil, on whose nature Nurture can never stick\" sets the two words against each other, blaming Caliban's nature.","The audience may question his judgement, since Caliban has been treated harshly.","Debates about the nature of native peoples were common in colonial writing.","Caliban's final \"I'll be wise hereafter, And seek for grace\" suggests he can change."],[12,"The play explores who owns the island and by what right.","\"This island's mine, by Sycorax my mother\" is a simple claim of inheritance that challenges Prospero's right to rule.","The audience must weigh competing claims to land.","England was beginning to found colonies, and the play reflects that debate.","Prospero's \"this thing of darkness I Acknowledge mine\" can be read as acceptance of responsibility or as ownership."],[59,"The Epilogue makes the audience part of the play's theme of forgiveness.","\"As you from crimes would pardon'd be, Let your indulgence set me free\" asks the audience to forgive as they hope to be forgiven.","The audience's applause becomes the magic that releases Prospero.","The prayer echoes the Lord's Prayer about forgiving others.","Many see the speech as Shakespeare's farewell to the stage, though this is speculation."]],"lang":{"un":[[164,"Full fathom five thy father lies","Thy father lies full fathom five"],[164,"Of his bones are coral made","Coral are made of his bones"],[610,"Our revels now are ended","Now our revels are ended"],[680,"Where the bee sucks, there suck I","I suck there, where the bee sucks"],[680,"Merrily, merrily shall I live now","I shall live merrily, merrily now"]],"pun":[[213,"Dolour comes to him, indeed","Dolour","pun","Sebastian says a dollar; Gonzalo turns it into dolour, grief. The courtiers' wordplay fills time while they are lost."],[233,"Temperance was a delicate wench","Temperance","pun","Temperance is a mild climate and also a girl's name. Antonio and Sebastian sneer by turning Gonzalo's praise into a joke about women."],[645,"Now is the jerkin under the line","line","pun","The line is the clothes line, but also the equator: Stephano jokes about clothes and travel at once."],[98,"I flam'd amazement","flam'd","shift","Flame, a noun, becomes a verb: Ariel did not make flames but became them."]],"st":[[150,"This island's mine",[["This","Points to the ground they stand on: this place, not another."],["mine","Claims ownership, a direct challenge to Prospero."],["island's","Makes the whole island the subject of the argument."]]],[708,"O brave new world",[["brave","Pure wonder at how splendid people look."],["new","Shows her innocence: everything is new to her."],["world","Expands her island life into the whole human world."]]],[669,"Mine would, sir, were I human",[["Mine","Quietly contrasts Ariel's feeling with Prospero's anger."],["would","Makes pity sound certain, not a maybe."],["human","Shames Prospero: a spirit feels more than a man."]]]]},"dir":[[154,"You taught me language","How should Caliban speak this?",[["As a monster","The audience side with Prospero."],["As a wronged native","The play becomes a story about colonial injustice."],["With bitter humour","He wins a moment of power over his master."]]],[519,"Be not afeard; the isle is full of noises","How should this speech land?",[["Still, with music under it","His poetry shows a sensitive mind."],["Whispered to the clowns","He shares something private with strangers who mock him."],["Comic, interrupted","His beauty is wasted on fools, which is sad."]]],[610,"Our revels now are ended","How does Prospero say this?",[["Sadly, to Ferdinand","He speaks about life's shortness, not just the masque."],["Agitated, distracted","He has remembered Caliban's plot; anger breaks through."],["To the audience","A theatre speaking about itself."]]],[669,"Mine would, sir, were I human","How does Ariel say this?",[["Gently","The spirit teaches its master compassion."],["Cool and neutral","A fact, not a plea; Prospero must decide alone."],["Cautiously, wanting freedom","Ariel is careful with a powerful master."]]],[736,"this thing of darkness I Acknowledge mine","How does Prospero say this about Caliban?",[["Taking responsibility","He admits his part in what Caliban has become."],["Possessive, as an owner","He still sees Caliban as property."],["Ashamed","He sees his own darkness in Caliban."]]],[758,"As you from crimes would pardon'd be","How should the epilogue be played?",[["Prospero alone, pleading","The audience must free him with applause."],["The actor half out of costume","The line between play and life blurs."],["In darkness, then lights up","Our applause literally restores the light."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["251","570"],["97","666"],["673","755","758"],["708","717","753"],["288","296","106"],["106","100","551"],["288","708","154"],["288","296","295"],["673","119"],["68","673","129"],["666","716"],["570","717"],["164","673","165"]],"cr":{"colony":[["154","151","623"],["154","153","151"],["154","519","736"]],"magic":[["673","758","755"]],"nature":[["623","153","748"]],"betrayal":[["74","350","676"]],"power":[["119","74","156"]],"love":[["570","195","438"]]}}};
